\section{Introduction}

% General Introduction
\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} rapid emergence of beyond 5G and 6G networks, placed network slicing and logical network abstraction at the forefront of optimization challenges.
Network services are forced to compete for a limited resource pool and a common infrastructure to ensure robust Quality of Service (QoS) and meet stringent Service Level Agreements (SLA).
New services not only demand higher cross-domain performance guarantees, but also operate in highly shared, multi-tenant environments, imposing significant pressure on both pre-deployment and post-deployment optimization processes.
This is where service partitioning becomes a pivotal strategy.
It segments resources and considers important constraints such as limited data availability for privacy reasons and cost-driven overbooking, network operators can maintain expected performance levels and satisfy SLA commitments despite the growing complexity.

% Technological enablers
The vision of the 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) that enabled End-to-End (E2E) logical network segmentation to isolate performance and ensure SLAs per service, was supported by a range of interlocking technologies \cite{8423711}:
At the core, Software-Defined Networking (SDN) allows centralized, programmatic control of data flows.
ETSI has been standardizing Network Functions Virtualization (NFV) the previous years, that decouples Network Functions (NF) from dedicated hardware and enables scaling services as network demands evolve \cite{BHAMARE2016138}.
Service Function Chaining (SFC) adds another layer of dynamism, allowing for the dynamic linking of multiple NFs to create flexible, policy-driven services.
