\section{Discussion and Limitations}

\subsection{Security \& Privacy Concern of Storing Historical Information}

In PDLRecover, we use secret sharing techniques to secure clients' historical data. This approach requires each client to eventually reconstruct the sum of its local updates for global model aggregation. However, this limits the range of applicable global aggregation methods, as secure aggregation techniques like median and trimmed mean may not be feasible. These methods compare local models to filter out significant deviations, enhancing global model robustness. In PDLRecover, to protect historical data, clients lack access to other clients' complete information, making such aggregation methods inapplicable. Therefore, exploring compatibility with other secure aggregation methods presents an intriguing direction for future research.

\subsection{Clients Dropout}

In PDLRecover, we address the challenge of recovering the global model after the detection and removal of malicious clients by the server. However, in real-world scenarios, honest clients may also drop out due to factors like network issues. These clients may wish for the global model to forget their private training data and erase any trace of their participation. In our experiments, we propose treating dropped honest clients as "malicious" to facilitate model recovery. In some cases, the recovered global model aligns with a model trained from scratch, effectively forgetting the presence of dropped honest clients and safeguarding their privacy. Exploring this privacy protection mechanism for dropped honest clients in other scenarios presents a promising direction for future research.
\end{comment}

\section{Conclusion}\label{conclustion}
In this paper, we proposed PDLRecover, a novel decentralized unlearning framework that enables secure and efficient recovery from poison attacks without requiring clients to access or reveal each other’s model updates. PDLRecover reconstructs the global model via Lagrange interpolation while ensuring complete local privacy. Each client independently computes its contribution to the recovery direction using only private gradient and curvature information, and the global model update is collaboratively reconstructed without exposing any individual values. Our theoretical analysis and empirical results demonstrate that PDLRecover not only preserves the confidentiality of historical updates but also enables accurate reconstruction of the global model in the presence of malicious or dropped clients. Unlike traditional detection-based defenses, PDLRecover provides a proactive, privacy-preserving solution to mitigating poison attacks in decentralized environments.

In future work, we will extend PDLRecover with advanced secure aggregation techniques to better address a wider variety of attack types and operating environments. We will also focus on developing multi-layered defense mechanisms to further bolster the security and recoverability of the global model. Additionally, we aim to investigate the potential for model recovery with reduced dependency on historical data, which could lower storage requirements and enhance the efficiency of model updates.

