\begin{abstract}
\noindent
    Graded modal substitution calculus (GMSC) and its variants has been used for logical characterizations of various computing frameworks such as graph neural networks, ordinary neural networks and distributed computing. 
    In this paper we introduce two different semantic games and formula size game for graded modal substitution calculus and its variants. Ultimately, we show that the formula size game characterizes the equivalence of classes of pointed Kripke models up to programs of GMSC of given size. Thus, the formula size game can be used to study the expressive power mentioned characterized classes of computing models. 
    Moreover, we show that over words GMSC has the same expressive power as deterministic linearly tape-bounded Turing machines also known as deterministic linear bounded automata. 
\end{abstract}




\section{Introduction}

In \cite{Kuusisto13} Kuusisto introduced a rule-based recursive bisimulation invariant logic called modal substitution calculus (MSC) and used it to characterize distributed automata. The logic MSC consists of programs that are lists rules. Informally, the semantics are defined over Kripke models; programs are run in each node of the model and the local configuration of the program in each node is update synchronously by using the rules (cf. the preliminaries).

MSC and its variants have been lately used to characterize various other computing frameworks.
In \cite{dist_circ_mfcs} distributed computing with circuits and identifiers were characterized via MSC and in \cite{ahvonen_et_al:LIPIcs.CSL.2024.9} general recurrent neural networks were characterized via the diamond-free fragment of MSC.
Moreover, in \cite{ahvonen2024logicalcharacterizationsrecurrentgraph} used an extension of MSC with counting modalities called graded modal substitution calculus (GMSC) was used to characterize recurrent graph neural networks.
Thus, 
MSC and its variants have proven to be quite useful in the field of descriptive complexity.

In this paper we consider GMSC with global modality (or GGMSC) as many of the results are easy to modify for fragments of GGMSC (e.g. MSC and GMSC). We define two semantic games for GGMSC and its variants. Informally, the first game is played locally on a single node and the other one is played globally on the whole model.
We also define an asynchronous game for MSC and show that MSC with asynchronous semantics has the same expressive power as mu-fragment of modal mu-calculus.
By using the same ideas as in the semantic games it is then easy to define a formula size game for GGMSC and its variants. Ultimately, the formula size game characterizes the equivalence of classes of pointed Kripke models up to programs of GGMSC of given size. The formula size game is heavily inspired by the formula size game defined in \cite{mu-calculus-formula-size}.

As an additional result, we provide a new logical characterization for deterministic tape-bounded Turing machines. Informally, deterministic tap-bounded automata are restrictive version of deterministic Turing machines, where the length of the tape is a bounded by a function. 


\section{Preliminaries}

We let $\PROP$ denote the countably infinite set of \textbf{proposition symbols} and respectively let $\mathrm{VAR}$ denote the countably infinite set of \textbf{schema variables}. Given a $\Pi \subseteq \PROP$, a \textbf{Kripke model over $\Pi$} (or simply $\Pi$-model) is tuple $(W, R, V)$, where $W$ is non-empty domain, $R \subseteq W \times W$ is accessibility relation and $V \colon W \to \cP(\Pi)$ is a valuation function. A \textbf{pointed Kripke model} is a pair $((W, R, V), w)$, where $w \in W$. Moreover, the set of \textbf{out-neighbours} of a node $v$ is $\{\, u \in W \mid (v, u) \in R \,\}$.

Let $X$ and $Y$ be sets. If $f \colon X \rightharpoonup Y$ is a partial function, $x \in X$ and $y \in Y$, then we let $f' = f[y/x]$ denote the partial function $f' \colon X \rightharpoonup Y$ defined by $f'(z) = f(z)$, when $z \neq x$ and otherwise $y$.
    % \[
    % f'(z) =
    % \begin{cases}
    %     f(z), & z \neq x \\
    %     y, & z = x.
    % \end{cases}
    % \]
Given two sets $X, Y$, we let $X^Y$ denote the sets of function of type $Y \to X$. Given a relation $R$ over $X$, if $(x, y) \in R$, then we say that $y$ is \textbf{$R$-successor} of $x$. Given a function $g$ from $X$ to $Y$, the \textbf{range} of $g$ is the set $\{\, g(x) \in Y \mid x \in X\,\}$.

\subsection{Modal variants of substitution calculus}

Before we define the programs of $\GGMSC$ we define the rules. Let $\Pi$ be a set of proposition symbols, $\cT$ a set of schema variables and $X \in \cT$. 
A \textbf{$(\Pi, \cT)$-schemata of graded modal logic with global modality} is defined by the following grammar
%
%
\[
%
%
\varphi \coloncolonequals \bot \mid \top \mid p \mid \neg \varphi \mid X \mid \neg X \mid \varphi \lor \varphi \mid \varphi \land \varphi \mid \Diamond_{\geq k} \varphi \mid \Box_{< k} \varphi \mid \langle E \rangle_{\geq k} \varphi \mid \BoxG_{< k} \varphi
%
%
\]
%
%
where $k \in \N$, $p \in \Pi$ and $X \in \cT$.\footnote{The connectives $\rightarrow$, $\leftrightarrow$ are considered abbreviations in the
usual way. If $D \in \{\Diamond, \DiamondG \}$, we let $D_{ < k} \varphi \colonequals \neg D_{\geq k} \varphi$, and if $B \in \{\Box, \BoxG \}$, we let $B_{ \geq k} \varphi \colonequals \neg B_{< k} \varphi$. 
We also use the abbreviations $\Diamond \varphi \colonequals \Diamond_{\geq 1} \varphi$, $\Box \varphi \colonequals \Box_{< 1} \varphi$, $\Diamond_{=k} \varphi \colonequals \Diamond_{\geq k} \varphi \land \Diamond_{< k+1} \varphi $ and $\Box_{=k} \varphi \colonequals \Box_{\geq k} \varphi \land \Box_{< k+1} \varphi $; the abbreviations $\langle E \rangle \varphi$, $\BoxG$, $\langle E \rangle_{=k} \varphi$ and $\BoxG_{=k}$ are defined analogously.} 
%
Moreover, when in the grammar the schema variables are excluded we have the set of \textbf{$\Pi$-formulae of graded modal logic with global modality} (or $\GGML$). 
The set of \textbf{literals} over $\Pi$ is $\mathrm{Lit}(\Pi) \colonequals \Pi \cup \{\neg p \mid p \in \Pi\} \cup \{\top, \bot\}$.
Now, assume that $\{X_1, \ldots, X_k\} = \cT$ is a non-empty finite set of $k$ distinct schema variables.
A $(\Pi, \cT)$-\textbf{program} $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$ consists two list of \textbf{rules}
\[
\begin{aligned}
    &X_1 (0) \colonminus \varphi_1\qquad\qquad &&X_1 \colonminus \psi_1 \\
    &\vdots &&\vdots \\
    &X_k (0) \colonminus \varphi_k\qquad\qquad &&X_k \colonminus \psi_k \\
\end{aligned}
\]
where each $\varphi_i$ is $\Pi$-formula of $\GGML$ and each $\psi_i$ is $(\Pi, \cT)$-schema of $\GGMSC$.
Moreover, each program is also associated with a set of \textbf{accepting predicates} $\cA\subseteq \cT$.

The strings of the form $X_i (0) \colonminus \varphi_i$ are called the \textbf{base rules} and strings of the form $X_i \colonminus \psi_i$ are called the \textbf{iteration rules}. The variables $X_i$ in the front of the rules are called the \textbf{head predicates} and the formulae $\varphi_1, \ldots, \varphi_k$ and $\psi_1, \ldots, \psi_k$ are the \textbf{bodies} of the rules.  
The programs of \textbf{graded modal substitution calculus} (or $\GMSC$) \cite{ahvonen2024logicalcharacterizationsrecurrentgraph} and \textbf{modal substitution calculus} (or $\MSC$) \cite{Kuusisto13, ahvonen2023descriptive} are defined analogously to $\GGMSC$-programs except that global diamonds $\DiamondG_{\geq k}$ and boxes $\BoxG_{<k}$ are excluded and the bodies can only use standard counting diamonds $\Diamond_{\geq k}$ and boxes $\Box_{< k}$ and the standard diamond $\Diamond$ and the box $\Box$ respectively. 
The programs of \textbf{substitution calculus} (or $\mathrm{SC}$) is obtained by excluding all the standard and global diamonds and boxes.
By omitting schema variables $\cT$, we let a $\Pi$-program of $\GGMSC$ refer to a $(\Pi, \cT)$-program of $\GGMSC$. 



To define semantics for $\Lambda$, we start by defining semantics for graded modal logic. The truth of $\Pi$-formulae $\varphi$ of $\GGML$ in a pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w)$ (denoted by $M, w \models \varphi$) is defined as follows. The semantics for Boolean connectives, $\bot$ and $\top$ is the usual one, while for proposition symbols $p \in \Pi$, we define $M, w \models p$ iff $p \in V(w)$, for $\Diamond_{\geq k} \varphi$, we define $M, w \models \Diamond_{\geq k} \varphi$ iff $M, v \models \varphi$ for $k$ distinct out-neighbours $v$ of $w$ and for $\Box_{< k} \varphi$, we define $M, w \models \Box_{< k} \varphi$ iff there are at most $k$ distinct out-neighbours $v$ of $w$ such that $M, v \not\models \varphi$.
Moreover, we define $M, w \models \langle E \rangle_{\geq k} \varphi$ iff $M, v \models \varphi$ for $k$ distinct nodes $v$ in $M$, and for $\BoxG_{< k} \varphi$, we define $M, w \models \BoxG_{< k} \varphi$ iff there are at most $k$ distinct nodes $v$ in $M$ such that $M, v \not\models \varphi$.\footnote{Note that by the definition $\Box_{<k}$ is the corresponding dual operator for $\Diamond_{\geq k}$, i.e., $\Box_{<k} \varphi$ is logically equivalent to $\neg \Diamond_{\geq k} \neg \varphi$. Analogously, $\BoxG_{<k} \varphi$ is logically equivalent to $\neg \DiamondG_{\geq k} \neg \varphi$.}


Now, we define the semantics for $\Lambda$.
First, we define \textbf{$n$th iteration formula $X^n_i$} (w.r.t. $\Lambda$) (or the iteration formula of $X_i$ at \textbf{round} $n \in \N$) for each head predicate $X_i$ recursively as follows.
%\begin{itemize}
%    \item 
    We define $X^0_i \colonequals \varphi_i$.
    %
%    \item 
    The formula $X^{n+1}_i$ is obtained by replacing each variable $X_j$ in the $\psi_i$ by the $X_j^n$.
%\end{itemize}
%
%
Thus, $X^n_i$ is just a $\GML$-formulae.
Analogously, given a $(\Pi, \cT)$-schemata $\varphi$, we let $\varphi^{k}$ denote the $\GGML$-formula, where each head predicate $X_i$ is replaced by $X_i^k$.
%
%



Now, we define $M, w \models \Lambda$ and say that $(M, w)$ is \textbf{accepted} by $\Lambda$ if and only if $M, w \models X^n$ for some accepting predicate $X$ for $n \in \N$. 
%
Moreover, we write $M, w \models_{\mathrm{fp}} \Lambda$ say that $(M, w)$ is \textbf{fixed-point accepted} by $\Lambda$ if and only if there exists $n \in \N$ such that $M, w \models X^m$ for every $m \geq n$.
%
Moreover, we say that two $\Pi$-programs of $\GGMSC$ are \textbf{equivalent} if they accept (resp. fixed-point accept) precisely the same $\Pi$-models.
%
Given two classes $\cA$ and $\cB$ of $\Pi$-models, we say that $\Lambda$ \textbf{separates $\cA$ from $\cB$} (resp. \textbf{fixed-point separates $\cA$ from $\cB$)}, if every $(A, w) \in \cA$ is accepted (resp. fixed-point accepted) by $\Lambda$ and every $(B, w) \in \cB$ is not accepted (resp. not fixed-point accepted) by $\Lambda$.







\begin{example}
    A pointed model $(M,w)$ has the \textbf{centre-point property} if there exists $n \in \N$ such that each path starting from $w$ leads to a node $v$ which has no successors in exactly $n$ steps. 
    The following program $X(0) \colonminus \Box \bot$, $X \colonminus \Diamond X \land \Box X$ accepts pointed models which has the centre-point property. 
%   
\end{example}
%

The \textbf{size} of a $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$ is defined as the number of literal $\mathrm{Lit}(\Pi)$, symbols $\top$ and $\bot$, and logical connectives $\lor$ and $\land$ occurring in $\Lambda$, augmented by the counting thresholds $k$ of all modalities $\Diamond_{\geq k}$, $\Box_{< k}$, $\DiamondG_{\geq k}$ and $\BoxG_{\geq k}$ present in $\Lambda$. 


A program is in \textbf{strong negation normal form}, if all the negations in the program appears in the front of a proposition symbol. In other words this means that negated schema variables can be omitted. 
%
The following lemma proves that each program can be translated into an equivalent program which is in strong negation normal form.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: strong negation normal form}
    Given a $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$ of size $n$, there exists an equivalent a program of $\GGMSC$ in strong negation normal form of size $\ordo(n)$.
\end{lemma}
%
%
\begin{proof}
    We assume that $\Lambda$ is already in a weak negation normal form since translating $\Lambda$ to weak negation normal form is trivial.

    We construct an equivalent program $\Lambda_d$ that is in strong negation normal form as follows. For each head predicate $X$ in $\Lambda$ with the rules $X (0) \colonminus \varphi$ and $X \colonminus \psi$, we simultaneously define a fresh head predicate $X_d$ with the following rules: $X_d (0) \colonminus \varphi_d$, where $\varphi_d$ is obtained from $\varphi$ by taking its dual and $X_d \colonminus \psi_d$, where $\psi_d$ is obtained from $\psi$ by first taking its dual and after that replacing each $\neg Y$ by $Y_d$. Then finally we modify each original rule $X \colonminus \psi$ in $\Lambda$ by replacing each $\neg Y$ by $Y_d$. 


    It is clear that $\Lambda_d$ is in strong negation normal form and the size is linear in the size of $\Lambda$. We show that the obtained program accepts (resp. fixed-point accepts) the same $\Pi$-models. More precisely, we show by induction on $n \in \N$ that for each pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w)$ and for each head predicate $X$ that appears in $\Lambda$ and $\Lambda_d$ that the following holds
    \[
    M, w \models X^n \text{ w.r.t. $\Lambda$} \iff M, w \models X^n \text{ w.r.t. $\Lambda_d$.}
    \]
    \[
    M, w \not\models X^n \text{ w.r.t. $\Lambda$} \iff M, w \models X_d^n \text{ w.r.t. $\Lambda_d$.}
    \]
\end{proof}

Lastly, we point out that schemata of $\GGMSC$ can be interpreted as $\GGML$-formulae when Kripke model is associated with an interpretation over variables.
A pointed $\Pi$-model $(M,w) = ((W, R, V), w)$ associated with a function $g \colon W \to \cP(\cT)$ called \textbf{labeled tuple}, denoted by $M_g \colonequals (M, g)$ is called a \textbf{Kripke model over} $(\Pi, \cT)$ or simply $(\Pi, \cT)$-model. 
In a pointed $(\Pi, \cT)$-model $(M_g, w)$ we can interpret $(\Pi, \cT)$-schema $\psi$ without a program as follows. We define $M_g, w \models X$ iff $w \in g(X)$. The rest of the semantics for connectives and diamonds are analogous to $\GGML$. Thus, over pointed $( \Pi, \cT ) $-models, $( \Pi, \cT ) $-schemata can be interpreted in an analogous way as $\GGML$-formulae.
A labeled tuple $f$ is \textbf{suitable} for a $\Pi$-model $M$ if the domain of $f$ is the domain of $M$.
%
%
%
Note that in each round $n \in \N$, in each $\Pi$-model $M = (W, R, V)$, a $(\Pi, \cT)$-program $\Lambda$ induces a labeled tuple $g_n \colon \cT \to \cP(W)$ called \textbf{global configuration} at round $n$ defined as follows. For each $X \in \cT$, we define $g_n(X) = W'$, where $M, w \models X^n$ iff $w \in W'$.



\section{Semantic games}


In this section we define two semantic games for $\GGMSC$, which are both easy to modify for variants of $\GGMSC$. Informally, the both games are played by two players Eloise and Abelard, where for a given $\GGMSC$-program $\Lambda$ and a pointed model $(M, w)$, Eloise tries to show that $(M, w)$ is accepted by $\Lambda$ and Abelard opposes this. There are two main differences between the games: The first game is played locally in a single node of the input model at the time and the number of rounds are bounded at the start of the game. 
In the second one, the game is played globally on the whole input model and the rounds are not fixed at the start of the game. The correctness of these games are formally stated in Theorems \ref{thrm: semantic game} and Theorem \ref{thrm: global semantic game}. Moreover, we study asynchronous and fixed-point variants of these games and show that the asynchronous variant of (graded) $\MSC$ corresponds to (graded) $\mu$-fragment of $\mu$-calculus.






\subsection{Standard semantic game}


Given a pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w) = ((W, R, V), w)$ and $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$,
a \textbf{semantic game $\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$ of $\GGMSC$} is defined as follows. The game has two players \textbf{Abelard} and \textbf{Eloise}. 
The \textbf{positions} of the game $\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$ are tuples $(\bV, v, \varphi, k)$, where 
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item $\bV \in \{\El, \Ab\}$ is the current \textbf{verifier} while the other player is \textbf{falsifier},
    %
    %
    \item $v$ is a node in $M$,
    %
    %
    %
    \item $\varphi$ is a subschemata of $\Lambda$ and
    %
    %
    \item $k \in \N$ is the \textbf{iteration round}.
\end{itemize}
Intuitively, a position $(\bV, v, \varphi, k)$ corresponds to the following claim:
%
\begin{itemize}
    \item[] ``The player $\bV$ can verify $\varphi$ at $v$ in $k$ iteration rounds.''
\end{itemize}
%
%
%
Moreover, the \textbf{initial positions} are the positions $(\El, w, \varphi, k)$, where 
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item if $k \geq 1$, $\varphi$ is the body of the iteration rule of an accepting predicate, and
    %
    %
    \item if $k = 0$, $\varphi$ is the body of the base rule of an accepting predicate.
    %
    %
\end{itemize}
%
%
A play of the game $\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$ begins from an initial position that is chosen by Eloise from the set of initial positions. Moreover, if the set of initial positions of the game is empty (i.e. $\Lambda$ does not have accepting predicates) then Eloise automatically loses and Abelard wins. So strictly speaking there is a ``starting position'', where Eloise chooses an initial position.


The \textbf{rules} of the game are defined as follows.
%
%
%
\begin{enumerate}
    \item In a position $(\bV, v, \top, \ell)$, the game ends and verifier wins.
    %
    %
    %
    \item In a position $(\bV, v, p, \ell)$, where $p \in \Pi$, the game ends. The verifier wins if $p \in V(v)$. Otherwise the falsifier wins.
    %
    %
    %
    \item In a position $(\bV, v, \neg \psi, \ell)$, the game continues from the position $(\bV', v, \psi, \ell)$, where $\bV' \in \{\El,\Ab\} \setminus \{\bV\}$.
    %
    %
    %
    \item In a position $(\bV, v, \psi \land \theta, \ell)$ the game continues as follows. The falsifier chooses a formula $\chi$ from the set $\{\psi, \theta\}$ and the game continues from the position $(\bV, v, \chi, \ell)$. 
    %
    %
    \item In a position $(\bV, v, \Diamond_{\geq k} \psi, \ell)$ the game continues as follows. The verifier $\bV$ chooses a set $\{u_1, \ldots, u_k\}$ of $k$ distinct out-neighbours of $v$, then the falsifier chooses a node $u \in \{u_1, \ldots, u_k\}$ and the game continues from the position $(\bV, u, \psi, \ell)$. If the verifier cannot choose $k$ out-neighbours of $v$, then the falsifier wins.
    %
    %
    \item In a position $(\bV, v, \DiamondG_{\geq k} \psi, \ell)$ the game continues as follows. The verifier $\bV$ chooses a set $\{u_1, \ldots, u_k\}$ of $k$ distinct nodes from $W$, then the falsifier chooses a node $u \in \{u_1, \ldots, u_k\}$ and the game continues from the position $(\bV, u, \psi, \ell)$. If the verifier cannot choose $k$ nodes, then the falsifier wins.
    %
    %
    %
    \item In a position $(\bV, v, X, \ell)$ the game continues as follows. If $\ell > 0$, then the game continues from the position $(\bV, v, \psi, \ell-1)$, where $\psi$ is the body of the iteration rule of $X$. If $\ell = 1$, then the game continues from the position $(\bV, v, \theta, 0)$, where $\theta$ is the body of the base rule of $X$. 
    %
    %
    %
\end{enumerate}
%
Note that after a position $(\bV, v, \theta, 0)$ is reached, then a play of the game will end into a position $(\bV, v, p, 0)$, where $p \in \Pi$, since the base rules are just $\GGML$-formulas. 
We write $M, w \Vdash \Lambda$ iff Eloise has a \textbf{winning strategy} in the semantic game $\cG(M,w, \Lambda)$, i.e., Eloise can win every play of the game.




A \textbf{$k$-bounded semantic game} $\cG(M, w, \Lambda, k)$, where $\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$ is a semantic game and $k \in \N$, is played almost identically as the $\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$, but the initial positions are of the form $(\El, w, \varphi, k)$.
We write $M, w \Vdash^k \Lambda$ iff Eloise has a \textbf{winning strategy} in the $k$-bounded semantic game $\cG(M, w, \Lambda, k)$, i.e., Eloise can win every play of the game. 
We write $M, w \models^k \Lambda$, if $M, w \models X^k$ for some accepting predicate $X$, i.e., $\models^k$ denotes the \textbf{$k$-bounded compositional semantics} of $\GGMSC$.


\begin{remark}
%
It is easy to obtain semantic game for $\GGMSC$-schemata from a semantic game of $\GGMSC$ as follows. Given, a $( \Pi, \cT ) $-schema $\psi$ of $\GGMSC$ and a pointed $( \Pi, \cT ) $-model $( M_g, w ) $, \textbf{semantic game} $\cG(M_g,w, \psi)$ is played like a semantic game of $\GGMSC$ but the initial position of the game is always $(\El, w, \varphi, 0)$ and variables are handled as follows. In position $( \bV, v, X, 0 ) $ the game ends and the current verifier $\bV$ wins if $X \in g(v)$, and otherwise falsifier wins. Thus $0$ can be omitted from the game positions and simply write $(\bV, v, \varphi)$.
%
Furthermore, may write $M_g, w \Vdash \psi$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M_g, w, \psi)$ and in the case of $\psi$ is a formula of $\GGML$, we may omit $g$.
%
\end{remark}


Now, we prove the correctness of our $k$-bounded local semantic game.
%
%
\begin{lemma}
    For each pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w)$ and $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$,
    \[
    M, w \models^k \Lambda \iff M, w \Vdash^k \Lambda.
    \]
\end{lemma}
%
%
%
\begin{proof}
    By induction on $k$. We prove the base case for $k = 0$ by induction on structure on $\varphi$. 
    Let $\varphi$ be the body of the base rule of an accepting predicate of $\Lambda$. It is easy to show by induction on structure of $\varphi$ that $M, w \models \varphi$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M, w, \varphi)$ iff Eloise wins $\cG(M, w, \Lambda, 0)$ starting from $(\El, w, \varphi, 0)$. Thus, $M, w \models^0 \Lambda$ iff there is a body $\varphi$ of the base rule of an accepting predicate of $\Lambda$ such that $M, w \models \varphi$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M, w, \varphi)$ iff $M, w \Vdash^0 \Lambda$.

    Assume that the induction hypothesis holds for $0 \leq \ell < k$ and we shall prove the claim for $k$. 
    %
    Let $\psi$ be the body of the iteration rule of an accepting predicate $X$. We prove by induction on the structure of $\psi$ that
    $M, w \models \psi^k$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M, w, \Lambda, k)$ starting from the position $(\El, w, \psi, k)$.
    %
    %
    \begin{itemize}
        %
        %
        \item If $\psi = p$, where $p \in \Pi$, $p^k = p$ and thus the claim holds trivially. Also, the case for Boolean connectives and $\top$ is trivial. 
        %
        %
        \item Assume that $\psi \colonequals \Diamond_{\geq m} \theta$. Now, 
        $M, w \models (\Diamond_{\geq m} \theta)^k$ iff then there are at least $m$ distinct successors 
        $\{u_1, \ldots, u_m\}$ of $w$ such that $M, u_i \models \theta^k$ for all $i \in [m]$. By the induction hypothesis Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M, u_i, \Lambda^*, k)$ for every $u_i$, where $\Lambda^*$ is obtained from $\Lambda$ by replacing the body of the iteration rule of $X$ by $\theta$. Which equivalent to that Eloise has the winning strategy in the game starting from the position $(\El, w, \Diamond_{\geq k} \theta, k)$. Other diamonds are analogously handled.
        %
        %
        %
        \item Assume that $\psi = Y$ for some head predicate $Y$ of $\Lambda$ and let $\psi_Y$ denote the body of its iteration rule. Now, by the induction hypothesis we have $M, w \models Y^{k}$ iff $M, w \models \psi_Y^{k-1}$ iff Eloise has the winning strategy in the game $\cG(M, w, \Lambda^*, k-1)$, where $\Lambda^*$ is obtained from $\Lambda$ by adding $Y$ to the set of accepting predicates. 
        %
    \end{itemize}
    Thus, $M, w \models^k \Lambda$ iff there is an accepting predicate $X$ of $\Lambda$ such that $M, w \models X^k$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M, w, \Lambda, k)$ starting from $(\El, w, X, k)$ iff $M, w \Vdash^k \Lambda$.
\end{proof}

From the above lemma it is trivial follows the theorem below.
%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{thrm: semantic game}
    For each pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w)$ and $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$,  
    \[
    M, w \models \Lambda \iff M, w \Vdash \Lambda.
    \]
\end{theorem}
%
Moreover, trivially semantic games for $\GGMSC$ extends to its other variants as well.




\subsection{Global semantic game}


A \textbf{global semantic game} $\cG^*(M, w, \Lambda)$ is played over a pointed $\Pi$-model $(M,w) = ((W, R, V), w)$ and a $(\Pi, \cT)$-program of $\GGMSC$. Again, the game has two players Abelard and Eloise, and Eloise tries to show that $(M,w)$ is accepted by $\Lambda$ and Abelard opposes this. 


Informally, a position of the game is simply a labeled tuple $f \colon W \to \cP( \cT ) $. Intuitively, the current poisition of the game records a global configuration of $\Lambda$ over $(M,w)$ and during the game Eloise tries to show that she can start from a global configuration where $(M, w)$ is accepted and ``backward'' to the initial global configuration. The role of Abelard is to check that each global configuration is valid with respect to the rules of $\Lambda$.



Formally, a \textbf{positions of the game} $\cG^* (M, w, \Lambda)$ is a labeled tuple $f$ in $\cP(\cT)^W$. Here $\cP(\cT)^{W}$ denotes the set of function from $W$ to $\cP(\cT)$.
The \textbf{initial positions} of the game are the labeled tuples $f$ such that for at least one accepting predicate $X$ of $\Lambda$, we have $X \in f(w)$. At the start of the game Eloise chooses some initial position of the set of initial positions. If the set of initial positions is empty (i.e. $\Lambda$ does not have accepting predicates), then Abelard wins. Again, strictly speaking, analogously to the semantic games, there is a ``starting position'' where Eloise chooses an initial position.


The \textbf{rules} of $\cG^* (M, w, \Lambda)$ are defined as follows. 
In each position $f$ of the game Eloise first declares if $f$ is the final position of the game.
%
%
%
\begin{enumerate}
    %
    %
    \item If Eloise declares that $f$ is the final position of the game then the game continues as follows.
    %
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Abelard chooses a node $v \in W$ and a head predicate $X$ of $\Lambda$. Let $\varphi$ denote the body of the base rule of $X$.
        %
        %
        \item Then Eloise wins if the following holds: $X \in f(v)$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M, v, \varphi)$. 
        %
    \end{enumerate}

    %
    %
    %
    %
    \item If Eloise declares that $f$ is not the final position of the game then the game continues as follows. Eloise gives a labeled tuple $g \in \cP(\cT)^W$, then Abelard can either choose to \textbf{challenge} $g$ or not. If Abelard chooses \emph{not} to challenge $g$, then the game continues from $g$. If Abelard challenges $g$, then the game continues as follows. 
    %
    %
    %
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Abelard chooses a node $v \in W$ and a head predicate $X$ of $\Lambda$. Let $\psi$ denote the body of the iteration rule of $X$.
        %
        %
        \item 
        Then Eloise wins if the following holds: $X \in f(v)$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M_g, v, \varphi)$. 
        %
        %
    \end{enumerate}    %
    %
    %
\end{enumerate}
We write $M, w \Vvdash \Lambda$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG^* (M, w, \Lambda)$.


Next, we prove the correctness of the global semantic game.
%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{thrm: global semantic game}
For each pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w)$ and $\Pi$-program of $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$,
\[
M, w \models \Lambda \iff M, w \Vvdash \Lambda.
\]
\end{theorem}
%
%
%
\begin{proof}
Assume that $M, w \models \Lambda$ and $M = (W, R, V)$. Let $k \in \N$ be the smallest round where $M, w \models X^k$ for some accepting predicate. Let $(g_0, \ldots, g_k)$ be the sequence of global configurations of $\Lambda$ in $M$ for each round $i \in [0; k]$. Eloise has the winning strategy where she starts by choosing $g_k$ as the initial position of the game and during the game in each position $g_i$ she picks a position $g_{i-1}$, where $i \in [k]$. Note that $g_k$ is an initial position since $X \in g_k(w)$. After reaching $g_0$ Eloise declares that $g_0$ is the final position of the game. Clearly, if Abelard does not challenge Eloise at any position, Eloise wins. On the other hand, if Abelard challenges $g_i$ for $i \in [0; k-1]$, then he will always lose for the following reason. 
Let $v \in W$ and let $Y$ be a head predicate of $\Lambda$ and $\psi$ the body of the iteration rule of $Y$ if $i \neq 0$ and otherwise let $\psi$ be the body of the base rule of $Y$. 
Now, by the definition of $g_i$ we have $Y \in g_{i+1}(v)$ iff $M, v \models \psi^i$ iff $M_{g_i}, v \models \psi$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M_{g_i}, v, \psi)$.


For the converse direction. Assume that $M, w \Vvdash \Lambda$. Let $f_k, \ldots, f_0$ enumerate the positions chosen by Eloise that guarantees the win in $\cG^*(M, w, \Lambda)$ such that $f_k$ is an initial position and $f_{i-1}$ is followed by $f_{i}$ during the game. Let $g_k, \ldots, g_0$ enumerate the global configurations of $\Lambda$ in $M$ from round $k$ to round $0$. 
We show by induction on $i \in [0; k]$ that $f_i = g_i$. The case $f_0 = g_0$ is trivial. 
Assume that $f_j = g_i$ for every $j < i$ and we show that $f_i = g_i$ also holds. 
Let $X$ be a head predicate of $\Lambda$ and $\psi$ the body of its iteration rule. 
Now, we have $M, g_{i-1}, u \models \psi$ iff $M, u \models \psi^{i-1}$ iff $M, u \models X^{i}$ iff $X \in g_i(u)$ by the definition of global configurations.
Moreover, it holds that
$M, f_{i-1}, u \models \psi$  iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M_{f_{i-1}}, u, \psi)$ iff $X \in f_i(u)$, since Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG^*(M, w, \Lambda)$.
By the induction hypothesis $f_{i-1} = g_{i-1}$, thus we have $X \in g_i(u)$ iff $X \in f_i(u)$, i.e. $f_i = g_i$.
\end{proof}



\subsection{Asynchronous and fixed-point variants of games}

In this section we define a notion on fixed-point semantic game and asynchronous semantic game for $\GGMSC$ and its variants, then we show that with these semantics $\GMSC$ (resp. $\MSC$) corresponds to $\mathrm{GMSCL}$ (resp. $\mathrm{MSCL}$). 
Let $(M, w)$ be a pointed $\Pi$-model and $\Lambda$ a $\Pi$-program of $\GGMSC$.


A \textbf{fixed-point semantic game} $\cF\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$ is obtained by modifying $\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$ as follows. The game positions and rules are the same except an initial position is determined as follows. In the beginning of the game Eloise gives an integer $n \in \N$, then after that Abelard chooses an integer $m \geq n$ which determines the initial position $(\El, w, \varphi, m)$. We write $M, w \Vdash_{\mathrm{fp}} \Lambda $ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cF\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$. Now, it is easy to prove the following.

\begin{theorem}
    For each pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w)$ and $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$,  
    \[
    M, w \models_{\mathrm{fp}} \Lambda \iff M, w \Vdash_{\mathrm{fp}} \Lambda.
    \]
\end{theorem}



A \textbf{fixed-point global semantic game} $\cF\cG^*(M, w, \Lambda)$ is obtained from $\cG^*(M, w, \Lambda)$ as follows. After Eloise has give an initial position $f$ of the game Abelard can challenge $f$. If Abelard challenges $f$, then he chooses a node $v \in W$ and a head predicate $X$ of $\Lambda$. Let $\varphi$ denote the body of the iteration rule of $X$. Now, Eloise wins if the following holds: $X \in f(v)$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cG(M_f, v, \varphi)$. That is, Abelard can check if $f$ is a fixed-point. If Abelard does not challenge $f$, then the game continues in the same way as $\cG^*(M, w, \Lambda)$. We write $M, w \Vvdash_{\mathrm{fp}} \Lambda$ iff Eloise has a winning strategy in $\cF\cG^*(M, w, \Lambda)$. The corresponding theorem is trivial to obtain.

\begin{theorem}
    For each pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w)$ and $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$,  
    \[
    M, w \models_{\mathrm{fp}} \Lambda \iff M, w \Vvdash_{\mathrm{fp}} \Lambda.
    \]
\end{theorem}


An \textbf{asynchronous semantic game $\cA\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$ of $\GGMSC$} is defined as follows. The game is defined analogously to $\cG(M, w, \Lambda)$, but the game positions are tuples of the form $(\bV, v, \varphi)$ instead of $(\bV, v, \varphi, k)$, i.e., iteration rounds are omitted from the positions. Furthermore, the initial positions are the positions $(\El, w, \varphi)$, where $\varphi$ is the body of the iteration rule or the base rule of an accepting predicate. The game is played similarly except when $\varphi$ is a variable as defined below, since in the standard semantic game iteration rounds do not affect on the other positions. 
%
\begin{itemize}
    %
    \item[] In a position $(\bV, v, X)$ the game continues as follows. Let $\varphi_X$ and $\psi_X$ denote the body of the base rule and the body of the iteration rule of $X$ respectively. Verifier chooses a formula $\chi$ from the set $\{\varphi_X, \psi_X\}$ and the game continues from the position $(\bV, v, \chi)$.
    %
\end{itemize}
That is, when a variable occurs in the game position, the current verifier can choose if variable is iterated more or not. Notice that the game can continue infinitely many rounds and in that happens then \emph{neither} of the players wins.




\subsection{Linking asynchronous MSC to MCL and the mu-fragment of mu-calculus}

In this section we conclude that $\MSC$ with asynchronous semantic games (or $\AMSC$) have the same expressive power as \textbf{modal computation logic} (or MCL) and \textbf{the mu-fragment of modal mu-calculus} (or $\Sigma^\mu_1$). 
In more detail, $\Sigma^\mu_i$ is the fragment of modal mu-calculus that does not allow $\nu$-operators and negations only in the atomic level.
MCL was introduced in \cite{HELLA2022104882} and it is a fragment of SCL that was recently studied in \cite{jaakkola2022firstorderlogicselfreference}. Furthermore, SCL is a fragment of a Turing complete logic called CL that was introduced in \cite{Kuusisto_2014}. 

The syntax of \(\mscl\) is given by the grammar
\[\varphi ::= p \mid C_L \mid \neg \varphi \mid \varphi \land \varphi \mid \lozenge \varphi \mid L\varphi,\]
where \(p\) is a proposition symbol, \(C_L\) is a claim symbol and \(L\) is a label.


The \textbf{reference formula} of \(C_L\) in a formula \(\varphi\) of \(\mscl\), denoted by \(\Rf_\varphi(C_L)\), is the unique (if it exists) subformula occurrence \(L \psi\) of \(\varphi\) such that there is a directed path from \(L\psi\) to \(C_L\) in the syntax tree of \(\varphi\), and \(L\) does not occur strictly between \(L\psi\) and \(C_L\) on that path.


Let \(\varphi\) be a formula of \(\mscl\) and \((M,w)\) a pointed Kripke model, where \(M = (W,R,V)\). We define the \textbf{unbounded evaluation game} \(\cG_\infty(M,w,\varphi)\) as follows. The game has two players, \textbf{the Falsifier} and \textbf{the Verifier}. The positions of the game are tuples \((\psi,v,\#)\), where \(v \in W\) and \(\# \in \{+,-\}\).

The game begins from the initial position \((\varphi,w,+)\) and it is then played according to the following rules.
\begin{itemize}
        \item In a position \((p, v, +)\), where \(p\) is a propositional symbol, the play of the game ends and the Verifier wins if \(v \in V(p)\). Otherwise the Falsifier wins.
        \item In a position \((p, v, -)\), where \(p\) is a propositional symbol, the play of the game ends and the Falsifier wins if \(v \in V(p)\). Otherwise the Verifier wins.
        \item In a position \((\neg \psi, v, +)\), the game continues from the position \((\psi,v,-)\). Symmetrically, in a position \((\neg \psi, v, -)\) the game continues from the position \((\psi,v,+)\).
        \item In a position \((\psi \land \theta, v, +)\), the Falsifier chooses whether the game continues from the position \((\psi,v,+)\) or \((\theta,v,+)\).
        \item In a position \((\psi \wedge \theta, v, -)\), the Verifier chooses whether the game continues from the position \((\psi,v,-)\) or \((\theta,v,-)\).
        \item In a position \((\lozenge \psi, v, +)\), the Verifier chooses \(u\) such that \((u,v) \in R\) and the game continues from the position \((\psi,u,+)\).
        \item In a position \((\lozenge \psi, v, -)\), the Falsifier chooses \(u\) such that \((u,v) \in R\) and the game continues from the position \((\psi, u, -)\).
        \item Consider a position \((C_L,v,\#)\). If \(\Rf_\varphi(C_L)\) exists, then the next position is \((\Rf_\varphi(C_L),v,\#)\). Otherwise the game stops and neither player wins.
        \item If the position is \((L\psi,v,\#)\), then the next position is \((\psi,v,\#)\).
\end{itemize}
If the Verifier has a winning strategy in \(\cG_\infty(M,v,\varphi)\), then we write \(M,w \Vdash_\infty \varphi\) and say that $\varphi$ accepts $(M, w)$.

Now, it easy to obtain the following theorem from the previous results in \cite{Kuusisto13} and \cite{jaakkola2022firstorderlogicselfreference}.
Below, giving two logics $L_1$ and $L_2$, the notation $L_1 \equiv L_2$ over finite models means that $L_1$ and $L_2$ have the same expressive power over finite models, i.e., for each formula $\varphi_1$ in $L_1$, there is a formula $\varphi_2$ in $L_2$ such that $\varphi_2$ accepts precisely the same Kripke models (or resp. is true precisely in the same Kripke models) as $\varphi_1$, and vice versa.
The theorem below can be generalized for counting modalities trivially.
%
\begin{theorem}
    Over the finite models the following holds:
    \[
    \Sigma^\mu_1 \equiv \AMSC \equiv \mscl \leq \MSC.
    \]
\end{theorem}
%
%
\begin{proof}
    From Proposition $7$ in \cite{Kuusisto13} it follows that $\Sigma^\mu_1 \leq \AMSC$ and $\Sigma^\mu_1 \leq \MSC$. 
    A formula $\varphi$ of $\mscl$ is in \emph{strong negation normal form} if the only negated subformulas of $\varphi$ are atomic FO-formulas.
    From Lemma 5.1 in \cite{jaakkola2022firstorderlogicselfreference} it follows that each formula of $\mscl$ can translated into a formula of $\mscl$ that is strong negation normal form, then it is straightforward to translate $\mscl$ to $\Sigma^\mu_1$. Finally, each program $\Lambda$ of $\AMSC$ translates to a formula of $\mscl$ by expanding the rules of $\Lambda$ and then translating the expanded program recursively to a formula of $\mscl$. 
\end{proof}


\section{Formula size game}

In this section we define a formula size game for $\GGMSC$ and prove that the game characterizes the logical equivalence of classes of pointed models up to programs of $\GGMSC$ of given size. The characterization is formally stated in Theorem \ref{thrm: uniform fs-game characterization}. The game is inspired by the formula size game defined for $\mu$-calculus in \cite{mu-calculus-formula-size}. We start by introducing auxiliary notions and notations, then we formally define the game and consider its properties.

\subsection{Syntax forest}

Informally, the syntax forest of a $\GGMSC$-program contains the directed tree for each body of each rule with the additional back edges which point from each variable to the corresponding bodies of its rules. We assume that the programs are in \emph{strong negation normal form}, \emph{contains precisely one accepting predicate} and \emph{cannot accept in the round zero}.
However, all the notations are easy to generalize for the programs that are not in the strong negation normal form and contains multiple accepting predicates.
These syntax forests are illustrated in Example \ref{example: syntax forest}. 


We start by defining the concepts of trees and forests. Given a non-empty set $L$ of labels, a \textbf{node-labeled directed tree} (over $L$) is a tuple $(V, E, \lambda)$, where $V$ is a non-empty set \textbf{nodes}, $\lambda \colon V \to L$ is \textbf{labeling function} and $E \subseteq V \times V$ is a set of \textbf{edges} defined as follows. There is node $v \in V$ called the \textbf{root} such that for every node $v \neq u \in V$ there is a single \textbf{directed walk}, i.e., sequence of nodes $v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_k$, where $k > 1$, $v_1 = v$, $v_k = u$ and $(v_i, v_{i+1}) \in E$ for every $i \in [k-1]$. A \textbf{directed path} is a directed walk, where every node is a distinct.
A \textbf{node-labeled directed forest} $(V, E, \lambda)$ is a disjoint union of node-labeled trees. 
We can also allow multiple node-labeling functions on trees and forests instead of one.
A node-labeled directed forest $(V, E, \lambda)$ \textbf{with back edges} $B \subseteq V \times V$ is a tuple $(V, E, B, \lambda)$, where for every $(v, u) \in B$, there is no directed walk from $v$ to $u$ through edges $E$.   
%
%


From now on, we may omit the word \emph{directed} in the concepts of directed trees, directed walks and so on above, since we do not consider non-directed trees, walks and so on. 

Now, we may define the \textbf{syntax tree} of a schema. Let $\psi$ a $(\cT, \Pi)$-schema of $\GGMSC$ written in strong negation normal form. The syntax tree of $\psi$ is a node-labeled directed tree $T_\psi = (V_\psi, E_\psi, \lambda_\psi)$ defined as follows. 
The set $V_\psi$ contains the set occurrence of subschemata of $\psi$ and the relation $E$ corresponds to the subschemata relation between subschemata of $\psi$ in a natural way. 
Moreover,
%
%
\[
%
%
\lambda_\psi \colon V_\psi \to \mathrm{Lit}(\Pi) \cup \cT \cup \{\land, \lor\} \cup \bigcup_{k \in \N} \{\Diamond_{\geq k}, \Box_{< k}, \DiamondG_{\geq k}, \BoxG_{< k}\}
%
%
\]
%
%
is a \textbf{labeling function} that labels each node in $V_\psi$ with its main connective, or with a symbol in $\mathrm{Lit}(\Pi) \cup \cT \cup \{\bot, \top\}$ respectively.\footnote{More formally, $V_\psi$ contains a set of sequences of subschemata defined as follows. \textbf{(1)} $(\psi) \in V_\psi$, 
\textbf{(2)} If $(\psi_1, \ldots, \varphi_1 \land \varphi_2) \in V_\psi$ and $\psi_m = \varphi_1 \land \varphi_2$, then $(\psi_1, \ldots, \psi_m, \varphi_1) \in V_\psi$ and $(\psi_1, \ldots, \psi_m, \varphi_2) \in V_\psi$, \textbf{(3)} If $(\psi_1, \ldots, \psi_m) \in V_\psi$ and $\psi_m =  \Diamond_{\geq \ell} \varphi$, then $(\psi_1, \ldots, \psi_m, \varphi) \in V_\psi$. The case for $\lor$ and $\Diamond_{< \ell}$ is defined analogously. Now, $E_\psi$ is defined as follows: if $\vec{\psi}\colonequals (\psi_1, \ldots, \psi_m) \in V_\psi$ and $\vec{\psi'} \colonequals (\psi_1, \ldots,\psi_{m}, \psi_{m+1}) \in  V_\psi$, then $(\vec{\psi}, \vec{\psi'}) \in E_\psi$, and there are no other edges. Now, for each $\vec{\psi} = (\psi_1, \ldots, p_m) \in V_\psi$, $\lambda_\psi(\vec{\psi})$ is defined as follows. If $\psi_m$ is a formula of the form $\varphi \in \mathrm{Lit}(\Pi) \cup \cT \cup \{\bot, \top\}$, then $\lambda(v) = \varphi$. On the other hand, if $\psi_m$ is a formula of the form $ \varphi_1 \land \varphi_2$, then $\lambda(\vec{\psi}) = \land$. The cases for $\lor$ and diamonds $\Diamond_{\geq k}$ and $\Diamond_{< k}$ are analogous to $\land$.}
%
%


Let $\mathsf{base}$ and $\mathsf{iter}$ be new constant symbols.
Let $\cT$ be a finite set of schema variables and $\Pi$ a finite set of proposition symbols. Given a $(\cT, \Pi)$-program $\Lambda$ in the strong negation normal form its \textbf{syntax forest} is a node-labeled forest $(V_\Lambda, E_\Lambda, B_{\Lambda}, \rho_\Lambda, \lambda_\Lambda)$ with back edges $B_\Lambda$ and two labeling functions $\rho_\Lambda \colon V_\Lambda \to \cT \times \{\mathsf{base}, \mathsf{iter} \}$ and $\lambda_\Lambda$ defined as follows.
%
%
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item 
    For each head predicate $X$ in $\Lambda$ we let $\varphi_X$ and $\psi_X$ denote its bodies of base rule and iteration rule respectively. Now, $(V_\Lambda, E_\Lambda, \lambda_\Lambda)$ is the disjoint union of node-labeled trees $\bigcup_{X \in \cT} (V_{\psi_X}, E_{\psi_X}, \lambda_{\psi_X}) \cup (V_{\varphi_X}, E_{\varphi_X}, \lambda_{\varphi_X})$. Moreover, for each $v \in V_{\varphi_X}$ and $u \in V_{\psi_X}$, we have $\rho(v) = (X, \mathsf{base})$ and $\rho(u) = (X, \mathsf{iter})$. 
    %
    %
    %
    \item 
    $B_{\Lambda} \subseteq V \times V$ is the set of back edges for the base rules and the iteration rules defined as follows. For each $w \in V_\Lambda$ such that $\lambda_\Lambda(w) \in \cT$ and for each $u \in V_\Lambda$ that is the root of a tree and $\rho(u) \in  \{X\} \times \{\mathsf{base}, \mathsf{iter} \}$, we define $(w, u) \in B_\Lambda$. 
    % 
    %
    %
\end{itemize}

Let $\Lambda$ be $\Pi$-program of $\GGMSC$ and let $\cF_\Lambda = (V_\Lambda, E_\Lambda, B_\Lambda, \rho_\Lambda, \lambda_\Lambda)$ be its syntax forest. Given a $k \in \N$ and $v \in V_\Lambda$, we define \textbf{$u$-subformula} of $\Lambda$, denoted by $\Lambda_u$, as follows.
%
%
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item If $\lambda_\Lambda(u) \in \mathrm{Lit}(\Pi) \cup \cT$, then $\Lambda_u = \lambda_\Lambda(u)$. Note that $\top$ and $\bot$ are included in the set of literals.
    %
    %
    \item If $\lambda_\Lambda(u) = * \in \{\lor, \land\}$ and $u_1, u_2$ are the successors of $u$, then $\Lambda_u = \Lambda_{u_1} * \Lambda_{u_2}$.
    %
    %
    \item If $\lambda_\Lambda(u) = \star \in \bigcup_{k \in \N} \{\Diamond_{\geq k}, \Box_{< k}, \DiamondG_{\geq k}, \BoxG_{< k}\}$ and $u'$ is the successors of $u$, then $\Lambda_u = \star \Lambda_{u'}$.
    %
    %
\end{itemize}
Since $\Lambda_u$ is a schema, $\Lambda^i_u$ denotes the $i$th iterated formula of $\Lambda_u$.



