\subsection{Clocked models and syntactic sugar}

In this section we define important notions and notations related to the clocked models which intuitively are models associated with an information how long they can be ``iterated'' by a program. We also define some syntactic sugar to simplify the definition of the formula size game which we will define in the next section.


A $\ell$\textbf{-clocked $\Pi$-model} is a tuple $(M, w, \ell)$, where $(M, w)$ is a $\Pi$-model and $\ell \in \N$. We simply say that a Kripke model is clocked if it is $\ell$-clocked model for some $\ell \in \N$.  
Intuitively, $\ell$ tells how many times $(M, w)$ can be ``iterated''. 
%
Let $\cA_0$ be a class of pointed Kripke models.
We let $\mathrm{CM}_\ell(\cA_0) \colonequals \{\, (A, w, \ell) \mid (A, w) \in \cA_0 \,\}$ denote set of $\ell$-clocked models obtained from $\cA$.
%
Respectively, we let $\cA_0^*$ denote the set of all $\ell$-clocked models obtained from $\cA$ for every $\ell \in \N$.
%

Next we define some syntactic sugar. 
Let $\cA \subseteq \cA^*_0$ be a set of clocked models. We let
\[
    \mathrm{iter}(\cA) = \{\, (M, w, \ell-1) \mid (M, w, \ell) \in \cA,\, \ell > 1 \,\}
\]
denote the set of \textbf{iterated} models of $\cA$,
and we let
\[
    \mathrm{init}(\cA) = \{\, (M, w, 0) \mid (M, w, \ell) \in \cA, \ell = 0 \,\}
\]
denote the set of \textbf{initialized} models of $\cA$.


We let 
\[
\Box \cA \colonequals \{\, ((W, R, V), w', \ell) \mid ((W, R, V), w, \ell) \in  \cA, (w, w') \in R \,\} 
\]
denote the set of \textbf{successor models of $\cA$}. Respectively, we let
\[
\BoxG \cA \colonequals \{\, ((W, R, V), w', \ell) \mid ((W, R, V), w, \ell) \in  \cA, w' \in W \,\} 
\]
denote the set of pointed models obtained from $\cA$.


An \textbf{$m$-successor function} over $\cA$ is a function $f \colon \cA \to \cP(\Box \cA)$, where for every $(A,w, \ell) \in \cA$, we have $f(A, w, \ell) \subseteq \Box \{(A, w, \ell)\}$ such that $\abs{f(A, w, \ell)} = m$. Intuitively, an $m$-successor function assigns for each clocked model $m$ successor model. Moreover, we let $\Diamond_f \cA = \bigcup_{(A, w, \ell) \in \cA} f(A, w, \ell)$. Analogously an \textbf{$m$-global function} over $\cA$ is a function $g \colon \cA \to \cP(\BoxG \cA)$, where for every $(A, w, \ell) \in \cA$, we have $g(A, w, \ell) \subseteq \BoxG \{(A, w, \ell)\}$ such that $\abs{g(A, w, \ell)} = m$. Moreover, we also define $\DiamondG_g = \bigcup_{(A, w, \ell) \in \cA} g(A, w, \ell)$. These definitions generalize for partial $m$-successor functions $f \colon \cA \rightharpoonup \cP(\Box \cA)$ and partial $m$-global functions $g \colon \cA \rightharpoonup \cP(\DiamondG \cA)$ in a natural way.



\subsection{Definition of the game}




In this section we formally define the formula size for $\GGMSC$. The main Theorem \ref{thrm: uniform fs-game characterization} shows that the game characterizes the logical equivalence of classes of pointed models of a given program size.


Given a set of proposition symbols $\Pi$, $k \in \N$ and two classes of pointed $\Pi$-models $\cA_0$ and $\cB_0$, we define the formula size game $\mathrm{FS}^{\Pi}_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$ as follows. The game has two players Samson and Delilah. Informally, Samson tries to show that there is a $\Pi$-program of $\GGMSC$ of size at most $k$ that separates $\cA_0$ from $\cB_0$. 

A position of the game is $(\cF, v_0, \mathrm{left}, \mathrm{right}, \mathrm{res})$, where 
%
%
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item $\cF = (V, E, B, \rho, \lambda)$ is a node-labeled forest with back edges $B$, where $\lambda$ is a partial function of the form
    \[
    V \rightharpoonup \mathrm{Lit}(\Pi) \cup \cT \cup \{\land, \lor\} \cup \bigcup_{m \in \N} \{\Diamond_{\geq m}, \Diamond_{< m}, \DiamondG_{\geq m}, \DiamondG_{< m}\}
    \] 
    and $\rho$ is a function of the form $V \to \{\mathsf{base}, \mathsf{iter} \}$,
    %
    %
    \item $v_0$ is a node in $\cF$, 
    %
    %
    \item $\mathrm{left} \colon V \to \cP(\cA^*_0)$, $\mathrm{right} \colon V \to \cP(\cB^*_0)$, and
    %
    %
    \item $\mathrm{res} \colon V \to \N$, where $\mathrm{res}(v) \leq k$ for each $v \in V$.
    %
    %
\end{itemize}



The set of possible \textbf{initial positions} of the game are tuples of the form 
\[
((\{v_0\}, \emptyset, \emptyset, \{(v_0, (Y, \mathsf{iter})) \}, \emptyset), v_0, \{(v_0, \cA)\}, \{(v_0, \cB)\}, \{(v_0, k)\} ),
\]
where $Y \in \mathrm{VAR}$, $\cA \subset \cA^*_0$ and $\cB \subset \cB^*_0$ are finite subsets. 



At the start of every play of the game Delilah first chooses a finite subset $\cA' \subset \cA_0$. 
Then for every pointed model $(A, w) \in \cA'$, Samson gives an integer $\ell_A \in \N$ and gives a variable $Y \in \mathrm{VAR}$. Let $\cA = \{\, (A, w, \ell_A) \mid (A, w) \in \cA \,\}$. 
After that Delilah chooses a finite subset $\cB \subset \cB^*_0$.
Then the initial position of the play is 
%
\[
%
%
((\{v_0\}, \emptyset, \emptyset, \{(v_0, (Y, \mathsf{iter}))\}, \emptyset), v_0, \{(v_0, \cA)\}, \{(v_0, \cB)\}, \{(v_0, k)\}). 
%
%
\]
%
%
Analogously to the semantic games defined before, strictly speaking, there is a starting position 
\[
((\{v_0\}, \emptyset, \emptyset, \emptyset, \emptyset), v_0, \{(v_0, \cA^*_0)\}, \{(v_0, \cB^*_0)\}, \{(v_0, k)\})
\]
where Delilah chooses a subsets $\cA'$ and $\cB$, and Samson gives a clock for every model in $\cA'$ and also gives a variable $Y$, and these choices determine the initial position.




The \textbf{rules} of the game are defined as follows.
Assume that the position of a play of the game is $P = (\cF, v, \mathrm{left}, \mathrm{right}, \mathrm{res})$, we let 
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item $\leftf(v) = \cL$ and $\rightf(v) = \cR$,
    %
    %
    \item $\mathrm{res}(v) = r$ and
    %
    %
    \item $\cF = (V, E, B, \rho, \lambda)$.
    %
    %
\end{itemize}

Below the position following $P$ is denoted by $P' = (\cF', v', \mathrm{left}', \mathrm{right}', \mathrm{res}')$, and $\cF'$ is denoted by $(V', E', B', \rho', \lambda')$.
Samson loses if $\mathrm{res}(v) = 0$ for any $v \in V$ or he cannot make the choices required by the move. Moreover, if in the definition of a move below we do not define a component of $P'$, then the component is the same as in $P$.



If $v \not\in \dom(\lambda)$, then Samson has a choice of the following moves.
%
%
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item \textbf{$\lor$-move}:
    First Samson gives two integers $r_1, r_2 \in \N$ such that $r_1 + r_2 + 1 = r$.
    Then Samson gives two sets $\cL_1, \cL_2 \subseteq \cL$ such that $\cL_1 \cup \cL_2 = \cL$. Then Delilah chooses an index $i \in \{1,2\}$. 

    Let $v_1$ and $v_2$ be fresh nodes not in the domain $V$. Now the position $P'$ following $P$ is defined as follows: $V' = V \cup \{v_1, v_2\}$, 
    $E' = E \cup \{(v, v_1), (v, v_2)\}$, $v' = v_i$, 
    $\rho' = \rho[\rho(v)/v_1, \rho(v)/v_2]$,
    $\lambda' = \lambda[\lor/v]$, 
    $\mathrm{left}' = \mathrm{left}[\cL_1/v_1, \cL_2/v_2, \emptyset/v]$, 
    $\mathrm{right}' = \mathrm{right}[\cR/v_1, \cR/v_2, \emptyset/v]$,
    and $\mathrm{res}' = \mathrm{res}[r_1/v_1, r_2/v_2]$. %
    %
    \item \textbf{$\land$-move}: The move is identical to $\lor$-move except that the node $v$ is labeled with $\land$ and the roles of $\cL$ and $\cR$ are switched as follows: Samson gives two sets $\cR_1, \cR_2 \subseteq \cR$ such that $\cR_1 \cup \cR_2 = \cR$. Then we define $\mathrm{left}' = \mathrm{left}[\cL/v_1, \cL/v_2, \emptyset/v]$ and $\mathrm{right}' = \mathrm{right}[\cR_1/v_1, \cR_1/v_2, \emptyset/v]$.
    %
    %
    \item \textbf{$\Diamond_{\geq m}$-move}: Samson gives an $m$-successor function $f$ for $\cL$ and Delilah gives a \emph{partial} $m$-successor function $g$ for $\cR$. 
    Then Delilah chooses a finite subset $\cL' \subseteq \Diamond_{f} \cL$. For every $(N, v, \ell) \in \dom(g)$, Samson chooses a \emph{non-empty} finite subset $\cR_{(N, v, \ell)} \subseteq \Diamond_{g} \{(N, v, \ell)\}$, then we set $\cR' = \bigcup_{(N, v, \ell)\in \dom(g)} \cR_{(N, v, \ell)}$.

    Let $v'$ be a fresh node not in $V$. The position $P'$ following $P$ is defined as follows: 
    $v' = v$,
    $V' = V \cup \{v'\}$, 
    $E' = E \cup \{(v, v')\}$, 
    $\rho' = \rho[\rho(v)/v']$,
    $\lambda' = \lambda[\Diamond_{\geq m}/v]$,
    $\mathrm{left}' = \mathrm{left}[\cL' /v', \emptyset/v]$, $\mathrm{right}' = \mathrm{right}[\cR'/v', \emptyset/v]$ and $\mathrm{res}' = \mathrm{res}[r-m/v']$. 
    %
    %
    \item \textbf{$\Box_{< m}$-move}: The move is identical to $\Diamond_{\geq m}$ move except that $v$ is labeled with $\Box_{< m}$ and the roles of $\cL$ and $\cR$ are switched as follows. Samson gives an $m$-successor function $f$ for $\cR$ and Delilah gives a \emph{partial} $m$-successor function $h$ for $\cL$. 
    Then Delilah chooses a finite subset $\cR' \subseteq \Diamond_{f} \cR$. For every $(M, w, \ell) \in \dom(h)$, Samson chooses a \emph{non-empty} finite subset $\cL_{(M, w, \ell)} \subseteq \Diamond_{h} \{(M, w, \ell)\}$, then we set $\cL' = \bigcup_{(M, w, \ell)\in \dom(h)} \cR_{(M, w, \ell)}$. Then we define $\mathrm{left}[\cL' /v', \emptyset/v]$ and $\mathrm{right}' = \mathrm{right}[\cR'/v', \emptyset/v]$
    %
    %
    \item \textbf{$\DiamondG_{\geq m}$-move}: Identical to $\Diamond_{\geq m}$-move except that $v$ is labeled with $\DiamondG_{\geq m}$, Samson gives an $m$-global function $f$ over $\cL$ instead of an $m$-successor function over $\cL$ and Delilah gives a partial $m$-global function $h$ over $\cR$. 
    %
    %
    \item \textbf{$\DiamondG_{< m}$-move}: Identical to $\Box_{< m}$-move except that $v$ is labeled with $\BoxG_{< m}$, Samson gives an $m$-global function $f$ over $\cR$ instead of an $m$-successor function over $\cR$ and Delilah gives a partial $m$-global function $h$ over $\cL$.
    %
    %
    \item \textbf{$\mathrm{Lit}(p)$-move}: Samson chooses a literal $\varphi \in \{p, \neg p, \bot, \top\}$, where $p \in \Pi$.
    In the position $P'$ following $P$ we define $\lambda' = \lambda[\varphi/v]$.
    If $\varphi$ separates $\cL$ from $\cR$, then Samson wins.
    Otherwise, Delilah wins. 
    %
    %
    %
    \item \textbf{$X$-move}: 
    Samson chooses a variable $X \in \mathrm{VAR}$. 
    If $\rho(v) = (Y,\mathsf{base})$ for any $Y \in \mathrm{VAR}$, 
    then Samson loses.
    Delilah can choose to \textbf{challenge} Samson.


    First assume that Delilah does not challenge Samson. If $\mathrm{iter}(\cL) = \mathrm{iter}(\cR) = \emptyset$, then Samson wins.
    Assume that there are a nodes $u, v' \in V$ such that $(u, v') \in B$ and $\rho(v') = (X,\mathsf{iter})$. If there does not exists such a nodes then we let $v'$ denote a fresh node.
    The position $P'$ is defined as follows: 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $v' = v$,
        %
        \item $V' = V\cup \{v'\}$ (if $v'$ exists we instead define $V' = V$), $B' = B \cup \{(v, v')\}$,
        %
        \item $\rho' = \rho[(X, \mathsf{iter})/v']$ (if $v'$ exists we instead define $\rho' = \rho$),
        %
        \item $\lambda' = \lambda[X/v]$.
        %
        \item $\leftf' = \leftf[\mathrm{iter}(\cL) \cup \leftf(v')/v', \mathrm{init}(\cL)/v]$, 
        %
        \item $\rightf' = \rightf[\mathrm{iter}(\cR) \cup \rightf(v') /v', \mathrm{init}(\cR)/v]$. 
        %
        \item $\res' = \res[r-1/v']$ (if $v'$ exists we instead define $\res' = \res$).
        %
    \end{itemize}


    Assume that Delilah challenges Samson. If $\mathrm{init}(\cL) = \mathrm{init}(\cR) = \emptyset$, then Samson wins.
    Let $v'$ be a fresh node not in $V$. The position $P'$ is defined as follows: 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $v' = v$,
        %
        \item $V' = V \cup \{v'\}$, $B' = B \cup \{(v, v')\}$,
        %
        \item $\rho' = \rho[(X, \mathsf{base})/v']$,
        %
        \item $\lambda' = \lambda[X/v]$,
        %
        \item $\leftf' = \leftf[\mathrm{init}(\cL)/v', \emptyset/v]$,
        %
        \item $\rightf' = \rightf[\mathrm{init}(\cR)/v', \emptyset/v]$,
        %
        \item $\mathrm{res}' = \mathrm{res}[r-1/v']$.
        %
    \end{itemize}
    The other components in $P'$ that we did not mention are the same as in $P$.    
    %
    %
\end{itemize}


If $v \in \dom(\lambda)$, then Samson has to play a move according to the label given by $\lambda(v)$.  
%
%
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item $\lambda(v) = \lor$: 
    Samson gives two sets $\cL_1, \cL_2 \subseteq \cL$ such that $\cL_1 \cup \cL_2 = \cL$. Then Delilah chooses an index $i \in \{1,2\}$. 
    Let $v_1$ and $v_2$ be the successors of $v$. Now the position $P'$ following $P$ is defined as follows: $v' = v_i$,  
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\mathrm{left}' = \mathrm{left}[\cL_1 \cup \leftf(v_1)/v_1, \cL_2 \cup \leftf(v_2)/v_2, \emptyset/v]$ and
        \item $\mathrm{right}' = \mathrm{right}[\cR \cup \rightf(v_1)/v_1, \cR \cup \rightf(v_2)/v_2, \emptyset/v]$. 
    \end{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item $\lambda(v) = \land$:
    The case is identical to $\lambda(v) = \lor$ except that the roles of $\cL$ and $\cR$ are switched in an analogous way as in the unlabeled case.
    %
    %
    \item $\lambda(v) = \Diamond_{\geq m}$:
    Samson gives an $m$-successor function $f$ for $\cL$ and Delilah gives a \emph{partial} $m$-successor function $h$ for $\cR$. 
    Then Delilah chooses a finite subset $\cL' \subseteq \Diamond_{f} \cL$. For every $(N, v, \ell) \in \dom(h)$, Samson chooses a \emph{non-empty} finite subset $\cR_{(N, v, \ell)} \subseteq \Diamond_{h} \{(N, v, \ell)\}$, then we set $\cR' = \bigcup_{(N, v, \ell)\in \dom(h)} \cR_{(N, v, \ell)}$.  

    Let $v'$ be the successor of $v$. The position $P'$ following $P$ is defined as follows: 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\mathrm{left}' = \mathrm{left}[\cL' \cup \leftf(v) /v', \emptyset/v]$ and 
        \item $\mathrm{right}' = \mathrm{right}[\cR' \cup \rightf(v) /v', \emptyset/v]$. 
    \end{itemize}
    The other components in $P'$ that we did not mention are the same as in the $P$.
    %
    %
    \item  $\lambda(v) = \Box_{< m}$: The case is identical to the case $\lambda(v) = \Diamond_{\geq m}$ except that the roles of $\cL$ and $\cR$ are switched in an analogous way as in the unlabeled case.
    %
    %
    \item $\lambda(v) = \DiamondG_{\geq m}$ and $\lambda(v) = \BoxG_{<m}$ are analogous obtained from the unlabeled cases to $\lambda(v) = \Diamond_{\geq m}$ and $\lambda(v) = \Box_{< m}$.
    %
    %
    \item $\lambda(v) \in \mathrm{VAR}$: 
    Identical to $X$-move.
    %
    %
\end{itemize}
Note that Samson might not be able to perform $\Diamond_{\geq m}$ (or resp. $\Diamond_{< m}$) move if there is a model in $\cL$ (or resp. in $\cR$) which does not have $m$ successor models.

Now, we have defined the formula size game and can start to study its properties. We first start with an proposition which shows that every play of each formula size game ends in a finite number of steps.
%
%
\begin{proposition}\label{prop: finite game tree}
    Given a set of proposition symbols $\Pi$ and two classes of pointed $\Pi$-models $\cA_0$ and $\cB_0$, every play of the game $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$ is finite.
\end{proposition}
%
%
\begin{proof}
    Assume for contradiction that some play of the game continues indefinitely. Thus, during the play of the game there must be a variable $X$ that has been iterated indefinitely. Therefore, there is a position $(\cF, v, \leftf, \rightf, \res)$ of the play where, $X$-move is played such that $\mathrm{iter}(\leftf(v)) = \mathrm{iter}(\rightf(v)) = \emptyset$ in which case Samson wins.
\end{proof}


Before we prove the correctness of formula size game, we define the notions of uniform strategies. Informally, in a formula size game, Samson's strategy is uniform if he has a program of $\GGMSC$ in his mind and during the game he constructs the syntax forest of that program.


More formally, let $\Lambda$ be a $\Pi$-program of $\GGMSC$ and let $\cF_\Lambda = (V_\Lambda, E_\Lambda, B_\Lambda, \rho_\Lambda, \lambda_\Lambda)$ be its syntax forest.
Recall that we assume that $\Lambda$ is in the strong negation normal form, contains precisely one accepting predicate and cannot accept in the round zero.
Now, let 
$P = (\cF, v, \mathrm{left}, \mathrm{right}, \mathrm{res})$ be a position of a play of the $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$-game, where $\cF = (V, E, B, \rho, \lambda)$. A function $f \colon V \to V_\Lambda$ is a \textbf{position embedding (w.r.t. $\Lambda$ and $P$)} if it satisfies the following conditions.
%
%
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item If $u$ is a root of the tree in $\cF$, then $f(u)$ is a root of a tree in $\cF_\Lambda$.
    %
    %
    \item $f$ is an embedding, i.e., satisfies the following properties.
    %
    %
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $f$ is an injection.
        %
        \item For every $u, u' \in V$, $(u, u') \in S$ iff $(f(u), f(u')) \in S_\Lambda$, where $S \in \{E, B\}$.
        %
        \item For every $u \in \dom(\rho)$, $\rho(u) = \rho_\Lambda(f(u))$.
        %
        \item For every $u \in \dom(\lambda)$, $\lambda(u) = \lambda_\Lambda(f(u))$.
    \end{itemize} 
    %
    %
    \item For each $u \in V$, $\abs{\Lambda_{g(u)}} \leq \res(u)$.
    %
    %
\end{itemize}
Let $\sigma$ be a strategy for Samson in $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$.
We say that $\sigma$ is \textbf{uniform w.r.t. $\Lambda$}, if in every position in every play of the game there is a position embedding w.r.t. $\Lambda$ such that in each play of the game the initial position is of the form 
\[
%
%
((\{v_0\}, \emptyset, \emptyset, \{(v_0, (Y, \mathsf{iter}))\}, \emptyset), v_0, \{(v_0, \cA)\}, \{(v_0, \cB)\}, \{(v_0, k)\}), 
%
%
\]
where $Y$ is the accepting predicate of $\Lambda$.
Moreover, we say that $\sigma$ is uniform if it is uniform w.r.t. some program of $\GGMSC$. The \textbf{game tree} induced by $\sigma$ is a tree where the nodes are positions of $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$ and there is an edge between the positions $P$ and $P'$ if $P'$ follows immediately after $P$. 


Now, we shall prove that the formula size game (over uniform strategies) characterizes the logical equivalence of $\GGMSC$-programs of given program size.
%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{thrm: uniform fs-game characterization}
    Let $\Pi$ be a set of proposition symbols and let $\cA_0$ and $\cB_0$ be classes of pointed $\Pi$-models and $k \in \N$. The following claims are equivalent.
    \begin{enumerate}
        %
        %
        \item Samson has a uniform winning strategy in $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$.
        %
        %
        %
        \item There is a $\Pi$-program of $\GGMSC$ of size at most $k$ that separates $\cA_0$ from $\cB_0$.
        %
        %
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
%
%
%
\begin{proof}
    %
    %
    ``$1 \Rightarrow 2$''
    Assume that Samson has a uniform winning strategy $\sigma$ in $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$ w.r.t. a $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GGMSC$. 
    We let $\cF_\Lambda = (V_\Lambda, E_\Lambda, B_\Lambda, \rho_\Lambda, \lambda_\Lambda)$ denote the syntax forest of $\Lambda$.
    By Proposition \ref{prop: finite game tree} every play of the game is finite and thus the game tree is finite.
    Therefore, we may prove by induction on the positions of the game tree induced by $\sigma$ (starting from leaves) that the following condition holds in every position $P = (\cF, v, \leftf, \rightf, \res)$ of the game tree.
    %
    \begin{equation}\tag{$*$}\label{induction_hypo_1}
    \begin{split}
    M, w \models \Lambda_{g(v)}^\ell &\text{ for each } (M, w, \ell) \in \cL, \\
    N, v \not\models \Lambda_{g(v)}^\ell &\text{ for each } (N, v, \ell ) \in \cR, \\
    \end{split}
    \end{equation}
    where $\leftf(v) = \cL$, $\rightf(v) = \cR$ and $g$ is a position embedding w.r.t. $\Lambda$ and $P$. 


    First assume that $v \notin \dom(\lambda)$.
    \begin{itemize}
        %
        %
        \item $\lambda(g(v)) = \varphi \in \mathrm{Lit}(p)$ for some $p \in \Pi$: Since the game tree is induced by $\sigma$, the next move of Samson is $\mathrm{Lit}(p)$-move choosing the literal $\varphi$. Since $\sigma$ is a winning strategy, $\varphi$ separates $\cL$ from $\cR$. 
        Thus Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in the position $P$.
        %
        %
        \item $\lambda(g(v)) = \lor$: Since the game tree is induced by $\sigma$, the next move of Samson is $\lor$-move. Let $s_1$ and $s_2$ be the successors of $g(v)$. Let $\cL_1, \cL_2 \subseteq \cL$ be the selections of Samson according to $\sigma$. 
        %

        By the induction hypothesis Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in the following positions with respect to the selections of Samson.
        %
        That is, for every $i \in \{1, 2\}$ and for each $(M, w, \ell) \in \cL_i$, $M, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{s_i}$. Also, for each $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR$, $N, v \not\models \Lambda^\ell_{s_i}$. 
        %
        Since $\cL_1 \cup \cL_2 = \cL$ and $\Lambda_{g(v)}^\ell = \Lambda_{s_1}^\ell \lor \Lambda_{s_2}^\ell$, we have for each $(M, w, \ell) \in \cL$ and $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR$ that $M, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$ and $N, v \not\models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$. Thus Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in the position $P$.
        %
        %
        \item $\lambda(g(v)) = \Diamond_{\geq m}$: Since the game tree is induced by $\sigma$, the next move of Samson is $\Diamond_{\geq m}$-move. 
        %
        %
        Let $s'$ be the successor of $g(v)$. Let $f$ be the $m$-successor function for $\cL$ selected by Samson according to $\sigma$.
        %
        %
        Let $h$ be a partial $m$-successor function for $\cR$ given by Delilah. 
        %
        By induction hypothesis Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in every following position, no matter which subset Delilah chooses $\cL' \subseteq \Diamond_{f} \cL$, 
        for each $(N, v, \ell) \in \dom(h)$, Samson can choose a non-empty finite subset $\cR_{(N, v, \ell)} \subseteq \Diamond_{h} \{(N, v, \ell)\}$ according to $\sigma$. 

        Since $f$ is an $m$-successor function it means that for each $(M, w, \ell) \in \cL$ there are at least $m$ models $(M, w', \ell) \in \Diamond_f\cL$ such that $w'$ is a successor of $w$ and $M, w' \models \Lambda^\ell_{s'}$. Thus $M, w \models \Diamond_{\geq m} \Lambda^\ell_{s'}$, i.e., $M, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$. Similarly, we can show that for each $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR$ it holds that $N, v \not\models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$. Therefore, Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in the position $P$.
        %
        %
        \item $\lambda(v) = X \in \mathrm{VAR}$: Since the game tree is induced by $\sigma$, the next move of Samson is $X$-move choosing $\varphi$.    
        %
        %
        There are two possible following positions: either Delilah challenges Samson or not.


        First assume that Delilah challenges Samson. Let $s'$ be the successor of $g(v)$ over back edges $B_\Lambda$ such that $\rho_\Lambda(s') = (X, \mathsf{base})$. By induction hypothesis Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in the position following $P$, i.e., for every $(M, w, 0) \in \mathrm{init}(\cL)$, $M, w \models \Lambda^0_{s'}$ and for every $(N, v, 0) \in \mathrm{init}(\cR)$, $N, v \not\models \Lambda^0_{s'}$. Therefore, for every $(M, w, 0) \in \cL$ and $(N, v, 0) \in \cR$, $M, w \models \Lambda^0_{g(v)}$ and $N, v \not\models \Lambda^0_{g(v)}$. Thus, Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds.


        Assume that Delilah does not challenge Samson. By induction hypothesis Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in the position following $P$, i.e., for every $\ell > 1$, and for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \mathrm{iter}(\cL)$, $M, w \models \Lambda^{\ell-1}_{s'}$ and for every $(N, v, \ell) \in \mathrm{iter}(\cR)$, $N, v \not\models \Lambda^{\ell-1}_{s'}$. Therefore, for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \cL$ and $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR$, we have $M, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$ and $N, v \not\models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$. Thus, Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds. 

        By the both cases Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in the position $P$.
        %
        %
    \end{itemize}
    The dual moves, i.e., $\land$-move and $\Box_{< m}$ are proved analogously to $\lor$ and $\Diamond_{\geq m}$ respectively. Moreover, $\DiamondG_{\geq m}$-move and $\BoxG_{< m}$ are analogous to standard counting operator moves.
    The cases where $v \in \dom(\lambda)$ are proved analogously to non-labeled moves.

    Now, consider the starting position before the actual game positions, where Delilah first chooses a finite subset $\cA' \subseteq \cA_0$. 
    Since $\sigma$ is a uniform winning strategy Samson can choose an integer $\ell_A \in \N$ for every $(A, w) \in \cA'$ such that Samson wins the game when starting with the clocked models $\{\, (A, w, \ell_A) \mid (A, w) \in \cA\,\}$, no matter which subset $\cB \subseteq \cB^*_0$ Delilah chooses.
    Thus, by the induction hypothesis Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_1}) holds in these clocked models. Therefore, $\Lambda$ separates $\cA_0$ from $\cB_0$.


    ``$2 \Rightarrow 1$''
    Next, we prove the converse direction. Let $\Lambda$ be a $\Pi$-program of $\GGMSC$ of size at most $k$ that separates $\cA_0$ from $\cB_0$. Let $\cF_\Lambda = (V_\Lambda, E_\Lambda, B_\Lambda, \rho_\Lambda, \lambda_\Lambda)$ be the syntax forest of $\Lambda$.

    We show by induction that Samson has a uniform winning strategy in $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$ w.r.t. $\Lambda$ and in every position $P = (\cF, v, \leftf, \rightf, \res)$ of the game the following condition (similar to the previous direction) holds in the every position of the game.
    %
    \begin{equation}\tag{$*$}\label{induction_hypo_2}
    \begin{split}
    M, w \models \Lambda_{g(v)}^\ell &\text{ for each } (M, w, \ell) \in \cL, \\
    N, v \not\models \Lambda_{g(v)}^\ell &\text{ for each } (N, v, \ell) \in \cR, \\
    \end{split}
    \end{equation}
    where $\leftf(v) = \cL$, $\rightf(v) = \cR$ and $g$ is a position embedding w.r.t. $\Lambda$. 
    In the proof we let $P' = (\cF', v', \leftf', \rightf', \res')$ denote the position following $P$ and let $\res(v) = r$ and we let $g'$ denote a position embedding in the position $P'$.

    In the starting position Delilah first chooses a subset $\cA' \subseteq \cA_0$. Then Samson chooses the smallest $\ell_A \in \N$ for every $(A, w) \in \cA'$ such that $\Lambda$ accepts $(A, w)$ in round $\ell_A$ and the accepting predicate $Y$ of $\Lambda$. Now, we see that for all $(B, w, \ell_B) \in \cB_0^*$, the pointed model $(B, w)$ is not accepted by $\Lambda$ in the round $\ell_B$. Thus, no matter which subset $\cB \subseteq \cB^*_0$ Delilah chooses, in each initial position 
    \[
    ((\{v_0\}, \emptyset, \emptyset, \{(v_0, (Y, \mathsf{iter})) \} \}, \emptyset), v_0, \{(v_0, \cA)\}, \{(v_0, \cB)\}, \{(v_0, k)\}), 
    \]
    %
    where $\cA = \{\, (A, w, \ell_A) \mid (A, w) \in \cA' \,\}$,
    Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds. 


    Assume that Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the current position $P$. We prove that Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the position following $P$ if Samson plays according to the uniform strategy $\sigma$ w.r.t. $\Lambda$.

    First assume that $v \notin \dom(\lambda)$.
    %
    %
    \begin{itemize}
        %
        %
        \item $\lambda_\Lambda(g(v)) \in \mathrm{Lit}(p)$ for some $p \in \Pi$: By induction hypothesis $\lambda_\Lambda(g(v))$ separates $\cL$ from $\cR$.
        %
        %
        \item $\lambda_\Lambda(g(v)) = \lor$: By induction hypothesis Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the current position. Let $s_1$ and $s_2$ be the successors of $g(v)$. Samson splits the set $\cL$ as follows $\cL_1 = \{\, (M, w, \ell) \in \cL \mid M, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{s_1} \,\}$ and $\cL_2 = \{\, (M, w, \ell) \in \cL \mid A, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{s_2} \,\}$. On the other hand, for every $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR$ and for every $i \in \{1,2\}$ it holds that $N, v \not\models \Lambda^\ell_{s_i}$, and since $\cR$ is not split the Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the following position. 

        Let $v_1$ and $v_2$ be fresh nodes not in $V$.
        For uniformity, we set $g' = g[v_1/s_1, v_2/s_2]$, $r_1 = \abs{\Lambda_{s_1}}$ and $r_2 = r - 1 - r_1$. Since $\abs{\Lambda_{g(v)}} \leq r$, then $\abs{\Lambda_{s_2}} = \abs{\Lambda_{g(v)}} - \abs{\Lambda_{s_1}} - 1 \leq r - r_1 - 1 \leq r_2$.
        %
        %
        \item $\lambda_\Lambda(g(v)) = \Diamond_{\geq m}$: By induction hypothesis Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the current position. Let $s$ be the successor of $g(v)$. 
        %
        Now, $\Lambda_{g(v)} \colonequals \Diamond_{\geq m} \Lambda_s$.
        %
        Therefore, for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \cL$ and for every $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR$, it holds $M, w \models \Diamond_{\geq m} \Lambda_s$ and $N, v \not\models \Diamond_{\geq m} \Lambda_s$.
        Thus, for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \cR$, there are $w_1, \ldots, w_m$ distinct successors of $w$ such that for every $i \in [m]$, $M, w_i \models \Lambda_s$ and for every $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR$, for some $m' < m$ there are precisely $v_1, \ldots, v_{m'}$ distinct successors of $v$ such that for every $i \in [m']$, $N, v_i \models \Lambda_s$.


        Thus, Samson can define an $m$-successor function $f$ of $\cL$ such that for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \cL$ and for every $(M, w', \ell) \in f(M, w, \ell)$ we have $M, w' \models \Lambda^\ell_s$.
        %
        On the other hand, for every non-empty partial $m$-successor function $h$ for $\cR$, for each $(N, v, \ell) \in \dom(h)$, there is $(N, v', \ell) \in h(N, v, \ell)$ such that $N, v' \not\models \Lambda^\ell_s$.

        Therefore, Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the next position.
        %
        To ensure uniformity, we set $g' = g[v'/s]$, where $v'$ is a fresh node. Moreover, we have $\abs{\Lambda_{s}} = \abs{\Lambda_{g(v)}} - m \leq k - 1$.
        %
        %
        \item 
        $\lambda_\Lambda(g(v)) = X \in \mathrm{VAR}$: By induction hypothesis Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the current position. Let $\varphi_X$ be the body of the base rule of $X$ and let $\psi_X$ be the body of the iteration rule of $X$. 

        Now, for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \cL$ with $\ell > 0$, we have $M, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$ iff $M, w \models \psi_X^{\ell-1}$. Also, for every $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR$ with $\ell > 0$, we have $N, v \not\models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$ iff $N, v \not\models \psi_X^{\ell-1}$. Moreover, for every $(M, w, 0) \in \cL$, we have $M, w \models \Lambda^0_{g(v)}$ iff $M, w \models \varphi_X$ and for every $(N, v, 0) \in \cR$, we have $N, v \not\models \Lambda^0_{g(v)}$ iff $N, v \not\models \varphi_X$. 


        Therefore, if $\lambda_\Lambda(g(v)) = X$, then for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \mathrm{iter}(\cL)$, we have $M, w \models \psi_X^{\ell}$ and for every $(M, w, 0) \in \mathrm{init}(\cL)$, we have $M, w \models \varphi_X$.
        %
        Analogously, for every $(N, v, \ell) \in \mathrm{iter}(\cR)$ we have $N, v \not\models \psi_X^{\ell'}$ and for every $(N, v, 0) \in \mathrm{init}(\cR)$, we have $N,v \models \varphi_X$. Thus, Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the next position.

        We can ensure the uniformity as follows.
        Let $s$ be a successor of $g(v)$ through $B$ back edges such that $\rho_\Lambda(s) = (X, \mathsf{iter})$ and let $s'$ be a successor of $g(v)$ through $B$ back edges such that $\rho_\Lambda(s') = (X, \mathsf{base})$. Let $v'$ be a fresh node. If Delilah challenges Samson, then we set $g' = g[v'/s']$. If Delilah does not challenge Samson, then there are two cases. Either $s$ is in the range of $g$ or not. Assume that $s$ is in the range of $g$, then the uniformity holds trivially. If $s$ is not in the range of $g$, then we set $g' = g[v'/s']$. Moreover, we have $\abs{\Lambda_s} = \abs{\Lambda_{g(v)}} - 1 \leq k - 1$. 
        %
        %
    \end{itemize}
    The cases for dual operators are analogous and the cases for $\DiamondG_{\geq m}$ and $\BoxG_{< m}$ are analogous to standard counting operators.

    If $g(v) \in \dom(\lambda_\Lambda)$, then the arguments are similar. The only difference is that there might be models in the case of disjunction and conjunction that are left to wait in the branch not chosen by Delilah. We consider the case where during a play of the game we enter into a node where there are models already. 

    Case $\lambda(v) = \lor$: The moves for Samson are the same as in the unlabeled case. Let $\cL_1, \cL_2 \subseteq \cL$ be the sets of models chosen by Samson as in the unlabeled case. 
    %
    Let $v_1$ and $v_2$ be the successors of $v$.
    %
    By induction hypothesis and the unlabelled case, Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds for the sets $\leftf(v_i)$ and $\rightf(v_i)$, i.e., for every $i \in \{1,2\}$, for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \leftf(v_i)$, $A, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v_i)}$ and also for every $(N, v, \ell) \in \rightf(v_i)$, $N, v \models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v_i)}$. 
    %
    %    
    Therefore, for every $i \in \{1,2\}$, for every $(M, w, \ell) \in \cL_i \cup \leftf(v_i)$, $M, w \models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$. Also, for every $i \in \{1,2\}$ and for every $(N, v, \ell) \in \cR \cup \rightf(v_i)$, $N, v \not\models \Lambda^\ell_{g(v)}$. Thus, Condition (\ref{induction_hypo_2}) holds in the next position.

    The case for conjunctions is analogous.
\end{proof}


\begin{remark}
The formula size game for $\GGMSC$ is trivial to modify for $\GMSC$, $\MSC$ and $\mathrm{SC}$, and for these fragments of $\GGMSC$, an analogous result is obtained for both Theorem \ref{thrm: uniform fs-game characterization}.
\end{remark}


Next, recall some notions related to bisimulations. Later, Theorem \ref{thrm: finite-bisimilar-delilah} informally states that in certain positions of the game appropriate finite bisimulations between the left and right clocked models suffice to guarantee winning strategy for Delilah.

Recall that global counting bisimilarity is defined as follows. Given two pointed $\Pi$-models $((W, R, V), w)$ and $((W', R', V'), w')$, we say that they are \textbf{global counting bisimilar} if there exists a relation $Z$ defined as follows: 
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item $(w, w') \in Z$.
    %
    %
    \item \emph{atomic}: For all $(w, w') \in Z$, $V(w) = V'(w')$. 
    %
    %
    \item \emph{local forth}: If $(v, v') \in Z$ for all $k \in \N$, for each $k$ distinct out-neighbours $v_1, \ldots, v_k \in W$ of $v$, there are $k$ distinct out-neighbours $v'_1, \ldots, v'_k \in W'$ of $v'$, such that $(v_1, v'_1), \ldots, (v_k, v'_k) \in Z$.
    %
    %
    \item \emph{local back}: If $(v, v') \in Z$, for all $k \in \N$, for each $k$ distinct out-neighbour $v'_1, \ldots, v'_k \in W'$ of $v'$, there are $k$ distinct out-neighbours $v_1, \ldots, v_k \in W$ of $v$, such that $(v_1, v'_1), \ldots, (v_k, v'_k) \in Z$.
    %
    %
    \item \emph{global forth}: If $(v, v') \in Z$ for all $k \in \N$, for each $k$ distinct nodes $v_1, \ldots, v_k \in W$, there are $k$ distinct nodes $v'_1, \ldots, v'_k \in W'$, such that $(v_1, v'_1), \ldots, (v_k, v'_k) \in Z$.
    %
    %
    \item \emph{global back}: If $(v, v') \in Z$, for all $k \in \N$, for each $k$ distinct nodes $v'_1, \ldots, v'_k \in W'$, there are $k$ distinct nodes $v_1, \ldots, v_k \in W$ of $v$, such that $(v_1, v'_1), \ldots, (v_k, v'_k) \in Z$.
    %
    %
\end{itemize}
Given $\ell \in \N$, \textbf{$\ell$-counting global bisimilarity} between two pointed $\Pi$-models $((W, R, V), w)$ and $((W', R', V'), w')$ is defined analogously to counting global bisimilarity, but we bound $k$ in local forth, local back, global forth and global back with $k \leq \ell$. 
%
Moreover, given a $n \in \N$, \textbf{counting global $n$-bisimilarity} between $(M, w)$ and $(N, v)$ is a relation $Z_k$ obtained from a counting bisimilarity $Z$ between $(M, w)$ and $(N, v)$ by restriction the nodes that are reachable from $w$ and $v$ in $k$ steps.
Analogously, we define $\ell$-counting global $n$-bisimilarity.


Counting bisimilarity are defined analogously to the global counting bisimiliarity but global forth and global back conditions are excluded. Analogously, $\ell$-counting bisimilarity, counting $n$-bisimilarity and $\ell$-counting $n$-bisimilarity are defined.



Now, we can prove the following theorem which intuitively states that if there are two global counting $n$-bisimilar pointed models (one on the left and another on the right) in the current positions and both models have the same clock which is less than $n$, then Delilah has a winning strategy from that position.
In the theorem below, we let $P =(\cF, v, \mathrm{left}, \mathrm{right}, \mathrm{res})$ denote a position in $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$. 
%
\begin{theorem}\label{thrm: finite-bisimilar-delilah}
    %
    Let $P =(\cF, v, \mathrm{left}, \mathrm{right}, \mathrm{res})$ be a position in $\mathrm{FS}^\Pi_k(\cA_0, \cB_0)$ and let $K = \res(v)-1 + M$, where $M$ is the number of modalities appearing in $\cF$.
    If there are are clocked models $(A, w_A, \ell) \in \leftf(v)$ and $(B, w_B, \ell) \in \rightf(v)$ such that $(A, w_A)$ and $(B, w_B)$ are global counting $n$-bisimilar, for some $n \geq K\ell$, then Delilah has a winning strategy from position $P$.
\end{theorem}
%
%
\begin{proof}
%
%
Delilah just have to maintain the following invariance: in each position $P' = (\cF', v', \leftf', \rightf', \res')$ after $P$ we have: $(A, w_A, \ell') \in \leftf'(v')$ and $(B, w_B, \ell') \in \rightf'(v')$ for some $\ell' \leq \ell$. 
%
%
Assume that the invariance holds in the current position $P$, i.e., $(A, w_A, \ell) \in \leftf(v)$ and $(B, w_B, \ell) \in \rightf(v)$ and we show that the invariance can be maintained in the next position. Furthermore, we let $\leftf(v) = \cL$ and $\rightf(v) = \cR$. 
\begin{itemize}
    %
    %
    \item Clearly, Delilah wins if a $\mathrm{Lit}(p)$-move is played.
    %
    %
    \item If a $\lor$-move or a $\land$-move is played, Delilah always picks the new position where both $(A, w_A)$ and $(B, w_B)$ appears. 
    %
    %
    \item If a $\Diamond_{\geq m}$-move is played, then either Samson loses (if the $(A, w_A, \ell)$ does not have successor models) or picks an $m$-successor function $f$ for $\leftf(v)$ and assume that $f(\{(A, w_A, \ell)\}) = \{(A, w_A^1, \ell), \ldots, (A, w_A^m, \ell) \}$. 
    Since $(A, w_A, \ell)$ and $(B, w_B, \ell)$ are global counting $n$-bisimilar, for some $n \leq K \ell$, there is a global counting $n$-bisimilar pointed model $(B, w_B^i, \ell)$ for each $(A, w_A^i, \ell)$, where $(B, w_B^i, \ell)$ is a successor model of $(B, w_B, \ell)$.
    Then Delilah gives a partial successor function $h$ for $\rightf(v)$ such that for $h(\{(B, w_B, \ell)\}) = \{(B, w_B^1, \ell), \ldots, (B, w_B^m, \ell)\}$. Finally, Delilah chooses a $\Diamond_f \cL$ and thus no matter which subsets Samson chooses the invariance holds in the following position.
    %
    %
    \item If a $X$-move is played, then Delilah does not challenge Samson if $\ell > 0$. Thus, clearly the invariance holds in the following position.
    %
    %
\end{itemize}
%
%
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem \ref{thrm: finite-bisimilar-delilah} trivially generalizes for $\GMSC$ and counting bisimilarity, and $\MSC$ and (standard) bisimilarity. 
\end{remark}




\section{Connecting tape-bounded Turing machines and GMSC}

In this section we give a logical characterization for deterministic linear bounded automata (or $\mathrm{LBA}s$) via $\GMSC$ over words.
Informally, linear bounded automata are tape bounded Turing machines, i.e., the length of the tape is bounded by some linear function on the length of the input string.
The main results of this section are formally stated in Theorems \ref{...} and \ref{...}.
Intuitively, the first theorem shows that over two-way word-models $\GMSC$-programs can simulate LBAs and the second theorem shows that over one-way word-models which lengths are extended $\GMSC$-programs can simulate LBAs. 
Therefore, the introduced formula size game for $\GMSC$ can be used as a formula size game for deterministic $\LBA$s.

\subsection{Tape-bounded Turing machines}

In this section we define tape-bounded Turing machines.
First we fix some constant symbols.
We let $\br$, $\bl$ and $\bs$ denote the \textbf{direction symbols} for ``right'', ``left'' and ``stay'' respectively.


A \textbf{tape-bounded Turing machine} (or tape-bounded $\mathrm{TM}$) is a tuple 
\[
M = (f, Q, \Sigma, \blacksquare, E_\bl, E_\br, \Gamma, q_0, \delta, F),
\]
where
%
%
\begin{itemize}
    %
    \item $f \colon \N \to \N$ is a \textbf{tape-bound function},
    %
    \item $Q$ is a set of \textbf{states},
    %
    \item $\Sigma$ is a \textbf{tape alphabet},
    %
    %
    \item $\blacksquare$, $E_\bl$ and $E_\br$, symbols in $\Sigma$, are the \textbf{blank symbol}, the \textbf{left end marker} and the \textbf{right end marker}, respectively, 
    %
    \item $\Gamma \subseteq \Sigma \setminus \{\blacksquare, E_\bl, E_\br\}$ is an \textbf{input alphabet},
    %
    \item $q_0$ is an \textbf{initial state},
    %
    \item $F$ is a set of \textbf{accepting states}.
\end{itemize}
Finally, $\delta \colon Q \times \Sigma \to \cP(Q \times \Sigma \times \{ \bl, \bs, \br\})$ is a \textbf{transition function} defined as follows. A transition function is restricted such that cannot print other symbols over the end markers and cannot move on the left of the left end marker and on the right of the right end marker. More formally, for every $q \in Q$, we define
$\delta(q, E_\bl) \subseteq Q \times \{E_\bl\} \times \{\bs, \br\}$ and $\delta(q, E_\br) \subseteq Q \times \{E_\br\} \times \{\bs, \bl\}$. 

Note that tape-bounded Turing machines allow non-determinism. A tape-bounded Turing machine is \textbf{deterministic} if for each $q \in Q$ and for each $a \in \Sigma$, $\delta(q, a)$ is a singleton. Moreover, if $f$ is a linear function or a polynomial function, then we may refer to $M$ as linear bounded $\TM$ or polynomial bounded $\TM$, respectively. 

Next, we define how tape-bounded $\TM$s compute over strings.
Intuitively, tape-bounded $\TM$s compute in an analogous way to Turing machines except that the header cannot bypass or rewrite the end markers. 
Informally, a configuration of a tape-bounded $\TM$ is a tuple which consists of the tape, a position of the tape and the current state. A run of $A$ over a string $w \in \Gamma^*$ is a configuration sequence starting from the initial configuration, where the tape is a string $E_\bl w \blacksquare^n E_\br$ with $n = f(\abs{w})$, the header is in the first symbol of $w$ and the current state is $q_0$. From the current configuration we obtain the set of possible new configurations as follows. Let $a$ be the symbol in the current position of the tape and let $q$ be the current state. If $(q', a', \bd) \in \delta(q, a)$, then a new configuration is a tuple consisting of the state $q'$, a new tape which is obtain from the old one by replacing the symbol $a$ in the old position of the header by $a'$, and the header is moved to the direction given by $\bd$.

More formally, let $M = (f, Q, \Sigma, \blacksquare, E_\bl, E_\br, \Gamma, q_0, \delta, F)$ be a tape-bounded $\TM$. A \textbf{configuration} of $M$ is a tuple $(T, i, q)$, where $T = t_0 \cdots t_m \in \Gamma^*$ is the current \textbf{tape}, $i \in [0;m]$ is the current \textbf{position} in the tape and $q \in Q$ is the current state. A \textbf{run} of $A$ is a sequence $c_0, c_1, \ldots$ of configurations of $M$ defined as follows. First of all we define $c_0 = (E_\bl w \blacksquare^n E_\br, 0, q_0)$ with $n = f(\abs{w})$. If $c_i = (t_0 \cdots t_m, j, q)$, then we define $c_{i+1} = (t_0 \cdots t'_j \cdots t_m, j + b, q')$, where $(q', t'_j, d) \in \delta(q, t_j)$ and $b = 0$ if $d = \bs$, $b = 1$ if $d = \br$ and $b = -1$ if $d= \bl$. Note that by the definition of tape-bounded $\TM$s, we have $t_0 = E_\bl$ and $t_m = E_\br$. We say that automaton is in a state or enters to a state $q$ during a run if the run contains a configuration of the form $(T, j, q)$.

A string $w \in \Gamma^*$ is \textbf{accepted} by $M$ if there is a run over $w$ such that during the run $M$ enters into an accepting state. The language $L(M) \subseteq \Gamma^*$ \textbf{accepted} by $M$ is the set of strings in $\Gamma^*$ accepted by $M$.


\subsection{Languages two-way accepted by GMSC}

During this section we do not make a distinction between proposition symbols and alphabet symbols. For this section we fix an arbitrary alphabet $\Pi$.

Let $p_{\blacksquare}$, $p_\bl$ and $p_\br$ be three distinct proposition symbols that are not in the set $\Pi$.
%
%
Given a $n \in \N$ and a string $w \in \Pi^*$, its \textbf{two-way $n$-word model} $M^n_w = ([0;\abs{w}+ n + 1], R, V)$ is a $\Pi \cup \{p_\blacksquare, p_\bl, p_\br\}$-model, where $R$ is the symmetric closure of the natural ordering between integers in $[0;\abs{w} + n + 1]$ and for each $p \in \Pi$ and for each $i \in [\abs{w}]$, we define $p \in V(i)$ iff $w(i) = p$, and lastly for each $\abs{w} < j \leq \abs{w} + n$, we have $p_\blacksquare \in V(j)$, $p_\bl \in V(0)$ and $p_\br \in V(\abs{w}+n+1)$.

Given a $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GMSC$, a function $f \colon \N \to \N$, and a string $w \in \Pi^*$, we say that $\Lambda$ \textbf{two-way accepts $w$ w.r.t. $f$} if its pointed two-way $f(\abs{w})$-word model is accepted by $\Lambda$.
%
%
Moreover, given a language $L \subseteq \Pi^*$, we say that $\Lambda$ \textbf{two-way accepts $L$ w.r.t. $f$} if $\Lambda$ precisely two-way accepts the strings in $L$ w.r.t. $f$. On the other hand, we say that a language $L \subseteq \Pi^*$ is \textbf{two-way accepted} by $\Lambda$, if there is a function $f \colon \N \to \N$ such that $\Lambda$ precisely two-way accepts the strings in $L$ w.r.t. $f$


