\subsubsection{Translations}


In this section we show that $\GMSC$-programs two-way accepts precisely the same languages as tape-bounded $\TM$s.
%
%
%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{thrm: lbas equiv 2-way GMSC}
Given a language $L \subseteq \Pi^*$, then $L$ is two-way accepted by a $\Pi$-program of $\GMSC$ iff $L$ is accepted by a deterministic tape-bounded $\TM$.
\end{theorem}
%
%
\begin{proof}
    First assume that $L$ is two-way accepted by a $(\Pi, \cT)$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GMSC$ w.r.t. $f \colon \N \to \N$. 
    Lemma B.1 in \cite{ahvonen2024logical} (and an analogous result for $\MSC$ stated in Theorem $11$ in \cite{ahvonen2023descriptive} and in Theorem $5.4$ in \cite{10.1093/logcom/exae087}) shows that $\Lambda$ can translated into a program $\Gamma$ of $\GMSC$ where the modal depth of base rules is zero and the modal depth of iteration rules is at most one and which two-way accepts the same language as $\Lambda$. 
    We can further assume that proposition symbols do not appear in the bodies of the iteration rules (since an equivalent program is easy to obtain). This means that if we know the global configuration at a node $w$ in round $n$ and the global configurations of its out-neighbours in round $n$ then based on these global configurations we can compute the global configuration for $w$ in round $n+1$. 

    Now, we can simulate the evaluation of $\Gamma$ by a tape-bounded $\TM$ as follows.
    Informally, we construct a tape-bounded $\TM$ $M_{\Gamma}$ that simulates in a periodic fashion a global configuration of $\Gamma$ by marking each position of the tape with the head predicates that are true in the corresponding node in the two-way word model of the input string. To compute a global configuration of $\Gamma$ from its previous global configuration at a single node, $M_\Gamma$ scans which head predicate are true at the node and its out-neighbours. It is easy but tedious to implement $M_{\Gamma}$, thus we only informally describe how the tape-bounded $\TM$ works.
    %
    %
    \begin{enumerate}
        %
        %
        \item The tape-bound function of $M_\Gamma$ is $f$.
        %
        %
        \item The tape alphabet of $M_{\Gamma}$ are sets of schema variables $\cP(\cT)$. 
        %
        %
        \item The global configuration at round $n=0$ is computed as follows. The header of $M_{\Gamma}$ scans the whole input string $w = w_1 \ldots w_m$ from left to right and marks during the scan which head predicates are true at each node as follows. If $w_i$ is the current alphabet symbol where the header is then it is replaced by the tape alphabet symbol of the form $\cT_i$, where $\cT_i$ are the true head predicates at node $i$ in the two-way word model $M_w$ in round $0$. Since the base rule has modal depth zero there is no need for scanning the tape symbols on the left or right. After scanning the whole string the header moves from right to the left.
        %
        %
        \item 
        Similarly, to compute the global configuration of $\Gamma$ in round $n+1$, $M_{\Gamma}$ proceeds as follows. Assume that the header is at the left end marker.
        The tape-bounded $\TM$ $M_{\Gamma}$ moves on the right and proceeds as follows.
        %
        \begin{itemize}
            \item 
            First assume that the header is on the left of the end marker, i.e. in the position $1$. We scan the position $2$ and record the tape symbol of that position and move back to the position $1$. Then we update the tape symbol in the position $1$ w.r.t. the tape symbol of the position $2$, the tape symbol of the position $1$ and the iteration rules of $\Gamma$. Notice that this is possible since the modal depth of the iteration rules of $\Gamma$ is at most $1$. However, we record the old tape symbol of the position $1$ to the state of $M_{\Gamma}$ and the other symbols can be forgotten.
            %
            %
            \item After update the tape symbol of position $1$, the header moves to the position $2$ and then scans the tape symbol in the position $3$, records the tape symbol in that position and moves back to the position $2$. After that based on the tape symbol in the position $1$, the tape symbol in the position $2$ and the tape symbol in the position $3$, $M_{\Gamma}$ updates the tape symbol in position $2$ w.r.t. the iteration rules of $\Gamma$. Again, we record the old tape symbol of the position $2$ to the state of $M_{\Gamma}$ and the other symbols can be forgotten.
            %
            %
            \item This process continues in an analogous way till we reach the position just before the right end marker (i.e. the position $m-1$). Notice that we do not need to scan and record the tape symbol of the right end marker to compute a new tape symbol for the position $m-1$.  
            %
            %
            \item After computing a new tape symbol for each node, we have computed the global configuration in round $n+1$ and move back to the left end marker and may start a cycle again.
            %
            %
        \end{itemize}
        %
        %
        \item If the tape symbol of the position $1$ (i.e. the position on the left of the left end marker) includes an accepting predicate then $M_{\Gamma}$ enters into an accepting state.
    \end{enumerate}
    Clearly, the constructed tape-bounded $\TM$ accepts the same language as $\Gamma$ two-way accepts and thus the same language as $\Lambda$ two-way accepts.

    For the converse direction, assume that $L$ is accepted by a tape-bounded Turing machine $M = (f, Q, \Sigma, \blacksquare, E_\bl, E_\br, \Pi, q_0, \delta, F)$.
    We will construct a $\Pi$-program $\Lambda_M$ of $\GMSC$ that two-way accepts $L(A)$ w.r.t. $f$ as follows. 
    For each $a \in \Sigma$, the head predicates $X_a$ records the current tape symbol at the node and thus in each round precisely one of these predicates is true at each node. For each $q \in Q$, $X_q$ records the current state of the node, where the header is. We construct the rules for each $X_q$ such that in each two-way word model precisely at one node a single $X_q$ is true in each round. Thus, we do not need a separate head predicate to record where the header of $M$ is currently. 
    The head predicates $X_\bl$ and $X_\br$ record the position on the left and right of the current position of the header. 


    Recall that $\Diamond \varphi \colonequals \Diamond_{\geq 1} \varphi$.
    For each $a \in \Sigma$, we define a head predicate $X_a$ and the rules as follows
    \[
    X_a (0) \colonminus p_a \qquad X_a \colonminus \bigvee_{\substack{(s, b) \in Q \times \Sigma \\ \delta(s, b) = (q, a, \bd)}} X_s \land X_b. 
    \]
    For each state $q \in Q$, we define a head predicate $X_q$ as follows. For $X_{q_0}$, we set $X_{q_0} (0) \colonminus r $ and for other states $q \in Q \setminus \{q_0\}$, we set $X_q (0) \colonminus \bot$. 
    Before we define the iteration rules, we define for each $a \in \Sigma$ and for each $q \in Q$ the following auxiliary formulae
    \[
    \varphi_{(q, a, \bl)} \colonequals X_\bl \land \Diamond (X_q \land X_a), \qquad \varphi_{(q, a, \bs)} \colonequals (X_q \land X_a), \qquad \varphi_{(q, a, \br)} \colonequals X_\br \land \Diamond (X_q \land X_a). 
    \]
    Now, the iteration rules for each $q \in Q$ are defined as follows
    \[
    X_q \colonminus \bigvee_{\substack{(q', a') \in Q \times \Sigma \\ \delta(q',a') = (q, a, \bx)}} \varphi_{\delta(q',a')}. 
    \]
    %
    %
    Now, for each $\bd \in \{\bl, \bs, \br\}$, we let 
    \[
    \psi_\bd \colonequals \bigvee_{\substack{(q', a') \in Q \times \Sigma \\ \delta(q',a') = (q, a, \bd)}} \varphi_{\delta(q',a')}.
    \]
    Lastly, we define
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
    &X_\bl (0) \colonminus p_\bl &&X_\bl \colonminus ( X_\bl \land \Diamond \psi_\bs) \lor (\Diamond X_\bl \land \Diamond \psi_\br) \lor (\neg \bigvee_{q \in Q} X_q \land \Diamond \psi_\bl) \\
    &X_\br (0) \colonminus \neg p_\bl \land \Diamond\Diamond p_\bl &&X_\br \colonminus ( X_\br \land \Diamond \psi_\bs) \lor (\Diamond X_\br \land \Diamond\psi_\bl) \lor (\neg \bigvee_{q \in Q} X_q \land \Diamond \psi_\br).
    \end{aligned}
    \]


    The set of accepting predicates are the head predicates $X_q$, where $q \in F$.
    It is easy to show that the constructed program two-way accepts $L(M)$.
\end{proof}

Note that we did not take an advantage of counting modalities during the proof of the theorem above. Thus, the proof also shows that programs of $\MSC$ also two-way accepts the same languages as deterministic tape-bounded $\TM$s and vice versa deterministic tape bounded $\TM$s accept the languages that $\MSC$-programs two-way accepts. 
%

The proof of Theorem \ref{thrm: lbas equiv 2-way GMSC} implies that the data complexity of the model checking problem for $\GMSC$ (and for $\MSC$) is PSPACE-hard, since the proof shows that for any PSPACE-complete problem there is a polynomial time reduction to the model checking of $\GMSC$ (resp. $\MSC$). Thus, the combined complexity of the model checking problem is also PSPACE-hard for $\GMSC$ and $\MSC$. Furthermore, clearly the combined complexity of the model checking for $\GMSC$ is in PSPACE: for a $\Pi$-program $\Lambda$ of $\GMSC$ and pointed $\Pi$-model $(M, w)$, we simulate $\Lambda$ in $(M, w)$ by tracking its global configurations at each node and accept if $w$ enters into an accepting state. Therefore, we obtain the following corollary.

\begin{theorem}
    Both the combined complexity and the data complexity of the model checking problem for $\GMSC$ and $\MSC$ are $\mathrm{PSPACE}$-complete. 
\end{theorem}



