\section{Introduction}
\label{sec: Introduction}

\cite{Causality} introduces Bayesian networks and causal models, arguing that these frameworks capture causal knowledge, enabling the treatment of queries about the effects of external interventions and counterfactuals. Notably, such queries cannot be answered solely based on probability distributions. However, to our knowledge, no existing theory explicitly characterizes the additional knowledge required to answer them.

This work presents the theoretical framework of causal systems to clarify Aristotle's distinction between knowledge-\textit{that} and knowledge-\textit{why} within artificial intelligence. It then argues that answering queries about the effects of external interventions requires knowledge-\textit{why}. Since probability distributions represent only knowledge-\textit{that}, we address this theoretical gap and explain why Bayesian networks and causal models can account for the effects of external interventions.






\subsection{The Notion of Knowledge in Aristotle's \emph{Posterior Analytics}}
\label{sec: The Notion of Knowledge in Aristotle's Posterior Analytics}

In the \textit{Posterior Analytics} Aristotle sets out his theory of scientific knowledge~(\greek{ἐπιστήμη})\footnote{While ``\greek{ἐπιστήμη}'' in other contexts could be translated simply as ``knowledge'', in the context of the \textit{Posterior Analytics} Aristotle is clearly concerned with a notion of \greek{ἐπιστήμη} best understood as \textit{scientific} knowledge. \citet{Barnes1995} translates ``\greek{ἐπιστήμη}'' with ``understanding''.}. According to Aristotle, a scientist must be able to explain facts on the basis of their most fundamental causes, which in Aristotle's metaphysical framework are essences. These essences are known through perception and induction, so that science has an empirical foundation. In the \textit{Posterior Analytics} Aristotle sets out how exactly science can be built on this foundation and what the structure of scientific explanations should look like. Aristotle's account provides us with four key insights into the logic of causal explanations. 



%\subsection{How is knowledge obtained? Three insights from Aristotle}
%\label{subsec: How is knowledge obtained? Three insights from Aristotle}

\subsubsection{Knowledge by Demonstration}
\label{subsubsec: Knowledge by demonstration}

In an Aristotelian science facts are explained by way of a so-called \textit{demonstration} (\greek{ἀπόδειξις}). A demonstration is a type of deduction which displays the scientific explanation of a fact by deducing it from the causally fundamental facts. Demonstrations are a proper subset of syllogisms, the valid deductions which Aristotle characterizes and classifies in his \textit{Prior Analytics}.\footnote{\citet{Barnes1995} also has a translation of the \textit{Prior Analytics}.} Aristotle's theory of causal explanation in the \textit{Posterior Analytics} thus builds on the logical theory set out in his \textit{Prior Analytics}. In the \textit{Posterior Analytics} Aristotle sets out the criteria which a syllogism must satisfy in order to count as a demonstration. In a nutshell, a demonstration is a syllogism which derives its conclusion from causally and explanatorily fundamental premises, thereby providing a scientific causal explanation of the fact which constitutes its conclusion.\footnote{Cf. \textit{Posterior Analytics} 1.2, 71b16-72b4, translated by \citet{Barnes1995}, pp. 115-116.} A scientific explanation therefore follows the causal order and derive its \textit{explanandum} from the causally fundamental and explanatory facts.  


\subsubsection{Indemonstrable Knowledge}
\label{subsubsec: Indemonstrable knowledge}

While demonstrations thus play a key role in an Aristotelian science, Aristotle denies that all scientific facts can be demonstrated and he thus denies that all scientific knowledge is demonstrative. Indeed, Aristotle argues that it would be impossible for all scientific knowledge to be demonstrative, for in that case scientific explanations would either have to form infinite chains, the premises of each given demonstrations being demonstrated by yet another demonstration, or be cyclical, the same premises functioning both as premises and as conclusions of demonstrations.\footnote{Cf. \textit{Posterior Analytics} 1.3, 72b5-73a20, translated by \citet{Barnes1995}, pp. 117-118.} Aristotle argues that both infinite chains and cyclical arguments cannot be genuinely explanatory and  that demonstrations must therefore start with facts which cannot themselves be demonstrated. The Aristotelian scientist therefore also needs to have \textit{indemonstrable knowledge}, knowledge of the most fundamental facts of a science, on whose basis all the other facts are demonstrated. 

But how can such indemonstrable knowledge be scientific if all scientific knowledge requires an understanding of the causes of facts? What does it mean to have an understanding of the cause of an indemonstrable fact? Aristotle argues that indemonstrable knowledge is based on insight into the essences of things, the fundamental constituents of reality to which all causal explanations in science should be traced back. This insight into the essences of things, acquired through perception and induction, is called \textit{nous} (\greek{νοῦς}).\footnote{Aristotle discusses \textit{nous} in the last chapter of the \textit{Posterior Analytics}, 2.19, translated by \citet{Barnes1995}, pp. 165-166, who renders ``\greek{νοῦς}'' as ``comprehension''.}  The indemonstrable facts are therefore known scientifically on the basis of the underlying essences and thus the ultimate causes of those facts. Rather than being a set of further facts about things, these essences are the fundamental things in the ontology, from which someone with \textit{nous} directly infers the indemonstrable facts. Consequently, only someone with \textit{nous} has an understanding of the causes of indemonstrable facts and is therefore in a position to have scientific knowledge at all. Without \textit{nous} one has no scientific knowledge of the indemonstrable facts and \textit{a fortiori} no scientific knowledge of the demonstrable facts.\footnote{In \textit{Posterior Analytics} 1.33, at 89a11-89b6 (translated by \citet{Barnes1995}, pp. 146-147), Aristotle argues that even someone who knows demonstrations lacks scientific knowledge if he does not know the indemonstrable facts which constitute their premises on the basis of the underlying essences and thus by way of \textit{nous}. According to Aristotle, such a person has mere opinion (\greek{δόξα}) as opposed to scientific knowledge.} For this reason Aristotle calls \textit{nous} the \textit{principle of scientific knowledge} (\greek{ἀρχή ἐπιστήμης}).\footnote{Cf. \textit{Posterior Analytics} 2.19, 100b5-17, translated by \citet{Barnes1995}, p. 166.}


\subsubsection{Knowledge-\textit{that} and Knowledge-\textit{why}}
\label{subsubsec: Knowledge that and knowledge why}

Another key insight from Aristotle's theory of science is that the facts can be established even if one does not yet have scientific explanations of them. Aristotle allows for this by distinguishing between knowledge of the \textit{that} (\greek{ὅτι}) and knowledge of the \textit{why} (\greek{διότι}). The scientist first makes observations and collects data and thus acquires knowledge-\textit{that} of a set of facts without yet knowing the scientific explanations of those facts \textemdash thus not yet having knowledge-\textit{why}. In order to acquire knowledge-\textit{why}, she must subsequently gain an understanding of the underlying essences and determine which facts follow directly from these essences and are thus indemonstrable and which facts can be demonstrated. On this basis she can then, in the final stage of her research, construct demonstrations and obtain knowledge-\textit{why}. 

Knowledge-\textit{that} may itself come with a kind of explanation which falls short of being scientific and therefore does not yield knowledge-\textit{why}. Such an explanation involved in knowledge-\textit{that} is deficient in that it does not follow the causal order of things, but rather derives something that is causally fundamental from something which is causally not fundamental but which might be more observable. Aristotle's example is a syllogism which derives the proximity of the other planets to earth (when compared to the stars) from the observable fact that they do not twinkle (whereas stars do):\footnote{Cf. \textit{Posterior Analytics} 1.13, 78a28-78b4, translated by \citet{Barnes1995}, pp. 127-128.} 


\begin{enumerate}
    \item[p1.] Celestial bodies that do not twinkle are nearby. 
    \item[p2.] The planets are celestial bodies that do not twinkle. 
    \item[c.] The planets are nearby. 
\end{enumerate}

This syllogism does not track the causal order since its middle term \textemdash not-twinkling \textemdash is not the cause of the planets' proximity to earth. Rather, the planets' proximity to earth is the cause of their not twinkling, so that the following syllogism does yield an adequate causal explanation: 

\begin{enumerate}
    \item[p1.$^{\prime}$] Nearby celestial bodies do not twinkle. 
    \item[p2.$^{\prime}$] The planets are nearby celestial bodies. 
    \item[c.$^{\prime}$] The planets do not twinkle. 
\end{enumerate}

In this case a directly observable fact \textemdash  the planets' not twinkling \textemdash is  explained on the basis of a fact that is not directly observable, but causally fundamental \textemdash the planets' proximity to earth. The middle term \textemdash proximity to earth \textemdash causally explains why the predicate \textemdash not twinkling \textemdash belongs to the subject \textemdash the planets.

\subsubsection{Subordinate and Superordinate Areas of Science}
\label{subsubsec: Subordinate and superordinate sciences}

The distinction between knowledge-\textit{that} and knowledge-\textit{why} also plays a role in Aristotle's subordination of areas of scientific inquiry. Aristotle holds that certain areas of science are subordinated to others, which means that the premises used by the subordinate areas —  for instance, optics —  are explained by the superordinate areas —  for instance, geometry, in the case of optics.  The subordinate area of science can take as a starting-point the results of the superordinate area  and on this basis explain %its 
the phenomena it is concerned with and thus yield knowledge-\textit{why}. 

This subordination of areas of science applies in particular to the relationship between the theoretical and the empirical sciences. The empirical areas of science rely on the the results of the theoretical areas of science to explain the observed phenomena. For instance, the study of the rainbow, an empirical science, explains the phenomenon of the rainbow on the basis of results from optics, which themselves are again based on results from geometry.\footnote{Cf. \textit{Posterior Analytics} 1.13, 79a10-16, translated by \citet{Barnes1995}, pp. 128-129.} 





\subsection{Applying Aristotle's Notion of Knowledge in Artificial Intelligence}
\label{subsec: Applying Aristotle's Notion of Knowledge in Artificial Intelligence}

The aim of this paper is to establish the distinction between knowledge-\textit{that} and knowledge-\textit{why}, as described in Section~\ref{subsubsec: Knowledge that and knowledge why}, within artificial intelligence by formalizing it within a logical framework. Aristotle's logic is a term logic in which each basic proposition describes a relationship between two terms. However, we are not concerned with the internal structure of propositions in Aristotle's logic but rather with his conceptual distinction between a syllogism and a demonstration, as discussed in Section~\ref{subsubsec: Knowledge by demonstration}. 

Following the approach of \cite{Bochman}, we extend propositional logic -- where the provability operator~${(\vdash)/2}$ corresponds to Aristotle's syllogisms -- by introducing an explainability operator~${(\Rrightarrow)/2}$ to capture demonstrations that establish knowledge-\textit{why}. In propositional logic, for a set of statements~$\Phi$ and a statement~$\psi$, the expression~${\Phi \vdash \psi}$ means that there exists a syllogism with conclusion~$\psi$ and premises~$\Phi$.



\begin{example}
    Consider a road that passes through a field with a sprinkler in it. Assume the sprinkler is switched on by a weather sensor if it is sunny. Suppose further that it rains whenever it is cloudy and that the road is wet if either it rains or the sprinkler is turned on. Finally, suppose that a wet road is slippery. 

    Denote by $\textit{cloudy}$ the event that the weather is cloudy, by $\textit{sprinkler}$ the event that the sprinkler is on, by $\textit{rain}$ the event of rainy weather, by $\textit{wet}$ the event that the road is wet, and by $\textit{slippery}$ the event that the road is slippery. 

    In this case, $\{ \textit{slippery} \} \vdash \textit{wet}$ indicates that there is a syllogism for the road being wet with the premise that the road is slippery. However, according to Section \ref{subsubsec: Knowledge by demonstration}, such a syllogism is not a demonstration, as it concludes from the effect that the road is slippery to the cause that the road is wet. 
    \label{example - introduction running example}
\end{example}



\mbox{\cite{Bochman}} extends propositional logic by introducing an additional operator, $(\Rrightarrow)/2$, to denote the acquisition of knowledge-\textit{why}. For a set of statements~$\Phi$ and a statement~$\psi$, the expression~${\Phi \Rrightarrow \psi}$ signifies that \mbox{knowledge-\textit{that}} about the truth of all propositions in~$\Phi$ leads to knowledge-\textit{why} about~$\psi$.
 %\cite{Bochman} deviates here from Aristotle in that, unlike Aristotle, he does not impose minimality constraints on the premises of demonstrations and allows for more than two premises or for superfluous premises.  

\begin{example}
    In the context of Example~\ref{example - introduction running example}, we write  
    $$
    \{ \textit{cloudy}, \textit{rain} \} \Rrightarrow \textit{slippery}
    $$ 
    to indicate that knowledge-\textit{why} about the road being slippery can be derived from knowledge-\textit{that} it is cloudy and raining, i.e., from the truth of the propositions in~$\{ \textit{cloudy}, \textit{rain} \}$.
     %In particular, we may conclude from the cause—rainy weather—to the effect—a slippery road—to obtain such a demonstration.  
    \label{example - demonstrability operator}
\end{example}



Furthermore, \cite{Bochman} applies the idea that causal relations are typically expressed in the form of rules or laws. According to Chapter~1 in \mbox{\cite{Hulswit}}, this idea was first articulated by Descartes as follows:  

\begin{principle}[Causal Rules]
``...we can obtain knowledge of the rules or laws of nature, which are the secondary and particular causes...''~(Ren\'e Descartes: Principles of Philosophy II:37; translation by \cite{Miller2009}).  
\label{principle - causal rules}
\end{principle}  

According to \cite{Bochman}, causal knowledge should be expressed in a causal theory~$\Delta$, which consists of a set of causal rules of the form~$\phi \Rightarrow \psi$ for statements~$\phi$ and~$\psi$. A causal rule~$\phi \Rightarrow \psi$ signifies that there is a demonstration of~$\psi$ based on the premise~$\phi$, meaning that knowledge-\textit{why} about~$\phi$ leads to knowledge-\textit{why} about~$\psi$. The corresponding explainability operator~$(\Rrightarrow_{\Delta})/2$ is then derived by extending the causal theory~$\Delta$ with well-motivated axioms.




\begin{example}
    The causal knowledge in Example~\ref{example - introduction running example} leads to the following causal theory $\Delta$:  
    \begin{align*}
        & \textit{cloudy} \Rightarrow \textit{rain} && \text{(cloudy weather causes rain)} \\
        & \neg \textit{cloudy} \Rightarrow \textit{sprinkler} && \text{(sunny weather causes the sprinkler being on)} \\
        & \textit{rain} \Rightarrow \textit{wet} && \text{(rain causes the road to be wet)} \\
        & \textit{sprinkler} \Rightarrow \textit{wet} && \text{(the sprinkler causes the road to be wet)} \\
        & \textit{wet} \Rightarrow \textit{slippery} && \text{(a wet road is slippery)}
    \end{align*}
    The corresponding explainability operator $(\Rrightarrow_{\Delta})/2$ is then obtained by completing $\Delta$ according to well-motivated axioms.  
    \label{example - causal reasoning is captured in rules}
\end{example}


According to Section \ref{subsubsec: Knowledge that and knowledge why}, in an area of science that describes a given situation, we find a set of external premises~$\mathcal{E}$ -- that is, statements~$\epsilon$ that, if observed, do not require any explanation or demonstration. One may then argue that the demonstration of $\epsilon$ would belong to another, superordinate area of science. We conclude that observing~$\epsilon$ immediately yields knowledge-\textit{why} about $\epsilon$ within the area of science under consideration. 

\begin{example}
    In Example~\ref{example - introduction running example}, we consider the following external premises~$\mathcal{E}$:  
    \begin{align*}
        & \textit{cloudy}, && \neg \textit{cloudy}, && \text{(it is either cloudy or not)} \\
        & && \neg \textit{rain}, && \text{(it usually does not rain)} \\
        & && \neg \textit{sprinkler}, && \text{(the sprinkler is initially off)} \\
        & && \neg \textit{wet}, && \text{(the road is usually dry)} \\
        & && \neg \textit{slippery} && \text{(the road is usually not slippery)}
    \end{align*}

    Suppose we observe that the weather is cloudy and rainy, i.e., we have knowledge-\textit{that} it is cloudy and rainy. Example~\ref{example - causal reasoning is captured in rules} states~${\textit{cloudy} \Rightarrow \textit{rain}}$, meaning that there is a demonstration of rainy weather based on the premise that it is cloudy. Since~$\textit{cloudy} \in \mathcal{E}$ is an external premise, demonstrating~$\textit{cloudy}$ lies beyond the scope of the given area of science -- one might argue that it belongs to meteorology. Thus, observing cloudy weather (i.e.,~$\textit{cloudy}$) yields knowledge-\textit{why} about~$\textit{cloudy}$, and a demonstration of~$\textit{rain}$ from~$\textit{cloudy}$ provides knowledge-\textit{why} about~$\textit{rain}$. In other words, we obtain~$\textit{cloudy} \Rrightarrow \textit{rain}$.  

    On the other hand, if we observe that the sprinkler is off (i.e.,~$\neg \textit{sprinkler}$), we immediately acquire knowledge-\textit{why} about~$\neg \textit{sprinkler} \in \mathcal{E}$, as it is an external premise. Since external premises are taken as given without requiring further causal explanation, we conclude~$\neg \textit{sprinkler} \Rrightarrow \neg \textit{sprinkler}$. This reflects our expectation that the sprinkler remains off unless actively switched on.  
    \label{example - indemonstrable knowledge}
\end{example}





Within a given area of science, we conclude that the demonstrations in Section~\ref{subsubsec: Knowledge by demonstration} yield knowledge-\textit{why} only if they originate from external premises~\( {\epsilon \in \mathcal{E}} \). 


\begin{principle}[Causal Foundation]
     \textit{Causal explanations} or \textit{demonstrations} that yield knowledge-\textit{why} must originate from external premises in \( \mathcal{E} \). That is, the external premises~\(  \mathcal{E} \) represent potential knowledge-\textit{that} for which no further explanation is required by agreement.
     \label{principle - causal foundation}
\end{principle}



In Definition~\ref{definition - causal system}, we formalize our observations as a consistent set of statements~$\mathcal{O}$ to obtain a deterministic causal system:  
$$
\textbf{CS} := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O}).
$$

To summarize, the causal theory~$\Delta$ and the external premises~$\mathcal{E}$ formalize an area of science, as described in Section~\ref{subsubsec: Subordinate and superordinate sciences}. The set~$\mathcal{O}$ denotes a collection of additional observations, i.e., knowledge-\textit{that}, which the system~$\textbf{CS}$ uses to reason within the area of science under consideration.



\begin{example}
    Recall the situation in Example~\ref{example - introduction running example}. Assume we describe this situation using an area of science that consists of causal knowledge, as captured in the causal theory~$\Delta$ of Example~\ref{example - causal reasoning is captured in rules}, and the external premises~$\mathcal{E}$ from Example~\ref{example - indemonstrable knowledge}. 

    Furthermore, assume that we observe rainy weather, that is,~${\mathcal{O} := \{ \textit{rain} \}}$, resulting in the causal system~$\textbf{CS}_1 := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O})$. Finally, assume we observe nothing, leading to the causal system~$\textbf{CS}_2 := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset)$.
\label{example - introducing causal systems}
\end{example}


\begin{remark}
    Note that a causal theory $\Delta$ may also mention expressions like~${\top \Rightarrow \phi}$ for a statement $\phi$, meaning $\emptyset \Rrightarrow \phi$. The best interpretation of this construct is that the truth of $\phi$ is directly obtained from the essences of the area of science. This would be a formal analogue of the way in which the indemonstrable facts are grasped in an Aristotelian science, namely by inferring them directly from the underlying essences through \textit{nous}, rather than deriving them from any further set of facts. However, this expression, so interpreted, requires additional justification in terms of the nature of essences.
    \label{remark - metaphysical justification}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
     Note that a causal theory $\Delta$ may also mention expressions like~${\phi \Rightarrow \bot}$ for a statements $\phi$, which are not considered by Aristotle. What this expression signifies is infeasible within an Aristotelian science, in which one first gathers knowledge-\textit{that} and then tries to arrange this knowledge-\textit{that} by way of demonstrations. Such demonstrations only have knowledge-\textit{that} as their premisses or conclusions, thus excluding falsehood, written ``$\bot$''. We conclude that further investigation is needed in order to determine whether this expression is meaningful.
     \label{remark - observations}
\end{remark}



Relying on \textit{nous}, causal systems assert knowledge-\textit{why} causal reasoning satisfies the principle of \textit{natural necessity}, which Aquinas formulated as follows:

\begin{principle}[Natural Necessity]
    ``... given the existence of the cause, the effect must necessarily follow.'' (Thomas Aquinas: Summa Contra Gentiles II: 35.4; translation by \cite{Anderson})
    \label{principle - Aquinas}
\end{principle}   

\begin{example}
    In Example~\ref{example - introduction running example}, this means, for instance, that the road is wet whenever it rains.
    \label{example - Mill's covering law}
\end{example}

Furthermore, relying on \textit{nous}, causal systems assert knowledge-\textit{why} causal reasoning satisfies the assumption of \textit{sufficient causation}, which Leibniz formulated as follows:

\begin{assumption}[Sufficient Causation]
    ``...there is nothing without a reason, or no effect without a cause.''  
    (Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz: First Truths; translation by 
\cite{Leibniz}, p.~268)
    \label{principle - Leibniz}
\end{assumption}

\begin{example}
    In Example~\ref{example - introduction running example}, this implies, for instance, that rain does not occur without a cause. Therefore, if we observe rain, it must be cloudy. We conclude that \textit{sufficient causation}, as stated in Assumption~\ref{principle - Leibniz}, ensures that all possible occurrences are explained by the available causal knowledge and external premises.
    \label{example - causal law}
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
    The letters $\Delta$, $\mathcal{E}$, and $\mathcal{O}$ in a causal system read ``Deo,'' which is a Latin translation for ``God.'' This represents a causal system that applies \textit{causal sufficiency} in Assumption~\ref{principle - Leibniz} in its reasoning and thus assumes something akin to God's perspective in the area of science under consideration.
\end{remark}

Let $\textbf{CS} := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset)$ be a causal system without observations. Denote by~${\omega_1, \dots, \omega_n}$ all possible states of the situation under consideration. In this case,~${\omega_i \cap \mathcal{E}}$, for $1 \leq i \leq n$, yields the possible states of the external premises, and the system~$\textbf{CS}$ acquires knowledge-\textit{why} about \mbox{$\omega_1 \cap \mathcal{E}$ or ... or $\omega_n \cap \mathcal{E}$}. Applying \textit{natural necessity} in Principle \ref{principle - Aquinas} and \textit{sufficient causation} in Assumption~\ref{principle - Leibniz}, the system $\textbf{CS}$ concludes that a state $\omega_i$, for $1 \leq i \leq n$, is only possible if it can be demonstrated with premises in $\omega_i \cap \mathcal{E}$. The system now checks, for every state~\mbox{$\omega_i$, $1 \leq i \leq n$}, whether there is a demonstration for~$\omega_i$ with premises in~${\omega_i \cap \mathcal{E}}$ to determine possible states $\omega_1, \dots, \omega_k$. Since it assumes \mbox{knowledge-\textit{why}} about \textit{natural necessity} in Principle \ref{principle - Aquinas} and \textit{sufficient causation} in Assumption \ref{principle - Leibniz}, the system $\textbf{CS}$ acquires knowledge-\textit{why} about~\mbox{$\omega_1$ or ... or $\omega_k$}. Note that Aristotle does not consider disjunctions as an object of knowledge. Since we want causal systems to acquire further knowledge-\textit{why} by case distinction, we agree on the following principle:

\begin{principle}[Consistency with Deduction]
    Explainability $(\Rrightarrow)/2$ is compatible with logical deduction $(\vdash)/2$ in both the explanandum and the explanans.
    \label{principle - consistency with logical reasoning}
\end{principle}

Suppose a statement $\phi$ holds in all possible states $\omega_i$ for $1 \leq i \leq k$; by case distinction, the system $\textbf{CS}$ obtains knowledge-\textit{why} about $\phi$ being true.


\begin{example}
    Recall the causal system without observations $\textbf{CS}_2 := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset)$ in Example~\ref{example - introducing causal systems}. In the case of cloudy weather, it concludes that it rains and that the road is wet, and in the case of sunny weather, it concludes that the sprinkler is on and that the road is wet as well. This yields demonstrations for a wet road in both cases. As $\textit{cloudy} \in \mathcal{E}$ and $\neg \textit{cloudy} \in \mathcal{E}$ are external premises, the system $\textbf{CS}_2$ acquires knowledge-\textit{why} about the road being wet.
    \label{example - introducing reasoning in causal systems without observations}
\end{example}



Let $\textbf{CS} := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O})$ be a causal system such that $\mathcal{O} \neq \emptyset$, i.e., it observes something. To determine the possible states of the world, the system~$\textbf{CS}$ first computes the states of the world $\omega_1, \dots, \omega_n$ that the system~$\textbf{CS}' := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset)$ would consider possible, thereby acquiring knowledge-\textit{why} about~$\omega_1$ or ... or $\omega_n$. It then checks whether the states $\omega_1, \dots, \omega_n$ are consistent with the observations in $\mathcal{O}$, yielding possible states $\omega_1, \dots, \omega_k$ and knowledge-\textit{that}~$\omega_1$ or ... or $\omega_k$. However, note that this does not lead to knowledge-\textit{why}, as drawing conclusions from the observations generally runs counter to the direction of cause and effect.



\begin{example}
    Recall the causal system $\textbf{CS}_1 := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O})$ from Example~\ref{example - introducing causal systems}. It first determines the possible states of the world, as the system ${\textbf{CS}_2 := (\Delta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset)}$ would, and then checks these states for consistency with the observation that it is raining. In particular, relying on \textit{sufficient causation} in Assumption \ref{principle - Leibniz}, it concludes that it is cloudy and obtains the only possible state $\omega$:
    \begin{align*}
       & \textit{cloudy} := \textit{True}, && \textit{rain} := \textit{True}, && \textit{sprinkler} := \textit{False}, && \textit{wet} := \textit{True}, && \dots
    \end{align*}
    In particular, the system $\textbf{CS}_1$ acquires knowledge-\textit{that} about the sprinkler being off, which requires concluding from the effect—rainy weather—to its cause—cloudy weather. Hence, the system $\textbf{CS}_1$ does not possess \mbox{knowledge-\textit{why}} about the sprinkler being off. 
    \label{example - introducing reasoning in causal systems with observations}
\end{example}

The following example illustrates that \textit{natural necessity}, as stated in Principle \ref{principle - Aquinas}, may fail in some situations: 

%  \begin{example}
%  Suppose that I am carrying a Ming vase in my hands and that the sudden sound of a dog's barking nearby scares me so much that I drop the vase from my hands, which then falls to pieces on the floor. In this situation, my dropping the vase from my hands is the cause of the vase falling to pieces on the floor. However, it does not seem to be necessary that my dropping the vase from my hands should have caused it to fall to pieces on the floor. One can easily imagine a possible scenario in which I also dropped the vase when the dogs scared me but in which a person walking next to me saw this happening and managed to catch the vase, thereby preventing it from falling to pieces.
% \end{example}

 \begin{example}
     In Example \ref{example - introduction running example}, suppose that it is cloudy and raining. In this situation, the clouds are clearly a cause for the rain. However, it is not necessarily the case that clouds lead to rain, as one can easily imagine a heavily overcast day on which it is not raining. 
 \end{example}

Section~\ref{sec: Knowledge under Uncertainty} weakens \textit{natural necessity} in Principle~\ref{principle - Aquinas}. We introduce weighted causal rules $(w, \phi \Rightarrow \psi)$, where the weight $w \in \mathbb{R} \cup \{ +\infty, -\infty \}$ quantifies our uncertainty about whether \textit{natural necessity} applies to the causal relation ${\phi \Rightarrow \psi}$. This idea leads us to the introduction of maximum entropy causal systems in Definition \ref{definition - maximum entropy causal system}, which yield a probabilistic version of Aristotle's knowledge-\textit{why}.

In this context, we reinterpret the Aristotelian distinction between syllogisms and demonstrations within the Bayesian networks and causal models of \mbox{\cite{Causality}}. Specifically, by considering the principle of \textit{maximum entropy} from \cite{Shannon} as an analogue to Aristotle's syllogisms, we conclude that greedily maximizing entropy along a causal order serves as an analogue to demonstrations. Indeed, \mbox{\cite{Williamson2001}} demonstrates that the distribution associated with a Bayesian network is obtained by extending its probabilistic information and greedily maximizing entropy along the given causal order. Consequently, Bayesian networks acquire a probabilistic analogue of Aristotle's \mbox{knowledge-\textit{why}}, enabling them to answer queries about the effects of external interventions.



\section{Knowledge in Deterministic Systems}
\label{sec: Knowledge in Deterministic Systems}

We begin our inquiry by investigating causal reasoning in a deterministic Boolean setting, where all parameters of interest take one of two values, represented as true or false. Section~\ref{subsubsec: Propositional Logic} briefly reviews the basics of propositional logic and introduces the provability operator $(\vdash)/2$, which we use to represent knowledge-\textit{that} and syllogisms. Following \cite{Bochman}, we consider explainability as a binary relation $(\Rrightarrow)/2$ on knowledge-\textit{that} represented by propositional formulas. Section~\ref{subsubsec: Bochman's Logical Theory of Causality} introduces Bochman's logical theory of causality. Section~\ref{subsec: Causal Systems} builds on the ideas of \cite{Bochman} and introduces causal systems as the most general framework for reasoning about knowledge-\textit{why} that evolves from well-motivated assertions. %In Section~\ref{subsubsec: Knowledge in Causal Systems}, we discuss the concept of knowledge captured within a causal system. 
Finally, in Section~\ref{subsec: Interpreting Artificial Intelligence Frameworks as Deterministic Causal Systems}, we interpret the structural causal models of \mbox{\cite{Causality}} as causal systems to evaluate the type of knowledge they provide. Section~\ref{subsubsec: External Interventions in Causal Systems} then extends the treatment of external interventions from \mbox{\cite{Causality}} to causal systems, and Section~\ref{sec: The Constraint and Explanatory Content of Causal Reasoning} reformulates the semantics of causal systems to prepare for probabilistic generalizations in Section~\ref{sec: Knowledge under Uncertainty}.


