Propositional formulas are connected by the semantic notion of entailment, denoted $(\models)/2$, and the syntactic notion of derivation, denoted $(\vdash)/2$ . 

 \begin{definition}[Semantic Entailment]
%      A formula $\phi$ \textbf{semantically entails} a formula $\psi$, written~${\phi \models \psi}$, if whenever $\omega \models \phi$ for a structure $\omega$ we also find~${\omega \models \psi}$, i.e.,~if every structure modeling $\phi$ also models~$\psi$. Analogously, a set of formulas $\Phi$ \textbf{semantically entails} a formula $\psi$, written $\Phi \models \psi$, if for every structure $\omega$ we find $\omega \models \psi$ whenever $\omega \models \Phi$. We call $\Phi$ \textbf{consistent} if $\Phi$ has at least one model, i.e.~if $\Phi \not \models \bot$.

      We call a set of formulas $\Phi$ \textbf{deductively closed} if whenever $\Phi \models \psi$ we find $\psi \in \Phi$. The \textbf{deductive closure} of a set of formulas $\Phi$ is the smallest deductively closed set $\bar{\Phi}$ such that $\Phi \subseteq \bar{\Phi}$.  Finally, we call a consistent deductively closed set of formulas $\Phi$ a \textbf{world} if $\Phi$ is maximal with respect to the subset relation $(\subseteq)/2$. 
      \label{definition - entailment}
 \end{definition}

\begin{remark}
    Every world $\Phi = \overline{\textbf{L}}$ is the deductive closure of the set of its~literals 
    $$
    \textbf{L} := \{ l \in \{ p , \neg p \} \text{ : } p \in \mathfrak{P},~l \in \Phi \}.
    $$ 
    As $\Phi$ is consistent and maximal regarding the subset relation $(\subseteq)/2$, we find 
    $$
    \textbf{L} := (\textbf{L} \cap \mathfrak{P}) \cup  \{ \neg p \text{: } p \in \mathfrak{P}, p \not \in \Phi \}.
    $$
    Hence, we can identify $\Phi$ with the set of propositions $\textbf{L} \cap \mathfrak{P}$, which is also a synonym for structures.

    % As we can identify structures with the set of propositions that hold in them, we shall henceforth  use the terms ``structure'', ``subset of propositions'', and ``world'' interchangeably. %Specifically, we treat worlds and structures as partially ordered by the subset relation $(\subseteq)/2$.
\end{remark}

