\subsubsection{The Principle of Maximum Entropy} %: Deduction under Uncertainty}
\label{subsubsec: The Principle of Maximum Entropy: Deduction under Uncertainty}

   Fix a sample space $\Omega$ together with $n$ probabilities~${\pi(A_i) \in [0,1]}$ of pairwise disjoint events~$A_i$, where $1 \leq i \leq n$ and~${n \in \mathbb{N}_{\geq 1}}$. We expect a rational agent to assume the distribution~$\pi$ on $\Omega$ according to the principle of indifference:



\begin{principle}[Indifference]
   Two events are equally probable if there is no reason to prefer one over the other.
   \label{principle - indifference}
   \label{law - law of indiffernence}
\end{principle}


\begin{example}
    Consider a six-sided die, which gives rise to the sample space ${\Omega := \{ 1,2,3,4,5,6 \}}$ of outcomes. If we have no further information about the die, according to \textit{indifference} in Principle~\ref{law - law of indiffernence}, we consider the die to be fair, i.e., we assume that throwing the die results in $i$ with a probability of~${\pi(i) := 1/6}$ for all $1 \leq i \leq 6$.

    Assume we are further informed that the die is biased and shows one or two with probability $1/2$, that is, $\pi( \{ 1, 2 \}) = 1/2$, and six with probability $1/10$, i.e., $\pi(6) = 1/10$. According to \textit{indifference} in Principle~\ref{law - law of indiffernence}, this results in: 
    \begin{align*}
        & \pi(1) = 1/4 && \pi(2) = 1/4 && \pi(3) = 4/30 \\
        & \pi(4) = 4/30 && \pi(5) = 4/30 && \pi(6) = 1/10
    \end{align*}
    In particular, we first distribute the probability $\pi(\{ 1, 2 \}) = 1/2$ uniformly to the events $1$ and $2$ and set $\pi (6) := 1/10$. Finally, we distribute the remaining probability mass of 
    $$
    1 - \pi (\{ 1, 2 \}) - \pi(6) = 4/10
    $$ 
    uniformly to the remaining events~\mbox{$3$, $4$, and $5$}.
    \label{example - principle of indifference}    
\end{example}

Unfortunately, we need the mutual exclusivity of the events $A_i$, $1 \leq i \leq n$, to apply \textit{indifference} in Principle \ref{law - law of indiffernence}.

\begin{example}
   Recall the situation of Example \ref{example - principle of indifference} and suppose we learn that the die shows one or two with probability $\pi( \{ 1,2 \}) = 1/3$. Further, assume we find the die to shows~two or three with probability~${\pi( \{ 2,3\}) = 1/3}$. Now, \textit{indifference} in Principle \ref{law - law of indiffernence} does not tell us how to merge the information about throwing two, provided by the probabilities $\pi( \{ 1,2 \}) = 1/3$ and~$\pi( \{ 2,3 \}) = 1/3$, to get a distribution $\pi$ on $\Omega$.
\end{example}

Suppose that we are given $n$ probabilities $\pi(A_i) \in [0,1]$ of the events~$A_i$, where~${1 \leq i \leq n}$ and $n \in \mathbb{N}_{\geq 1}$, which are no longer assumed to be pairwise disjoint. A rational agent assumes the distribution $\pi$ on $\Omega$ that is given by the principle of maximum entropy:

\begin{principle}[Maximum Entropy]
The distribution~$\pi$ on $\Omega$ results from maximizing the \textbf{entropy}
$$
H (\pi) := \sum_{\omega \in \Omega} (- \ln (\pi (\omega))) \cdot \pi (\omega)
$$
under the constraint that $A_i$ occurs with probability $\pi (A_i)$ for~${1 \leq  i \leq n}$.
\label{principle - maximum entropy}
\end{principle}

Since the function $-\ln : (0,1] \rightarrow [0, \infty)$ is strictly monotonically decreasing, with $-\ln(0) = \infty$ and $-\ln(1) = 0$, we interpret $-\ln(\pi(\omega))$ as our degree of surprise about the event ${\omega \in \Omega}$. In particular, the number $-\ln(\pi(\omega))$ becomes large for small probabilities $\pi(\omega)$; that is, the more we are surprised about $\omega$ being true, the larger the number $-\ln(\pi(\omega))$ becomes. Hence, we interpret~$H(\pi)$ as the average degree of surprise in a distribution $\pi$. We further assume that the more we are surprised by our observations, the more randomness is captured in $\pi$, and that randomness stands for missing information. In fact, the entropy $H(\pi)$ is known to measure missing information in a distribution~$\pi$ \mbox{\citep{Shannon}}. 

Given only knowledge about the probabilities~${\pi(A_1), \ldots, \pi(A_n)}$, it makes sense that a rational agent assumes the distribution $\pi$ on $\Omega$, which extends these probabilities by maximizing the missing information, i.e., the entropy~$H(\pi)$.  Maximizing entropy also resembles \textit{indifference} in Principle~\ref{law - law of indiffernence} if the events $A_i$ are pairwise disjoint~\citep{DEMARTIN}. 

\begin{remark}
    Note that $-\ln : (0,1] \rightarrow [0, \infty)$ is used in the definition of entropy, as one wants the entropy to be additive regarding distributions of independent random variables.
\end{remark}



Next, we assume the random variables in $\mathfrak{P} := \{ p_1,...,p_m \}$ to be Boolaen, i.e, they yield a propositional alphabet. As in Example \ref{example - modeling with RV},  we identify a world~$\omega$ with a value assignment on $\mathfrak{P}$ and a formula $\phi$ with the event  corresponding to the worlds $\omega$ with $\omega \models \phi$. Suppose that we are given the probabilities $\pi (\phi_i)$ of the formulas $\phi_i$. In general, maximizing the entropy does not yield a distribution that can be easily described using the probabilities~\mbox{$\pi (\phi_i)$, $1 \leq i \leq n$}. Nevertheless, as the distribution $\pi$ is essentially determined by giving one number for every formula $\phi_i$, $1 \leq i \leq n$, one aims for a parameterization of $\pi$ that is also given by one number $w_i \in \mathbb{R}$ for every formula~\mbox{$\phi_i$, $1 \leq i \leq n$}. %Here, we apply the following result:


\begin{parametrization}[\cite{Berger}]
 We find $n$ \textbf{degrees of certainty}, i.e., real numbers~${w_i \in \mathbb{R}}$ for ${1\leq i \leq n}$ such that $\pi$ is given by setting
\begin{align*}
    & \pi (\omega) := \dfrac{{\displaystyle \exp \left( \sum_{\omega \models \phi_i} w_i \right)}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{\omega' \text{ world}} \exp \left( \sum_{\omega' \models \phi_i} w_i \right)}} && \text{ for every world $\omega$.}
\end{align*}

\label{parametrization - maximum entropy model}
\end{parametrization}

 Finally, a LogLinear model of~\cite{MLN} formalizes a set of formulas with degrees of certainty in the sense of Parametrization~\ref{parametrization - maximum entropy model}. 

\begin{definition}[LogLinear Models]
    A \textbf{LogLinear model} is a finite set~$\Phi$ consisting of \textbf{weighted constraints} $(w, \phi)$, where~${w \in \mathbb{R} \cup \{ + \infty, - \infty \}}$ is a real weight and $\phi$ is a formula.
    \label{definition - LogLinear model}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
    Recall the situation from Example~\ref{example - introduction running example}, which is described by the propositional alphabet in Example~\ref{example - propositional alphabet}. Reasoning on this scenario may lead to the LogLinear model:
    \begin{equation*}
        \Phi := \{ (\ln (2),  \textit{cloudy} \rightarrow \textit{rain} ), (\ln (3), \neg \textit{cloudy} \rightarrow \textit{sprinkler}), (+\infty, \textit{wet} \leftrightarrow \textit{rain})  \}.
    \end{equation*}
    \label{example - LogLinear model}
\end{example}


Parametrization \ref{parametrization - maximum entropy model} then yields the following semantics for LogLinear models.

\begin{definition}[Semantics of LogLinear Models]
    Given a LogLinear model $\Phi$, a \textbf{possible world} $\omega$ is a world that models each \textbf{hard constraint} $(\pm \infty,\phi) \in \Phi$, i.e.,~${\omega \models \phi}$ whenever~${(+ \infty, \phi) \in \Phi}$ and ${\omega \models \neg \phi}$ whenever~${(- \infty, \phi) \in \Phi}$. We then associate with every possible world~$\omega$ the \textbf{weight}
    $$
    w_{\Phi} (\omega) := w (\omega)  := \prod_{\substack{(w, \phi) \in \Phi \\ w \not \in \{ \pm \infty \} \\ \omega \models \phi}} \exp(w) = \exp \left( \sum_{\substack{(w, \phi) \in \Phi \\  w \not \in \{ \pm \infty \} \\ \omega \models \phi}} w \right) 
    $$
    and set $w (\omega) = 0$ for every world $\omega$ that is not a possible world. Furthermore, we define the \textbf{weight} of a formula $\phi$ to be 
    $$
    w (\phi) := \sum_{\substack{\omega \text{ world} \\ \omega \models \phi}} w(\omega).
    $$
    Finally, we interpret weights as degrees of certainty and assign to each world or formula~$\_$ the \textbf{probability}
    $$
    \pi_{\Phi} (\_) := \pi (\_) := \dfrac{w(\_)}{w(\top)}.
    $$
\label{definition - semantics LogLinear model}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
    Let $\Phi$ be a LogLinear model. Upon committing to Parametrization~\ref{parametrization - maximum entropy model}, the weighted constraints ${(w, \phi) \in \Phi}$, where $w \in \mathbb{R}$, lack an intuitive interpretation. Only hard constraints $(\pm \infty, \phi) \in \Phi$ enforce that the formula $\phi$ or $\neg \phi$ necessarily holds.  
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
    In the situation of Example \ref{example - LogLinear model}, we find $\pi (\textit{rain} \vert \textit{cloudy}) := 2/3$ as well as~${\pi (\textit{sprinkler} \vert \neg \textit{cloudy}) := 3/4}$.  Furthermore, we deduce that the road is slippery if and only if it is wet.
    \label{example - semantics LogLinear model}
\end{example}


\subsubsection{Bayesian Networks: Causal Relations and Independence}
\label{subsubsec: Causal Bayesian Networks and the Principle of Causal Irrelevance}

Let us recall how causal relations give rise to conditional independencies: We identify a \textbf{causal structure} on a set of random variables $\textbf{V}$ with a directed acyclic graph $G$, i.e.~a partial order, on $\textbf{V}$. The intuition is that $V_1 \in \textbf{V}$ is a \textbf{cause} of $V_2 \in \textbf{V}$ if there is a directed path from $V_1$ to $V_2$ in $G$. In this case, we also say that~$V_2$ is an \textbf{effect} of $V_1$. Furthermore, we say that~$V_1$ is a \textbf{direct cause} of~$V_2$ if the edge $V_1 \rightarrow V_2$ exists in $G$, i.e. if and only if the node~${V_1 \in \Pa(V_2)}$ lies in the set~$\Pa(V_2)$ of \textbf{parents} of~$V_2$. 

\begin{example}
Example~\ref{example - introduction running example} gives rise to the following causal structure on the Boolean random variables~$
    {\mathfrak{P} := \{ \textit{cloudy},~ \textit{rain},~ \textit{sprinkler},~ \textit{wet},~ \textit{slippery} \}}:
$
$$    \begin{tikzcd}
        & \textit{rain} \arrow[rd] &               &          \\
        \textit{cloudy} \arrow[ru] \arrow[r] & \textit{sprinkler} \arrow[r] & \textit{wet} \arrow[r] & \textit{slippery}        
    \end{tikzcd}
$$
In particular, we observe that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\textit{cloudy}$ is a cause of $\textit{slippery}$ but not a direct cause.
    \item $\textit{wet}$ is a direct cause of $\textit{slippery}$.
    \item there is no causal relationship between $\textit{sprinkler}$ and $\textit{rain}$.
\end{itemize}
\label{example - causal structure}
\end{example}


A joint distribution $\pi$ on the random variables $\textbf{V}$ is consistent with a causal structure~$G$ if the influence of any cause $V_1$ on an effect $V_2$ is moderated by the direct causes of $V_2$. This intuition is captured in the Markov condition:

\begin{definition}[Markov Condition]
The joint distribution $\pi$ on the set of random variables $\textbf{V}$ satisfies the \textbf{Markov condition} with respect to a causal structure $G$ if a random variable $V_2 \in \textbf{V}$ is independent of its causes $V_1 \in \textbf{V}$ in $G$, once we observe its direct causes in $\Pa (p)$. In this case, we say that $\pi$ is \textbf{Markov} to $G$ and write $\pi \models G$.
\label{definition - Markov condition}
\end{definition} 

\begin{example}
In Example \ref{example - causal structure} the Markov condition states for instance that the influence of $\textit{cloudy}$ on the event $\textit{slippery}$ is completely moderated by the event $\textit{wet}$. Once we know that the pavement of the road is wet, we expect it to be slippery regardless of the event that caused the road to be wet. 
\end{example}

If a distribution $\pi \models G$ satisfies the Markov condition with respect to a given causal structure $G$, it is represented by a Bayesian network on $G$ and vice versa~\mbox{\cite[§1.2.3]{Causality}}:

\begin{definition}[Bayesian Network]
Let $\textbf{V}$ be a finite set of random variables. A \textbf{Bayesian network} $\textbf{BN} := (G, \pi(\_ \vert \pa(\_)))$ on~$\textbf{V}$ consists of a causal structure~$G$ and the probabilities $\pi (v \vert \pa (V)) \in [0,1]$ of the possible values $v$ of the random variables $V \in \textbf{V}$ conditioned on value assignments $\pa(V)$ of their direct causes. By applying the chain rule of probability calculus and the Markov condition in Definition \ref{definition - Markov condition}, the Bayesian network $\textbf{BN}$ assigns to  a  value assignment~$\textbf{v}$ on~$\textbf{V}$ the probability:
$$
\pi_{\textbf{BN}} (\textbf{v}) := \pi \left( \textbf{v} \right) :=
\prod_{i=1}^k \pi \left( \textbf{v}(V_i) \vert \pa(V_i) \right), \text{ where} \pa(V_i) := \textbf{v} \vert_{\Pa(p_i)} \text{ for }
1 \leq i \leq k$$    
\label{definition - Bayesian network}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
    The causal structure $G$ from Example~\ref{example - causal structure}, together with the probabilities below, gives rise to a Bayesian network~${\textbf{BN} := (G, \pi(\_\vert \pa (\_)))}$:
    \begin{align*}
        \pi (\textit{cloudy}) &= 0.5, \\
        \pi (\textit{rain} \vert \textit{cloudy}) &= 0.6, \quad &\pi (\textit{rain} \vert \neg \textit{cloudy}) &= 0, \\
        \pi (\textit{sprinkler} \vert \textit{cloudy}) &= 0.1, \quad &\pi (\textit{sprinkler} \vert \neg \textit{cloudy}) &= 0.7, \\
        \pi (\textit{wet} \vert \textit{rain}, \textit{sprinkler}) &= 0.9, \quad &\pi (\textit{wet} \vert \neg \textit{rain}, \textit{sprinkler}) &= 0.9, \\
        \pi (\textit{wet} \vert \textit{rain}, \neg \textit{sprinkler}) &= 0.9, \quad &\pi (\textit{wet} \vert \neg \textit{rain}, \neg \textit{sprinkler}) &= 0, \\
        \pi (\textit{slippery} \vert \textit{wet}) &= 0.8, \quad &\pi (\textit{slippery} \vert \neg \textit{wet}) &= 0.
    \end{align*}
    We obtain 
    $
        \pi(\textit{cloudy}, \textit{rain}, \textit{sprinkler}, \textit{wet}, \textit{slippery}) = 
        0.5 \cdot 0.6 \cdot 0.1  \cdot 0.9 \cdot 0.8.
    $
    \label{example - Bayesian network}
\end{example}




\subsubsection{Probabilistic Causal Models}
\label{subsubsec: Probabilistic Functional Causal Models}

\cite{Causality} introduces probabilities into a functional causal model $\mathcal{M}$ by specifying a probability distribution over the situations of $\mathcal{M}$. %In this way, he obtains a probabilistic version of Aristotelian knowledge. 

\begin{definition}[Probabilistic Causal Model]
    A \textbf{probabilistic (Boolean) causal model} $\mathbb{M} := (\mathcal{M}, \pi)$ is given by a (Boolean) functional causal model~$\mathcal{M}$ together with a probability distribution $\pi$ on the situations of $\mathcal{M}$. The \textbf{causal diagram} $\graph(\mathbb{M})$ of the probabilistic causal model $\mathbb{M}$ is given by the causal diagram $\graph(\mathcal{M})$ of the functional causal model $\mathcal{M}$. We call the model $\mathbb{M}$ \textbf{acyclic} if the functional causal model $\mathcal{M}$ is.
    \label{definition - probabilistic causal model}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
Modify Example \ref{example - sprinkler deterministic} and consider a road that passes through a field with a sprinkler~in~it. The sprinkler is turned on, written $\textit{sprinkler}$, by a weather sensor with probability~$0.1$ if it is cloudy, denoted by $\textit{cloudy}$ and with probability~$0.7$ if it is not cloudy. In addition, it rains, denoted by~$\textit{rain}$, with probability $0.6$ if the weather is cloudy. If it rains or the sprinkler is on, the pavement of the road gets wet, denoted by~$\textit{wet}$, with probability $0.9$. And in the case where the pavement is wet, we observe with a probability of $0.8$ that the road is slippery, denoted by~$\textit{slippery}$. 

This mechanism can be represented by a Boolean functional causal model,~$\mathcal{M}$, with internal variables $\textbf{V} := \{ \textit{cloudy},~ \textit{rain},~ \textit{sprinkler},~ \textit{wet},~ \textit{slippery} \}$, external variables $\textbf{U} := \{ u_1,...,u_6 \}$ and structural equations:
\begin{align*}
&\mathcal{M}: && \textit{cloudy} := u_1 \\
&&& \textit{rain} := \textit{cloudy} \land u_2 \\
&&& \textit{sprinkler} := ( \textit{cloudy}  \land u_3) \lor (\neg \textit{cloudy} \land u_4) \\
&&&  \textit{wet}   := ( \textit{rain}  \lor \textit{sprinkler}) \land u_5 
&&  slippery   :=  \textit{wet}   \land u_6
\end{align*}

To represent the uncertainties in our story, we specify the probabilities: ${\pi(u_1) = 0.5}$, ${\pi(u_2) = 0.6}$, ${\pi(u_3) = 0.1}$, ${\pi(u_4) = 0.7}$, ${\pi(u_5) = 0.9}$ and ${\pi(u_6) = 0.8}$. 
Asserting that $u_1,...,u_6$ are mutually independent random variables defines a unique distribution $\pi$ on the situations of~$\mathcal{M}$, resulting in the probabilistic Boolean causal model $\mathbb{M} := (\mathcal{M}, \pi)$. 

Here, we assume that uncertainty arises from hidden variables that we do not explicitly model. However, the influence of these hidden variables is encapsulated in the external variables ${\textbf{U} := \{ u_1,...,u_6 \}}$. For example, the variable $u_3$ summarizes the potential causes of why the sprinkler could be on if it is cloudy. These potential causes, such as sensor failure or children playing and manually switching on the sprinkler, are not explicitly modeled in~$\mathcal{M}$. Nevertheless, the potential influence of these missing parameters is represented by the external variables~$\textbf{U}$ and the distribution $\pi$. 
\label{example - introduction - sprinkler}
\end{example}

Let $\mathbb{M} := (\mathcal{M}, \pi)$ be a probabilistic causal model. Since this implies that~$\mathcal{M}$ is a functional causal model, every situation $\textbf{u}$ corresponds to a unique solution~$\textbf{s}(\textbf{u})$ of the corresponding system of equations. Hence, by defining 
$$
\pi_{\mathbb{M}}(\omega) :=
\begin{cases}
    \pi (\textbf{u}), & \text{if } \omega = \textbf{s}(\textbf{u}) \\
    0, & \text{else}
\end{cases}
$$
for every value assignment $\omega$ of the variables $\textbf{U} \cup \textbf{V}$, the model $\mathbb{M}$ gives rise to a joint probability distribution of the random variables in $\textbf{U} \cup \textbf{V}$.

\begin{example}
In Example \ref{example - introduction - sprinkler}, the causal model $\mathbb{M}$ yields a probability distribution $\pi_{\mathbb{M}}$ on the truth value assignments for the variables 
$$
\textbf{U} \cup \textbf{V} := \{ \textit{cloudy},~ \textit{rain},~ \textit{sprinkler},~ \textit{wet},~ \textit{slippery},~ u_1,...,u_6
 \}
.$$  
This allows us, for instance, to calculate the probability $\pi_{\mathbb{M}} (\textit{rain})$ that it rains:
\begin{align*}
 &   \pi_{\mathbb{M}} (\textit{rain}) = \pi (u_1) \cdot \pi (u_2) = 0.5 \cdot 0.6 = 0.3
\end{align*}
\end{example}

Let us recall the relation between Bayesian networks and causal models:

\begin{definition}[Markovian Causal Models]
    An acyclic probabilistic causal model $\mathbb{M} := (\mathcal{M}, \pi)$ is \textbf{Markovian} if $\pi$ interprets the error terms as mutually independent random variables. 
    \label{definition - Markovian Causal Models}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\cite{Causality}, §1.4.2]
    A Markovian causal model $\mathbb{M}$ gives rise to a distribution $\pi$ that is Markov to its causal diagram, i.e., $\pi \models \graph (\mathbb{M})$. We conclude that the distribution~$\pi$ can be represented by a Bayesian network on the causal diagram $\graph (\mathbb{M})$.~$\square$
    \label{theorem - causal models and Bayesian networks}
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
    The causal model in Example \ref{example - introduction - sprinkler} is Markovian. It gives rise to the Bayesian network in Example \ref{example - Bayesian network}.
\end{example}

Finally, probabilistic causal models do not only support queries about conditional and unconditional probabilities. They also support queries for the effect of external interventions:

Fix a probabilistic causal model $\mathbb{M} := (\mathcal{M}, \pi)$ with external variables $\textbf{U}$ and internal variables~$\textbf{V}$.
Assume we are given a subset of internal variables~\mbox{$\textbf{I} \subseteq \textbf{V}$} together with a value assignment~$\textbf{i}$. To calculate the effect of forcing the variables in $\textbf{I}$ to attain the values specified by~$\textbf{i}$, we build the \textbf{modified model} or \textbf{submodel}  $\mathbb{M}_{\textbf{i}} := (\mathcal{M}_{\textbf{i}}, \pi)$. From the modified model $\mathbb{M}_{\textbf{i}}$ we can then calculate the desired \textbf{post-interventional probabilities}. According to §1.4.3 in \mbox{\cite{Causality}}, we denote the resulting probability distribution by 
$$
\pi_{\mathbb{M}} (\_ \vert \Do(\textbf{I} := \textbf{i})) := \pi_{\mathbb{M}} (\_ \vert \Do(\textbf{i})).
$$ 
Here, again Pearl's do-operator $\Do (\_)$ indicates that we actively intervene to change our model. For an event $A$ we therefore call $\pi(A \vert \Do (\textbf{i}))$ the probability of $A$ after \textbf{intervening} according to $\textbf{i}$.

\begin{example}
We recall Example \ref{example - introduction - sprinkler} and ask for the post-interventional probability that the road is slippery after turning off the sprinkler. In this case, we query the modified model~$\mathbb{M}_{\neg \textit{sprinkler}}$ for $\textit{slippery}$ to obtain the probability
$$
\pi_{\mathbb{M}} (\textit{slippery} \vert \Do (\neg \textit{sprinkler})) = \pi(u_1) \cdot \pi (u_2) \cdot \pi (u_5) \cdot\pi (u_6) =  0.216
$$ 
for the road to be slippery after switching the sprinkler off. 

We highlight that this result differs from the conditional probability 
$$
\pi_{\mathbb{M}} (\textit{slippery} \vert \neg \textit{sprinkler}) = \dfrac{\pi(u_1) \cdot \pi(u_2) \cdot \pi (\neg u_3) \cdot \pi (u_5) \cdot \pi (u_6)}{\pi(u_1) \cdot \pi (\neg u_3) + \pi(\neg u_1) \cdot \pi (\neg u_4)} = 0.432
$$ that it is slippery if we observe the sprinkler to be off. In particular, observing the sprinkler being off enhances the probability that it is cloudy, making rain more probable, while manually turning the sprinkler off does not allow such a conclusion. Therefore, in general, intervening in a model yields outcomes that differ from deriving conclusions solely from observations.
\label{example - intervantions in probabilistic causal models}
\end{example}

Theorem \ref{theorem - causal models and Bayesian networks} yields the following notion of intervention in Bayesian networks:

\begin{definition}[Intervention in Bayesian Networks]
    Let $G := (\textbf{V}, \textbf{E})$ be a directed acyclic graph and let $\textbf{I} \subseteq \textbf{V}$ be a subset of its nodes. The graph
    $$
    G_{\textbf{I}} := (\textbf{V}, \textbf{E}_{\textbf{I}}) \text{, } \textbf{E}_{\textbf{I}} := \{ (V_1,V_2) \in \textbf{E} \text{: } V_2 \not \in \textbf{I}  \})
    $$
    is obtained by erasing all arrows from $G$ that point into a node in $\textbf{I}$.

    Let $\textbf{BN} := (G, \pi (\_ \vert \pa(\_)))$ be a Bayesian network that induceds the distribution~$\pi$ and let $\textbf{i}$ be a value assignment on the random variables in $\textbf{I}$. Intervening and forcing the vatiables in $\textbf{I}$ to attain the valued specified by $\textbf{i}$ leads to the \textbf{modified Bayesian network} $\textbf{BN}_{\textbf{i}} := (G_{\textbf{I}}, \pi_{\textbf{i}} (\_ \vert \pa_{G_{\textbf{I}}}(\_)))$,  where
    $$
    \pi_{\textbf{i}} (v \vert \pa_{G_{\textbf{I}}}(V)) := 
    \begin{cases}
         1, &  \text{if } V \in \textbf{I} \text{ and } v = \textbf{i}(V) \\
         0, &   \text{if } V \in \textbf{I} \text{ and } v \neq \textbf{i}(V) \\ 
         \pi (v \vert \pa (V)), & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}.
    $$
    The modified Bayesian network gives rise to the \textbf{post-interventional} distribution~$\pi(\_ \vert \Do (\textbf{i}))$. For an event $A$ we call $\pi(A \vert \Do (\textbf{i}))$ the probability of $A$ being true after \textbf{intervening according} to $\textbf{i}$.
    \label{definition - intervention in Bayesian networks}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
    Recall the Bayesian network from Example~\ref{example - Bayesian network}. 
    Intervening and switching the sprinkler on results in the modified Bayesian network $\textbf{BN}_{\textbf{i}}$ with the causal structure:
    $$
    \begin{tikzcd}
        & \textit{rain} \arrow[rd] &               &          \\
        \textit{cloudy} \arrow[ru]  & \textit{sprinkler} \arrow[r] & \textit{wet} \arrow[r] & \textit{slippery}        
    \end{tikzcd}
    $$
    The corresponding probabilities are obtained by replacing the conditional probabilities $\pi(\textit{sprinkler} \vert (\neg) \textit{cloudy})$  with ${\pi(\textit{sprinkler})=1}$, reflecting the intervention.
    \label{example - intervening in a Bayesian network}
\end{example}

Observe that the notion of intervention in causal models aligns with that in Bayesian networks.

\begin{theorem}[\cite{Causality}, §1.4.3]
    Let $\mathbb{M}$ be a Markovian causal model that gives rise to the Bayesian network $\textbf{BN}$. Further, let $\textbf{i}$ be a value assignment on a subset of internal variables  $\textbf{I}$ on $\mathbb{M}$. The modified causal model $\mathbb{M}_{\textbf{i}}$ and the modified Bayesian network $\textbf{BN}_{\textbf{i}}$ induce the same post-interventional distribution~${\pi (\_ \vert \Do (\textbf{i}))}$.~$\square$
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Causal Irrelevance and Reichenbach's Common Cause Assumption}
\label{subsec: Causal Irrelevance and Reichenbach's Common Cause Assumption}

Similar to \textit{sufficient causation} in Assumption~\ref{principle - Leibniz}, \mbox{\cite{Reichenbach}} argues in Chapter 19 that probabilistic dependence typically arises from causal relations, leading to the following assertion:

\begin{assumption}[Common Causes]
    If two random variables~\( X \) and \( Y \) are dependent, then either \( X \) is a cause of \( Y \), \( Y \) is a cause of \( X \), or there exists a common cause \( Z \) of \( X \) and \( Y \).
    \label{assumption - common cause assumption}
\end{assumption}

Let \( \mathbb{M} \) be an acyclic probabilistic causal model. In this case, \textit{common causes} in Assumption~\ref{assumption - common cause assumption} states that all probabilistic dependencies in the induced distribution \( \pi(\_) \) originate from causal knowledge in~\( \mathbb{M} \). Here, we adopt the viewpoint that this holds if and only if \( \pi(\_) \) interprets the error terms as mutually independent random variables. In summary, an acyclic probabilistic causal model~\( \mathbb{M} \) satisfies \textit{common causes} in Assumption~\ref{assumption - common cause assumption} if and only if it is Markovian.

According to Theorem~\ref{theorem - causal models and Bayesian networks}, a Markovian causal model corresponds to a Bayesian network \( \textbf{BN} := (G, \pi(\_ \vert \pa(\_))) \) on the internal variables \( \textbf{V} \) of \( \mathbb{M} \). \mbox{\cite{Williamson2001}} further shows that the induced distribution \( \pi(\_) \) is obtained by maximizing the entropy \( H(\pi) \) under the following constraints:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Each variable \( V \in \textbf{V} \) attains the value \( v \) with probability \( \pi(v \vert \pa(V)) \), conditioned on its direct causes \( \Pa(V) \) taking the values $\pa (V)$.
    \item The following principle of \textit{causal irrelevance} holds:
    \begin{principle}[Causal Irrelevance]
        Observing that a random variable \( V_1 \) has an effect \( V_2 \) with an unknown value does not affect our belief in \( V_1 \).  
        \label{principle - causal irrelevance}
    \end{principle}
\end{itemize}

Intuitively, \textit{causal irrelevance} in Principle~\ref{principle - causal irrelevance} implies that a rational agent expects information to flow only from causes to effects. Thus, the agent assumes a joint distribution \( \pi(\_) \) on \( \textbf{V} \) that maximizes the entropy \( H(\pi) \) greedily along an order consistent with the causal structure \( G \): 

\begin{formalization}
    The agent begins by maximizing the entropy \( H(\pi) \) under the constraint that the source variables \( V \) in \( G \) take their possible values \( v \) with probabilities~\( \pi(v) \) as specified in \( \textbf{BN} = (G, \pi (\_ \mid \pa(\_))\). This yields a joint distribution \( \Tilde{\pi} (\_) \) on a subset of variables~\( {\textbf{W} \subseteq \textbf{V}} \). The agent then iteratively maximizes the entropy~\( H(\pi) \) under the following constraints until a distribution~\( \pi \) on \( \textbf{V} \) is obtained:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item The joint distribution on \( \textbf{W} \) is given by \( \Tilde{\pi} \).
        \item For each variable \( V \in \textbf{V} \) with direct causes \( \Pa(V) \subseteq \textbf{W} \), the variable~\( V \) takes its possible values \( v \) with probability \( \pi(v \vert \pa(V)) \) when its direct causes \( \Pa(V) \) take the values~\( \pa(V) \). 
    \end{itemize}
    \label{formalization - causal irrelevance}
\end{formalization}


To summarize, \cite{Williamson2001} obtains the following result:

\begin{theorem}[§5.2, \cite{Williamson2001}]
    Let \( \textbf{BN} := (G, \pi (\_ \vert \pa(\_))) \) be a Bayesian network. The induced distribution \( \pi_{\textbf{BN}}(\_) \) is the distribution extending the probability specifications \( \pi (\_ \vert \pa(\_)) \) by maximizing entropy under \textit{causal irrelevance} in Principle~\ref{principle - causal irrelevance}, as expressed in Formalization \ref{formalization - causal irrelevance}.   
    \label{theorem - causal irrelevance and Bayesian networks}
\end{theorem}

We henceforth assume that a rational agent applies \textit{causal irrelevance} in Principle~\ref{principle - causal irrelevance} if and only if he excludes the possibility that information eventually flows through omitted common causes. Thus, we argue for the following result:  

\begin{formalization}
    A rational agent applies \textit{causal irrelevance} in Principle~\ref{principle - causal irrelevance}, as expressed in Formalization~\ref{formalization - causal irrelevance}, if and only if \textit{common causes} in Assumption~\ref{assumption - common cause assumption} is satisfied.  
    \label{formalization - common cause assumption}
\end{formalization}  

We adopt the viewpoint that the principle of maximum entropy corresponds to the \textit{syllogisms} in Section~\ref{subsubsec: Knowledge by demonstration}. According to Section~\ref{subsubsec: Knowledge that and knowledge why}, \mbox{knowledge-\textit{why}} arises from \textit{demonstrations}, i.e., \textit{syllogisms} that follow a given causal order. We argue that greedily maximizing entropy along the causal order in Bayesian networks in Formalization \ref{formalization - causal irrelevance} provides a probabilistic generalization of Aristotle’s notion of \mbox{knowledge-\textit{why}}.


\subsection{Causal Systems: A Generic Representation of Causal Reasoning}
\label{subsec: Causal Systems: A Generic Representation of Causal Reasoning}

To express uncertainty about \textit{natural necessity} in Principle~\ref{principle - Aquinas}, we propose the following notion of a weighted causal theory:

\begin{definition}[Weighted Causal Theory]
    A \textbf{weighted causal rule}~\( (w, R) \) consists of a weight \( w \in \mathbb{R} \cup \{ +\infty, -\infty \} \) and a literal causal rule \( R \). A \textbf{weighted causal theory}~\( \Theta \) is a finite set of weighted causal rules. 

    The \textbf{explanatory content} of a weighted causal theory \( \Theta \) is the \mbox{causal theory}  
    \[
    \explanatory (\Theta) := \{ R \mid \exists w \text{ such that } (w,R) \in \Theta \}.
    \]
    The \textbf{constraint content} of a weighted causal theory \( \Theta \) is the \mbox{LogLinear model}  
    \[
    \constraint (\Theta) := \{ (w, \constraint(R)) \mid (w,R) \in \Theta \}.
    \]
    The \textbf{causal structure} \( \graph (\Theta) \) of \( \Theta \) is the directed graph where an edge \( p \to q \) is drawn if and only if there exists a weighted causal rule of the form  
    \[
    (w,~ b_1 \land \dots \land (\neg) p \land \dots \land b_n \Rightarrow (\neg) q) \in \Theta.
    \]
    \label{definition - weighted causal theory}
\end{definition}

Motivated by Formalizations~\ref{formalization - natural necessity} and~\ref{formalization - sufficient causation}, we adopt the following approach to introducing uncertainty into causal reasoning:  

\begin{Language}[Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning]
    Weights $w \in \mathbb{R} \cup \{ +\infty, -\infty \}$ in a weighted causal theory $\Theta$ represent our degree of belief in \emph{natural necessity}, as stated in Principle~\ref{principle - Aquinas}. Specifically, the information in $\constraint (\Theta)$ defines a distribution that quantifies our degree of belief that a given world $\omega$ satisfies \emph{natural necessity} with respect to $\explanatory(\Theta)$. The causal theory $\explanatory (\Theta)$ is then used to determine whether a given world $\omega$ can be causally explained, i.e., whether \emph{sufficient causation}, as formulated in Assumption~\ref{principle - Leibniz}, applies.    
    \label{language - Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning}
\end{Language}

\begin{example}
    Recall the situation in Example~\ref{example - counterexample Bochman}. However, this time we are uncertain whether a fire in house \( h_1 \) necessarily leads to a fire in the neighboring house \( h_2 \) and vice versa. Our uncertainty may lead to the weighted causal theory \( \Theta \):
    \begin{align*}
         & (\ln(2),~ \textit{fire}(h_2) \Rightarrow \textit{fire}(h_1)),  && (\ln(2),~ \textit{fire}(h_1) \Rightarrow \textit{fire}(h_2)). %\\
         %& (+\infty,  \textit{start\_fire}(h_1) \Rightarrow \textit{fire}(h_1)),  && (+\infty, \textit{start\_fire}(h_2) \Rightarrow \textit{fire}(h_2)).
     \end{align*}
     \label{example - cyclic weighted causal theory}
\end{example}

\begin{example}
    Calculating the weights $w_1$ -- $w_8$ according to Equation~(\ref{equation - transforming parameters of Bayesian network}), the situation in Examples  \ref{example - Bayesian network} and \ref{example - introduction - sprinkler}  give rise to the weighted causal theory~$\Theta$:
    \begin{align*}
        &(w_1,~ \top \Rightarrow \textit{cloudy})  \\
        &(w_2,~ \textit{cloudy} \Rightarrow \textit{rain})  && (- \infty,~ \neg \textit{cloudy} \Rightarrow \textit{rain} ), \\
        &(w_3,~ \textit{cloudy} \Rightarrow \textit{sprinkler})  && (w_4,~ \neg \textit{cloudy} \Rightarrow \textit{sprinkler}) \\
        &(w_5,~ \textit{rain}\land \textit{sprinkler} \Rightarrow \textit{wet}  ) && (w_6,~ \neg \textit{rain} \land \textit{sprinkler} \Rightarrow \textit{wet} )  \\
        &(w_7,~ \textit{rain}\land \neg \textit{sprinkler} \Rightarrow \textit{wet} ) &&  (- \infty,~ \neg \textit{rain} \land \neg \textit{sprinkler} \Rightarrow \textit{wet})  \\
        &(w_8,~ \textit{wet} \Rightarrow \textit{slippery} ) &&
        (- \infty,~ \neg \textit{wet} \Rightarrow \textit{slippery}  )
    \end{align*}
    Note, however, that according to Remark \ref{remark - metaphysical justification} the expression $\top \Rightarrow \textit{cloudy}$ requires additional justification.
    \label{example - acyclic weighted causal theory}
\end{example}

Let $\Theta$ be a weighted causal theory. We begin by examining the impact of \textit{common causes}, as stated in Assumption~\ref{assumption - common cause assumption}, on the distribution $\pi$ associated with the LogLinear model $\constraint (\Theta)$.%, which quantifies our degree of belief in \textit{natural necessity}, as formulated in Principle~\ref{principle - Aquinas}.  

Suppose the causal knowledge represented by the causal structure \( \graph (\Theta) \) satisfies \textit{common causes}, as stated in Assumption~\ref{assumption - common cause assumption}. According to Formalization \ref{formalization - common cause assumption}, we conclude that \textit{causal irrelevance} in Principle \ref{principle - causal irrelevance}, as expressed in Formalization~\ref{formalization - causal irrelevance}, applies.  Together with Parametrization~\ref{parametrization - maximum entropy model}, this leads to the following semantics of the weighted causal theory \( \Theta \):  



\begin{definition}[Common Cause Semantics of a Weighted Causal Theory]
    Recall that two nodes \( p \) and \( q \) of a directed graph \( G := (V,E) \) are 
    \textbf{strongly connected}, written~\( p \sim q \), if there exist directed paths 
    from \( p \) to \( q \) and from~\( q \) to \( p \) in~\( G \). Strong connectedness 
    (\(\sim\))/2 is an equivalence relation, and the equivalence classes \( [p] \in V/\sim \) 
    are called the \textbf{strongly connected components} of \( G \). Lastly, the resulting 
    \textbf{factor graph} \( G/\sim := (V/\sim, E/\sim) \) is a directed acyclic graph, 
    where \( E/\sim := \{ ([p],[q]) \in (V/\sim)^2 \mid (p,q) \in E \} \).

    Let \( \Theta \) be a weighted causal theory. We write \( \textbf{G} := (\textbf{V}, \textbf{E}) \) 
    for the factor graph \( \graph(\Theta)/\sim \) on the strongly connected components \( \textbf{V} \) 
    of the causal structure \( \graph (\Theta) \). To each strongly connected component \( V \in \textbf{V} \), 
    we associate a random variable that takes the assignments \( v : V \rightarrow \{ \textit{True},~ \textit{False} \} \) as its values. Furthermore, we associate with a strongly connected component \( V \in \textbf{V} \) the LogLinear model:
    \[
    \constraint(\Theta)_V :=  \{ (w, \phi \rightarrow p) \in \constraint(\Theta) \mid p \in V \}.
    \]
    The \textbf{common cause semantics} of the weighted causal theory \( \Theta \) is then given by the Bayesian network \( \textbf{BN}(\Theta) := (\textbf{G}, \pi_{\Theta}(\_ \mid \pa(\_))) \), where
    \[
    \pi_{\Theta} (v \mid \pa(V)) := \pi_{\constraint(\Theta)_V}(v \mid \pa(V)) 
    \]
    for \( V \in \textbf{V} \), a possible value \( v \) of \( V \), and a value assignment \( \pa(V) \) on the direct causes \( \Pa_{\textbf{G}}(V) \).
    By abuse of language, we also call the joint distribution~\( {\pi_{\Theta}(\_) :=  \pi_{\textbf{BN}(\Theta)}(\_) }\) the \textbf{common cause semantics}. As every value assignment on \( \textbf{V} \) can be identified with a world~\( \omega \), the common cause semantics~\( \pi_{\Theta} \) yields a distribution on worlds \( \omega \). 
    \label{definition - Semantics of a Weighted Causal Theory}
\end{definition}


\begin{example}
    The common cause semantics of the weighted causal theory~\( \Theta \) in Example \ref{example - cyclic weighted causal theory} is the distribution of the LogLinear model~\( \constraint(\Theta) \), as both propositions \( \textit{fire}(h_1) \) and \( \textit{fire}(h_2) \) lie in the same strongly connected component of the causal structure \( \graph(\Theta) \). Theorem~\ref{theorem - weighted causal theories an Bayesian networks} yields that the weighted causal theory in Example \ref{example - acyclic weighted causal theory} results in the same distribution as the Bayesian network in Example \ref{example - Bayesian network}.
    \label{example - common cause semantics}
\end{example}

Further analysis of the common cause semantics leads to the following result:


\begin{proposition}
Let $\Theta$ be a weighted causal theory. The common cause semantics~$\pi_{\Theta}$ is obtained by greedily maximizing the entropy~$H (\pi)$ along an order consistent with the causal structure $\graph (\Theta)$, subject to the \mbox{constraint}  
    \[
    \pi (\constraint (R)) = \sum_{\pa (V)}\pi_{\constraint(\Theta)_V} (\constraint (R) \vert \pa(V)) \cdot \pi (\pa(V))
    \]
    for every causal rule $R \in \explanatory(\Theta)$ with effect in the strongly connected component~$V$ of $\graph (\Theta)$. 
    \label{proposition - common cause semantics}
\end{proposition}  

\begin{proof}
    All nodes in a strongly connected component $V$ of~$\graph (\Theta)$ cannot be distinguished in any causal order consistent with $\graph(\Theta)$. Hence, according to Principle \ref{principle - causal irrelevance}, as expressed in Formalization \ref{formalization - causal irrelevance}, the entropy $H (\pi)$ for all random variables in $V$ needs to be maximized simultaneously under the constraint that for all rules~${R := (w,\phi \Rightarrow p )  \in \Theta}$ with $p \in V$ we find:
    \begin{align*}
        &\pi (\constraint (R)) = \sum_{\pa(V)}
        \pi (\constraint (R) \vert \pa(V)) \cdot \pi (\pa(V)) = \\
        & = \sum_{\pa (V)}\pi_{\constraint(\Theta)_V} (\constraint (R) \vert \pa(V)) \cdot \pi (\pa(V)) = \\
        & = \left( \sum_{\substack{v~\text{ass.~on V}  \\ v \cup \pa(V) \models \constraint(R)}} \pi_{\constraint(\Theta)_V} (v \vert \pa(V)) \right) \cdot \pi (\pa(V))
    \end{align*}
    Conclude inductively that $\pi_{\Theta} (\pa(V)) = \pi (\pa (V))$. The result then follows from Theorem \ref{theorem - causal irrelevance and Bayesian networks}, Parametrization \ref{parametrization - maximum entropy model} and Definition \ref{definition - Semantics of a Weighted Causal Theory} as the distribution ${\pi_{\constraint(\Delta)_V}(\_ \vert \pa(V))}$ is obtained by maximizing the entropy $H(\pi)$ under the constraints 
    $
    \pi (\constraint (R) \vert \pa (V)) = \pi_{\constraint(\Theta)_V} (\constraint (R) \vert \pa(V)).
    $
\end{proof}


According to Proposition \ref{proposition - common cause semantics}, the common cause semantics~$\pi_{\Theta}$ follows from the following principles:  
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textit{Maximum entropy}, as stated in Principle~\ref{principle - maximum entropy}, and Parametrization~\ref{parametrization - maximum entropy model}.
        \item \textit{Causal irrelevance}, as formulated in Principle~\ref{principle - causal irrelevance} and Formalization \ref{formalization - causal irrelevance}, which corresponds to \textit{common causes}, as stated in Assumption~\ref{assumption - common cause assumption}, according to Formalization \ref{formalization - common cause assumption}.
    \end{itemize}
Language~\ref{language - Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning} implies that under Assumption \ref{assumption - common cause assumption} the common cause semantics~$\pi_{\Theta}$ quantifies our degree of belief that \textit{natural necessity}, as expressed in Principle~\ref{principle - Aquinas}, holds with respect to~$\explanatory(\Theta)$.
We argue for the following result:

\begin{formalization}
    According to Languages~\ref{language - propositional logic}, \ref{language - causal reasoning}, \ref{formalization - causal theories}, \ref{language - Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning}, and Parametrization~\ref{parametrization - maximum entropy model}, causal reasoning under uncertainty gives rise to a weighted causal theory~$\Theta$. If we further assume that Assumption~\ref{assumption - common cause assumption} is satisfied, the common cause semantics~$\pi_{\Theta}(\_)$ quantifies our degree of belief that \emph{natural necessity}, as expressed in Principle~\ref{principle - Aquinas}, holds with respect to~$\explanatory(\Theta)$.
    \label{formalization - justification for common cause semantics}
\end{formalization}


Weighted causal theories under the common cause semantics consistently generalize Bayesian networks:

\begin{definition}[Necessity Interpretation]
    Define the \textbf{sigmoid function} by  
    \begin{align*}
       & \sigma && : && \mathbb{R} \cup \{ + \infty, - \infty \} && \rightarrow && [0,1] \\
       &&&&& w && \mapsto && 
       \begin{cases}
       \frac{\exp(w)}{1 + \exp(w)} , & w \in \mathbb{R},  \\
       0, & w = - \infty, \\
       1, & w = + \infty.
       \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    The sigmoid function is~\mbox{bijective}, and we write $\sigma^{-1} : [0,1] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ for its inverse.

    Let $\textbf{BN} := (G, \pi (\_ \vert  \pa(\_)))$ be a Boolean Bayesian network on the set of random variables $\mathfrak{P}$. The \textbf{necessity interpretation} of $\textbf{BN}$ is the weighted causal theory $\Theta(\textbf{BN})$ with one weighted causal rule  
    \begin{equation}
            (w, \pa(p) \Rightarrow p), \quad  w := \sigma^{-1} \left[~ \pi_{\textbf{BN}}(p \vert \pa(p)) \cdot \pi_{\textbf{BN}}(\pa(p)) + \pi_{\textbf{BN}} (\neg \pa(p)) ~\right]
    \label{equation - transforming parameters of Bayesian network}
    \end{equation}
    for every $p \in \mathfrak{P}$ and value assignment $\pa (p)$ on the direct causes $\Pa(p)$ of~$p$.  
    Here, we associate truth value assignments $\pa(p)$ with formulas  
    $ \displaystyle
    \pa(p) :=  \bigwedge_{\substack{l \text{ literal} \\ \pa(p) \models l}} l.
    $
    \label{definition - necessity interpretation}
\end{definition}


\begin{example}
    The necessity interpretation of the Bayesian network in Example \ref{example - Bayesian network} is presented in Example \ref{example - acyclic weighted causal theory}.
    \label{example -  necessity interpretation}
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}
    Let $\textbf{BN} := (G, \pi (\_ \vert \pa (\_)))$ be a Boolean Bayesian network. The necessity interpretation $\Theta := \Theta (\textbf{BN})$ induces the same joint distribution as $\textbf{BN}$,~i.e.,  
    $
    \pi_{\textbf{BN}} (\omega) = \pi_{\Theta} (\omega)
    $
    for all worlds $\omega$.    
    \label{theorem - weighted causal theories an Bayesian networks}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    According to Proposition \ref{proposition - common cause semantics}, 
    the distribution $\pi_{\Theta}(\_)$ results from greedly maximizing the entropy $H(\pi)$ along an order consistent with the causal structure~$G$ under the constraint that for every rule $R := (w, \pa(p) \Rightarrow p) \in \Theta (\textbf{BN})$ we find
    \begin{align*}
    & \pi_{\Theta} (\pa(p) \rightarrow p) =  \pi_{\constraint(\Theta)_{\{p\}}} (\pa(p) \rightarrow p \vert \pa (p)) \cdot \pi_{\Theta} (\pa (p)) =  \\
    & \stackrel{\text{construction}}{=} \pi_{\textbf{BN}} (p \vert \pa (p)) \cdot \pi_{\Theta} (\pa (p)).    
    \end{align*}
    As we may inductively conclude that $\pi_{\Theta} (\pa(p)) = \pi_{\textbf{BN}} (\pa (p))$, we obtain that~$\pi_{\Theta}(\_)$ also results from greedly maximizing the entropy $H(\pi)$ along an order consistent with the causal structure $G$ under the constraint that 
    $$
    \pi_{\Theta} (p \vert \pa(p)) := \pi_{\textbf{BN}} (p \vert \pa(p)).
    $$
    Hence, the desired result follows from Theorem \ref{theorem - causal irrelevance and Bayesian networks}.
\end{proof}

%\subsubsection{Maximum Entropy Causal Systems}
%\label{subsubsec: Maximum Entropy Causal Systems}

To reason on knowledge-\textit{why} within an area of science, we extend causal reasoning, as captured in weighted causal theories, by incorporating external premises and propose the notion of a maximum entropy causal system.


\begin{definition}[Maximum Entropy Causal System]   
    A \textbf{(maximum entropy) causal system} is a tuple
    $$
    \textbf{CS} := (\Theta,~\mathcal{E},~\mathcal{O},~\Sigma,~\texttt{complete})
    $$ 
    consisting of the following components:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item A weighted causal theory~$\Theta(\textbf{CS}) := \Theta$, called \textbf{causal knowledge} of~$\textbf{CS}$.
        \item A set of literals $\mathcal{E}(\textbf{CS}) := \mathcal{E}$, called the \textbf{external premises} of~$\textbf{CS}$.
        \item A set of formulas $\mathcal{O}(\textbf{CS}) := \mathcal{O}$, called the \textbf{observations} of~$\textbf{CS}$.
        \item A \textbf{superordinate science}, that is, a LogLinear model~${\Sigma(\textbf{CS}) := \Sigma}$.
        \item A Boolean value $\texttt{complete} \in \{ \top, \bot \}$.
    \end{itemize}
    If $\texttt{complete} = \top$, we say that the causal system $\textbf{CS}$ is \textbf{complete}. Otherwise, we say that the causal system $\textbf{CS}$ is \textbf{incomplete}. 

    A \textbf{situation} $\textbf{s} \subseteq \mathcal{E}$ is a subset of external premises such that $\textbf{s} = \omega \cap \mathcal{E}$ for a world $\omega$. The \textbf{external domain} $\textbf{D}(\textbf{CS})$ of $\textbf{CS}$ is the set of all situations.

    The \textbf{constraint part} of the causal system $\textbf{CS}$ is the LogLinear model
    $$
    \CONST (\textbf{CS}) :=  \constraint (\Theta).
    $$ 
    The \textbf{explanatory part} of $\textbf{CS}$ is the deterministic causal system 
    $$
    \EXP (\textbf{CS}) := (\EXP (\Theta), \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O}).
    $$
    The causal system $\textbf{CS}$ has the \textbf{causal structure} 
    $
    \graph(\textbf{CS}) := \graph (\Theta).
    $
    The system $\textbf{CS}$ is \textbf{without observations} or applies \textbf{default negation} if its explanatory content $\EXP (\textbf{CS})$ does. 
    \label{definition - maximum entropy causal system}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
    The letters $\Theta$, $\mathcal{E}$, $\mathcal{O}$, and $\Sigma$ in Definition~\ref{definition - maximum entropy causal system} spell ``Theos,'' which is the Greek word for ``God.'' 
\end{remark}


This work uses Definition \ref{definition - maximum entropy causal system} together with the following guideline:

\begin{Language}
    Fix an area of science, as described in Section~\ref{subsec: Applying Aristotle's Notion of Knowledge in Artificial Intelligence}. Let~$\mathcal{E}$ denote a set of external premises that do not require further explanation. If $\mathcal{E}$ represents the entire set of external premises, we set $\texttt{complete} := \top$; otherwise, we set~${\texttt{complete} := \bot}$.

    Let~$\Theta$ be the weighted causal theory in Language~\ref{language - Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning}, assume the \mbox{knowledge-\emph{that}} of the superordinate science is captured in a LogLinear model~$\Sigma$, and express all observations as a set of formulas~$\mathcal{O}$ to obtain a maximum entropy causal system:
    $$
    \mathbf{CS} := (\Theta, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O}, \Sigma, \texttt{complete}).
    $$
    We assume that the causal structure $\graph (\textbf{CS})$ satisfies \emph{common causes} in Assumption~\ref{assumption - common cause assumption} if and only if $\texttt{complete} = \top$. Note that \emph{default negation} in Assumption \ref{assumption - default negation} is satisfied if and only if the system $\textbf{CS}$ applies default negation.
    \label{language - maximum entropy causal systems}
\end{Language}

\begin{example}
    Recall the causal theory $\Theta$ from Example~\ref{example - acyclic weighted causal theory}. The scenario in Examples~\ref{example - Bayesian network} and \ref{example - introduction - sprinkler} give rise to the maximum entropy causal system
    \[
    \textbf{CS} := \left( \Theta \setminus \{ (w_1, \top \Rightarrow \textit{cloudy}) \}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O}, \Sigma, \texttt{complete} \right).
    \]

    Here, the external premises are given by  
    \[
    \mathcal{E} := \{ \textit{cloudy},~\neg \textit{cloudy},~ \neg \textit{rain},~ \neg \textit{sprinkler},~ \neg \textit{wet},~ \neg \textit{slippery} \}.
    \]
    There are no observations, i.e., $\mathcal{O} = \emptyset$, and the superordinate science gives rise to the LogLinear model:
    \[
    \Sigma := \{ (w_1, \textit{cloudy}) \}.
    \]
    If we are convinced that no causal relation has been omitted in modeling this scenario, we set $\texttt{complete} := \top$; otherwise, we set $\texttt{complete} := \bot$.

    % The system $\textbf{CS}$ gives rise to two situations 
    % $$
    % \textbf{s} := \{ (\neg) \textit{cloudy},~ \neg \textit{rain},~ \neg \textit{sprinkler},~ \neg \textit{wet},~ \neg \textit{slippery} \}
    % .$$ 

    Note that we have avoided the expression $\top \Rightarrow \textit{cloudy}$, which requires additional justification, as discussed in Remark~\ref{remark - metaphysical justification}.
    \label{example - acyclic weighted causal system}
\end{example}


Languages \ref{language - Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning}, \ref{language - maximum entropy causal systems} and Formalization \ref{formalization - common cause assumption} motivate the following definition:

\begin{definition}[Explainability and A Priori Distribution]
    Choose a maximum entropy causal system $\textbf{CS} := (\Theta,~\mathcal{E},~\mathcal{O},~\Sigma,~\texttt{complete})$.

    A formula~$\phi$ is \textbf{explainable} in a world $\omega$, written ${\omega \models \explains (\phi)}$, if it is explainable with the explanatory content $\EXP (\textbf{CS})$. A world $\omega$ is \textbf{explainable} with~$\textbf{CS}$ if it is explainable with the explanatory content~${\EXP (\textbf{CS})}$, that is, if every literal $l \in \omega$ is explainable in $\omega$. We define the event that $\textbf{CS}$ is \textbf{(causally) sufficient} as the set of all explainable worlds, i.e.,
    $$
    \sufficient (\textbf{CS}) := \{ \omega \text{ world: } \omega \models \explains (l) \text{ for all literals } l \}.
    $$

    If $\textbf{CS}$ is incomplete, the \textbf{a priori distribution} $\pi_{\textbf{CS}} := \pi_{\Phi}$ of $\textbf{CS}$ is given by the LogLinear model $\Phi :=\constraint(\textbf{CS}) \cup \Sigma$. Otherwise, the \textbf{a priori distribution}~$\pi_{\textbf{CS}}$ of~$\textbf{CS}$ is defined for every world $\omega$ as:
    $$
    \pi_{\textbf{CS}} (\omega) := \pi_{\Theta} (\omega \mid \omega\cap \mathcal{E}) \cdot \pi_{\Sigma} (\omega \cap \mathcal{E} \mid \textbf{D}(\textbf{CS})),
    $$
    where $\pi_{\Theta}$ denotes the common cause semantics of $\Theta$, and $\textbf{D}(\textbf{CS})$ denotes the external domain of the system $\textbf{CS}$.
    \label{definition - Explainability and A Priori Distribution}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
    For the causal system $\textbf{CS}$ in Example~\ref{example - acyclic weighted causal system}, we find that every world is explainable, i.e., $\sufficient (\textbf{CS})$ is the set of all worlds. Furthermore, if~${\texttt{complete} = \top}$, the a priori distribution $\pi_{\textbf{CS}}$ is given by the Bayesian network in Example~\ref{example - Bayesian network}.
    \label{example - Explainability and A Priori Distribution}
\end{example}



Let $\textbf{CS} := (\Theta,~\mathcal{E},~\mathcal{O},~\Sigma,~\texttt{complete})$ be a maximum entropy causal system, and let $\omega$ be a world. According to Languages~\ref{language - Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning} and~\ref{language - maximum entropy causal systems}, the probability~$\pi_{\textbf{CS}} (\omega)$ represents our degree of belief that \textit{natural necessity}, as stated in Principle~\ref{principle - Aquinas}, is satisfied in $\omega$. Furthermore, \textit{sufficient causation}, as formulated in Assumption~\ref{principle - Leibniz}, holds in $\omega$ if and only if $\omega \in \sufficient (\textbf{CS})$. These observations motivate the following definition:



\begin{definition}[Semantics of Maximum Entropy Causal Systems]
Let 
$$
\textbf{CS} := (\Theta,~\mathcal{E},~\mathcal{O},~\Sigma,~\texttt{complete})
$$ 
be a maximum entropy causal system with a priori distribution $\pi_{\textbf{CS}}$. 

The causal system $\textbf{CS}$ assumes \textbf{knowledge-\textit{that}} about a formula $\phi$ with probability: 
$$
{\pi}_{\textbf{CS}}^{\textit{that}} (\phi) := \pi_{\textbf{CS}}(\phi \vert \mathcal{O}).
$$

If the events $\phi$ and $\mathcal{O}$ are independent with respect to the conditional distribution $\pi_{\textbf{CS}}(\_ \vert \sufficient(\textbf{CS}))$, the causal system~$\textbf{CS}$ assumes \textbf{knowledge-\textit{why}} about $\phi$ with probability: 
$$
\pi_{\textbf{CS}}^{\textit{why}} (\phi) := \pi_{\textbf{CS}}(\phi \vert \mathcal{O}, \sufficient (\textbf{CS})) = \pi_{\textbf{CS}}(\phi \vert \sufficient (\textbf{CS}))
$$
and \textbf{confidence} $\pi (\sufficient(\textbf{CS}) \vert \mathcal{O})$.
\label{definition - semantics of maximum entropy causal systems}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
    According to Languages~\ref{language - Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning} and \ref{language - maximum entropy causal systems}, $\pi (\sufficient(\textbf{CS}) \vert \mathcal{O})$ represents the degree of belief that \textit{sufficient causation}, as stated in Assumption~\ref{principle - Leibniz}, applies, given that \textit{natural necessity}, as described in Principle~\ref{principle - Aquinas}, holds and the evidence $\mathcal{O}$ is observed. We therefore interpret $\pi (\sufficient(\textbf{CS}) \vert \mathcal{O})$ as an epistemic probability in the definition of knowledge-\textit{why}.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
    According to Example \ref{example - Explainability and A Priori Distribution}, the causal system in Example \ref{example - acyclic weighted causal system} assumes knowledge-\textit{why} with confidence one whenever it assumes \mbox{knowledge-\textit{that}}.
    \label{example - semantics of maximum entropy causal systems}
\end{example}

To summarize, we argue for the following result:

\begin{formalization}
    Upon committing to Principles \ref{principle - causal rules}, \ref{principle - maximum entropy}, Languages \ref{language - propositional logic}, \ref{language - causal reasoning}, \ref{formalization - causal theories} and Parametrization \ref{parametrization - maximum entropy model}, we model an area of science with a maximum entropy causal system, as described in Languages \ref{language - Uncertainty in Causal Reasoning} and \ref{language - maximum entropy causal systems}. In this case, \textit{causal foundation} in Principle \ref{principle - causal foundation}, \textit{natural necessity} in Principle \ref{principle - Aquinas}, and \textit{sufficient causation} in Assumption \ref{principle - Leibniz} yield that the system $\textbf{CS}$ possesses knowledge-\emph{that} and \mbox{knowledge-\emph{why}}, as described in Definition \ref{definition - semantics of maximum entropy causal systems}.
    \label{formalization - semantics of maximum entropy causal systems}
\end{formalization}


\subsection{Interpreting Current Artificial Intelligence Technologies as Causal Systems}
\label{subsec: Interpreting Current Artificial Intelligence Technologies as Causal Systems}

This section embeds LogLinear models~\mbox{\citep{MLN}}, as well as Bayesian networks and probabilistic causal models~\citep{Causality}, into the framework of causal systems. This enables the application of Language~\ref{language - maximum entropy causal systems} and Formalization~\ref{formalization - semantics of maximum entropy causal systems} to assess the type of knowledge captured by these formalisms and to extend the treatment of external interventions.




\subsubsection{Pearl's Probabilistic Causal Models}

Maximum entropy causal systems without observations, which apply default negation, can express the probabilistic causal models of \mbox{\cite{Causality}}:


\begin{definition}[Bochman Transformation]
    The \textbf{Bochman transformation} of a probabilistic causal model $\mathbb{M} := (\mathcal{M}, \pi)$ with $\mathcal{M} :=  (\textbf{U}, \textbf{V}, \Error, \Pa, \textbf{F})$ is the causal system~${\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M}) := (\Theta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset, \Sigma, \top)}$, defined as follows:
    \begin{align*}
        & \Theta := \{ (+ \infty, F_V \Rightarrow V) \mid V \in \textbf{V} \}, &&
        \mathcal{E} := \textbf{U} \cup \{ \neg V \mid V \in \textbf{U} \cup \textbf{V} \}\\
        & \Sigma := \{ (\ln(\pi(\textbf{s})),~\textbf{s}) \text{: } \textbf{s}~\text{situation of } \mathcal{M} \}
    \end{align*}
    Here, we identify a situation $\textbf{s}$ of $\mathcal{M}$ with the formula $\displaystyle \bigwedge_{\substack{l \text{ literal} \\ \textbf{s} \models l}} l$.

    %A causal system $\textbf{CS}$ is a \textbf{Pearl causal system} if the deterministic causal system $\explanatory(\textbf{CS)}$ is.

    %Let $\textbf{CS} := (\Theta, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O}, \Sigma, \texttt{complete})$ be a Pearl causal system. An atom $p \in \mathfrak{P}$ is called an \textbf{external variable} of $\textbf{CS}$ if it is an external variable of $\explanatory(\textbf{CS)}$. Otherwise, $p$ is an \textbf{internal variable}. 
    \label{definition - Bochman transformation of probabilistic causal models}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
   Let $\mathbb{M} := (\mathcal{M},\pi)$ be as in Example~\ref{example - introduction - sprinkler}. The Bochman transformation $\textbf{CS} (\mathbb{M}) := (\Theta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset, \Sigma, \top)$ is given by the following data:
\begin{align*}
&\Theta := && \{ (+ \infty,~\textit{cloudy} \land u_2 \Rightarrow \textit{rain}),  \\
&&&  (+\infty,~( \textit{cloudy}  \land u_3) \lor (\neg \textit{cloudy} \land u_4) \Rightarrow \textit{sprinkler}),  \\
&&&  (+ \infty,~( \textit{rain}  \lor \textit{sprinkler}) \land u_5 \Rightarrow \textit{wet}),   \\
&&&    (+ \infty,~\textit{wet}   \land u_6 \Rightarrow \textit{slippery})\} \\
& \mathcal{E} := && \{ u_1, \neg u_1,...,u_6, \neg u_6, \neg \textit{cloudy},..., \neg \textit{sprinkler}  \} \\
& \Sigma := && \{ (\ln(0.5 \cdot 0.6 \cdot 0.1  \cdot 0.9 \cdot 0.8), u_1 \land ... \land u_6 ) \\  
&&& ,..., \\
&&&(\ln(0.5 \cdot 0.4 \cdot 0.9  \cdot 0.1 \cdot 0.2), \neg u_1 \land ... \land \neg u_6 ) \}
\end{align*}


\label{example - Bochman transformation of probabilistic causal models}
\end{example}

The knowledge-\textit{why} $\pi_{\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M})}^{\textit{why}}$ of the Bochman transformation~$\textbf{CS} (\mathbb{M})$ of an acyclic causal model $\mathbb{M}$ corresponds to the distribution $\pi_{\mathbb{M}}$ associated with $\mathbb{M}$.

\begin{theorem}
    Let $\mathbb{M}$ be an acyclic probabilistic causal model with Bochman transformation $\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M})$. For every formula $\phi$, the causal system $\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M})$ assumes the knowledge-\emph{why} 
    $
    \pi_{\textbf{CS}}^{\textit{why}} (\phi) = \pi_{\mathbb{M}}(\phi)
    $
    with confidence one. 
    \label{theorem - Bochman transformation for deterministic causal models}
\end{theorem}


\begin{proof}
    Since the system $\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M})$ is without observations, it follows that it assumes knowledge-\textit{why} whenever it assumes knowledge-\textit{that}.  
    By Theorems \ref{theorem - completion of a causal theory} and \ref{theorem - causal models and Bayesian networks}, every world $\omega$ with $\pi_{\mathbb{M}}(\omega) > 0$ is a causal world of $\explanatory(\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M}))$.  
    Finally, by Theorem \ref{proposition - splitting in logical and explanatory content under causal sufficiency}, we conclude that  
    $
        \pi (\sufficient (\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M}))) = 1.
    $
\end{proof}


\subsection{External Interventions in Maximum Entropy Causal Systems}
\label{subsubsec: External Interventions in Maximum Entropy Causal Systems}

Similar to Section~\ref{subsubsec: External Interventions in Causal Systems}, we introduce the following notion of intervention in maximum entropy causal systems.


\begin{definition}[Modified Causal Systems]
    Let $\textbf{CS} := (\Theta, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O}, \Sigma, \texttt{complete})$ be a causal system, and let $\textbf{i}$ be a value assignment on a set of atoms $\textbf{I} \subseteq \mathfrak{P}$. To represent the intervention of forcing the atoms in $\textbf{I}$ to attain values according to the assignment $\textbf{i}$, we construct the \textbf{modified causal system}
    $$
    \textbf{CS}_{\textbf{i}} := (\Theta_{\textbf{i}}, \mathcal{E}_{\textbf{i}}, \mathcal{O}, \Sigma, \texttt{complete}),
    $$
    which is obtained from $\textbf{CS}$ by applying the following modifications:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Remove all rules $(w, \phi \Rightarrow p) \in \Theta$ and $(w, \phi \Rightarrow \neg p) \in \Theta$ for all~${p \in \textbf{I}}$.
        \item Remove all external premises $p \in \mathcal{E}$ and $\neg p \in \mathcal{E}$ for all~${p \in \textbf{I}}$.
        \item Add a  weighted rule $(+\infty, \top \Rightarrow l)$ to $\Theta_{\textbf{i}}$ for all literals ${l \in \textbf{I}}$.
    \end{itemize} 
    \label{definition - modified maximum entropy causal systems}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
    Definition~\ref{definition - modified maximum entropy causal systems} does not modify the superordinate science $\Sigma$, because LogLinear models are not modular with respect to the removal of weighted constraints. Hence, modifying the superordinate science $\Sigma$ would conflict with Assumption \ref{assumption - causal independence}.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
    Recall the causal system $\textbf{CS} := (\Theta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset, \Sigma, \texttt{complete})$ 
    from Example~\ref{example - acyclic weighted causal system}.
    Suppose we intervene by switching the sprinkler off, i.e., we apply the intervention~${\textbf{i} := \{ \neg \textit{sprinkler} \}}$. 

    The modified system~${\textbf{CS}_{\textbf{i}} := (\Theta_{\textbf{i}}, \mathcal{E}_{\textbf{i}}, \emptyset, \Sigma, \texttt{complete})}$ is then given by the following data:  
    \begin{align*}
            & \Theta_{\textbf{i}} := (\Theta \setminus \{ (w_{3/4}, (\neg) \textit{cloudy} \Rightarrow \textit{sprinkler}) \}) \cup \{ (+ \infty,~\top \Rightarrow \neg \textit{sprinkler})\}, \\
            & \mathcal{E}_{\textbf{i}} := \{ \textit{cloudy},~ \neg \textit{cloudy},~\neg \textit{rain},~\neg \textit{wet},~\neg \textit{slippery} \}.
    \end{align*}
    \label{example - intervention in a maximum entropy causal system}
\end{example}

Once again, for acyclic probabilistic causal models, the concept of intervention defined in Definition~\ref{definition - modified maximum entropy causal systems} is consistent with the Bochman transformation in Definition~\ref{definition - Bochman transformation of probabilistic causal models}.

\begin{proposition}
    Let $\mathbb{M} := (\mathcal{M}, \pi)$ be an acyclic probabilistic causal model and let $\textbf{i}$ be a truth value assignment on the internal variables ${\textbf{I} \subseteq \textbf{V}}$. 

    The causal systems~${\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M}_{\textbf{i}})}$ and $\textbf{CS} (\mathbb{M})_{\textbf{i}}$ assume the same knowledge-\emph{why}, i.e.,~${
    \pi_{\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M}_{\textbf{i}})}^{\textit{why}} (\omega) = \pi_{\textbf{CS} (\mathbb{M})_{\textbf{i}}}^{\textit{why}} (\omega)
    }$ for every world  $\omega$.
    \label{proposition - maximum entropy Bochman transformation is well-defined}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
    Let $\omega$ be a world. According to Proposition \ref{proposition - deterministic Bochman transformation is well-defined}, the deterministic causal systems $\explanatory(\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M}_{\textbf{i}}))$ and $\explanatory(\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M})_{\textbf{i}}) = \explanatory(\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M}))_{\textbf{i}}$ have the same causal worlds. Since all rules in the causal systems under consideration have weight $+\infty$, Proposition \ref{proposition - splitting in logical and explanatory content under causal sufficiency} implies that $\pi_{\textbf{CS}(\mathbb{M}_{\textbf{i}})}^{\textit{why}} (\omega) > 0$ if and only if $\pi_{\textbf{CS} (\mathbb{M})_{\textbf{i}}}^{\textit{why}} (\omega) > 0$, and in that case, $\omega$ is a causal world of the aforementioned deterministic causal systems. In particular, the probability of $\omega$ is uniquely determined by the corresponding situation, which is calculated from the same LogLinear model in all causal systems under consideration.
\end{proof}

Assumption \ref{assumption - feasibility of external interventions} then motivates the following definition:

\begin{definition}[Semantics of External Interventions]
    Let $\textbf{CS}$ be a causal system, and let $\textbf{i}$ be a value assignment on a set of atoms~${\textbf{I} \subseteq \mathfrak{P}}$, leading to the modified causal system~$\textbf{CS}_{\textbf{i}}$. We say that $\textbf{CS}$ \textbf{assumes} that a formula $\phi$ is true \textbf{after intervening} according to $\textbf{i}$ with probability $\pi_{\textbf{CS}} (\phi \vert \Do (\textbf{i})) \in [0,1]$,
    if and only if 
    $
    \pi_{\textbf{CS}} (\phi \vert \Do (\textbf{i})) = \pi_{\textbf{CS}_{\textbf{i}}}^{\textit{why}} (\phi) 
    $.
    \label{definition - semantics of external interventions in maximum entroy causal systems}
\end{definition}  

Relying on Assumption \ref{assumption - causal independence}, we argue for the following result.

\begin{formalization}
    Let us fix an area of science such that Formalization~\ref{formalization - semantics of maximum entropy causal systems} yields a causal system $\textbf{CS}$. Under these conditions and Assumptions \ref{assumption - feasibility of external interventions} and~\ref{assumption - causal independence}, Definitions~\ref{definition - modified maximum entropy causal systems} and~\ref{definition - semantics of external interventions in maximum entroy causal systems} correctly characterize the knowledge represented by~$\textbf{CS}$ regarding the effects of external interventions.  
    \label{formalization - external interventions deterministic}
\end{formalization}


\subsubsection{LogLinear Models}
We define the \textbf{Bochman interpretation} of a LogLinear model $\Phi$ as the maximum entropy causal system 
$$
\textbf{CS}(\Phi) := (\emptyset, \emptyset, \emptyset, \Phi, \bot). 
$$
It follows that $\pi_{\textbf{CS}(\Phi)}^{\textit{that}}(\omega) = \pi_{\Phi} (\omega)$ for all worlds $\omega$. Since $\sufficient(\textbf{CS}(\Phi)) = \emptyset$, the causal system ${\textbf{CS}(\Phi) := (\emptyset, \emptyset, \emptyset, \Phi, \bot)}$ does not possess knowledge-\textit{why}. 

Now, assume that we intervene according to a truth value assignment $\textbf{i}$ on the atoms $\textbf{I} \subseteq \mathfrak{P}$, yielding the modified system
$$
\textbf{CS}(\Phi)_{\textbf{i}} := (\{ \top \Rightarrow l \mid l \in \textbf{i} \}, \emptyset, \emptyset, \Phi, \bot). 
$$
Unless $\textbf{i}$ represents a world with $\textbf{I} = \mathfrak{P}$, we find that ${\sufficient(\textbf{CS}(\Phi)_{\textbf{i}}) = \emptyset}$, implying that $\textbf{CS}(\Phi)$ lacks knowledge about the effects of external intervention. Hence, we conclude that LogLinear models do not encode knowledge about the effects of external interventions. 

Interpreting every probability distribution as a LogLinear model, we conclude that probability distributions neither possess knowledge-\textit{why} nor knowledge of the effects of external interventions.



\subsubsection{Bayesian Networks}
To a Boolean Bayesian network $
{\textbf{BN} := (G, \pi (\_, \pa(\_)))}
$ we assign the \textbf{Bochman transformation}, which is  the causal system with default negation
$$
\textbf{CS}(\textbf{BN}) := (\Theta, \mathcal{E}, \emptyset, \Sigma, \top), 
$$
defined as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The weighted causal theory $\Theta$ consists of the weighted causal rules
    $$
    (w, \pa(p) \Rightarrow p)
    $$
    for every non-source node $p$ in $G$ and every truth value assignment $\pa(p)$ of its parents $\Pa(p) \neq \emptyset$, where $w$ is computed as in Equation~(\ref{equation - transforming parameters of Bayesian network}).
    \item The external premises are given by
    $$
    \mathcal{E} := \textbf{S} \cup \{ \neg p \mid p \in \mathfrak{P} \},
    $$
    where $\textbf{S}$ denotes the set of source nodes in the graph $G$.
    \item The superordinate science is given by
    $
    \Sigma := \{ (\sigma^{-1}(\pi (s)),~ s) \mid s \in \textbf{S} \}.
    $
\end{itemize}

We observe that every world $\omega$ is explainable with $\textbf{CS}(\textbf{BN})$. Moreover, a similar argument as in Theorem~\ref{theorem - weighted causal theories an Bayesian networks} shows that
$$
\pi_{\textbf{BN}} (\omega) = \pi_{\textbf{CS}(\textbf{BN})}^{\textit{why}} (\omega)
$$
for every world $\omega$. Hence, Bayesian networks encode \mbox{knowledge-\textit{why}} with confidence one.

\begin{example}
    The maximum entropy causal system in Example~\ref{example - acyclic weighted causal system} is the Bochman transformation of the Bayesian network in Example \ref{example - Bayesian network}.
\end{example}

Now, consider a Boolean Bayesian network $\textbf{BN} := (G,\pi(\_,\pa(\_)))$ on the variables $\mathfrak{P}$, and let $\textbf{i}$ be a truth value assignment on a subset of variables~${\textbf{I} \subseteq \mathfrak{P}}$. Intervene according to $\textbf{i}$ to obtain the Bayesian network ${\textbf{BN}_{\textbf{i}} := (G_{\textbf{I}},\pi_{\textbf{i}}(\_,\pa(\_)))}$ and the causal system
$
\textbf{CS}_{\textbf{i}} := \textbf{CS}(\textbf{BN})_{\textbf{i}} := (\Theta_{\textbf{i}}, \mathcal{E}_{\textbf{i}}, \emptyset, \Sigma, \top).
$

By definition, the distribution $\pi_{\textbf{CS}}^{\textit{why}}$ is Markov to the graph $\textbf{G}_{\textbf{I}}$. As in Theorem~\ref{theorem - weighted causal theories an Bayesian networks}, we can verify that 
$
\pi_{\textbf{CS}}^{\textit{why}}(p \mid \pa (p)) = \pi_{\textbf{BN}_{\textbf{i}}}(p \mid \pa (p))
$ 
for all~${p \in \mathfrak{P}}$. We conclude that the Bayesian network $\textbf{BN}$ and its Bochman transformation~$\textbf{CS}(\textbf{BN})$ predict the same effects of external interventions, i.e., 
$$
\pi_{\textbf{BN}}(\_ \mid \Do(\textbf{i})) = \pi_{\textbf{CS}(\textbf{BN})}(\_ \mid \Do(\textbf{i})).
$$



\section{Conclusion}
This paper introduces causal systems as a formal framework for distinguishing between knowledge-\textit{that} and \mbox{knowledge-\textit{why}}, as defined in Aristotle's \textit{Posterior Analytics}. It argues that external interventions can be meaningfully treated only on the basis of knowledge-\textit{why}. Embedding existing artificial intelligence technologies into the formalism of causal systems enables a classification of the type of knowledge they provide and an assessment of the feasibility of handling external interventions.

This work embeds LogLinear models \citep{MLN}, as well as Bayesian networks and causal models \citep{Causality}, into the framework of causal systems. In future work, it is envisaged to interpret abductive logic programs \citep{AbductiveLogicPrograms} as deterministic causal systems, while ProbLog programs \citep{ProbLog, Problog1}, along with $\LP^{\MLN}$ programs \citep{LPMLN}, will be analyzed as maximum entropy causal systems.

We further propose extending maximum entropy causal systems to the context of first-order logic. We conjecture that the resulting theory will be expressive enough to encompass probabilistic logic programming \citep{PLP}, Markov logic networks \citep{MLN}, and relational Bayesian networks \citep{Jaeger97}. This would establish a unifying framework for \textit{relational artificial intelligence} \citep{StarAI}, interpreting it as the study of formalisms that capture the fundamental concepts of symmetry, uncertainty, and causal explanation.

According to \cite{Causality}, causal models can answer counterfactual queries, whereas Bayesian networks cannot. As a direction for future research, we propose characterizing the additional knowledge captured in causal models that enables this type of query.




