    \item If one of the oriented points $L$, $B$, $R$ is oriented upwards, the dilation between $L_1$ and $L_2$ is smaller than $t' := 1.043$.
    \item If none of the oriented points $L$, $B$, $R$ is oriented upwards, the shortest closed walk containing $L_1$ and $L_2$ %leaves the ellipse and thus their dilation is greater than $1.043$.
either leaves the ellipse or takes at least to points from $\{L,B,R\}$ and thus their dilation is greater than $t'$.   
\end{itemize}

Therefore, formula $\varphi$ is satisfiable if and only if there exists an orientation of our constructed graph with dilation at most $1.043$.
