\subsection{Theoretical Properties}
We will show that MPAD satisfies some desired mathematical properties and why those properties are meaningful. Notice that MPAD is built upon the objective function, which is a signed measure, as
\begin{equation*}
    \phi\bigl(\{\boldsymbol{w}_k\}_{k=1}^m\bigr)=\sum_{k=1}^m\left[\mu_b(\boldsymbol{w}_k)-\alpha\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} \Bigl(\boldsymbol{w}_j\cdot \boldsymbol{w}_k\Bigr)^2\right],
\end{equation*}
where the utility function is defined as
\begin{equation*}
    \mu_b(\boldsymbol{w}_k)=\frac{1}{|D_{k,b}|}\sum_{d_{k,ij}\in D_{k,b}} d_{k,ij},\quad
    d_{k,ij}=\Bigl|\boldsymbol{w}_k\cdot(\boldsymbol{x}_i-\boldsymbol{x}_j)\Bigr|.
\end{equation*}
\subsubsection*{Objective Function $\phi$ being a Signed Measure}
Defining $\phi$ as a \textit{signed measure} is more than a mathematical formality—it provides a structured way to assess and decompose the quality of our basis vectors. First, the measure $\phi$ naturally splits into positive contributions, via $\mu_b(\boldsymbol{w}_k)$ (which quantifies the preservation of local neighborhood order), and negative contributions, via the orthogonality penalty. This decomposition allows us to \textbf{analyze how each basis vector {improves} or {degrades}} the overall preservation of the $k$-NN structure. Second, through the property of countable additivity (detailed below), we can \textbf{assess the effect of subsets of basis vectors independently}. This modularity is valuable when designing algorithms, as it \textbf{enables parallel optimization and local performance analysis} that aggregates to a global measure. Besides, a measure-theoretic foundation ensures that every part of our objective is derived from well-defined mathematical principles. In this way, we \textbf{avoid arbitrary choices} in the design of the method, thereby ensuring \textbf{consistency and reproducibility}.

\subsubsection*{Other Mathematical Properties: Boundedness, Compactness, Continuity, and Monotonicity}

These mathematical properties are not merely theoretical conveniences—they are essential to ensuring the practical reliability of our proposed dimensionality reduction algorithm. \emph{Boundedness} guarantees that the optimization objective remains \textbf{well-defined} and \textbf{numerically stable across iterations}. \emph{Compactness} ensures the \textbf{existence of optima}, preventing divergence or undefined behavior. \emph{Continuity} \textbf{allows gradient-based or continuous optimization strategies} to be applicable, as small changes in the parameters yield predictable changes in the objective. Finally, \emph{monotonicity} provides a formal assurance that the algorithm will improve or maintain performance at every step, thereby \textbf{ensuring convergence}. Together, these properties lay a robust theoretical foundation for the correctness and stability of the approach. Below we elaborate on these key properties and provide supporting equations and reasoning.


\subsubsection*{Proofs of the above properties}


\begin{proof}[Proof of Boundedness.]
    Each basis vector $\boldsymbol{w}_k$ is constrained to lie on the unit sphere in $\mathbb{R}^n$, i.e., $\|\boldsymbol{w}_k\|=1$. For any two vectors $\boldsymbol{x}_i,\boldsymbol{x}_j \in X$, by the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,
    \begin{equation*}
        \Bigl|\boldsymbol{w}_k\cdot(\boldsymbol{x}_i-\boldsymbol{x}_j)\Bigr|\le \|\boldsymbol{w}_k\| \cdot \|\boldsymbol{x}_i-\boldsymbol{x}_j\| = \|\boldsymbol{x}_i-\boldsymbol{x}_j\|.
    \end{equation*}

    Let $D_{max}=\max_{i,j}\|\boldsymbol{x}_i-\boldsymbol{x}_j\|$.

    Then, for every $\boldsymbol{w}_k$, we have $\mu_b(\boldsymbol{w}_k)\le D_{max}$. Similarly, since $\Bigl(\boldsymbol{w}_j\cdot \boldsymbol{w}_k\Bigr)^2\le 1$, the penalty term satisfies
    \begin{equation*}
    P_{\text{orth},k}=\alpha\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\Bigl(\boldsymbol{w}_j\cdot \boldsymbol{w}_k\Bigr)^2\le \alpha(k-1).
    \end{equation*}

    Thus, for $m$ basis vectors,
    \begin{equation*}
    \phi\bigl(\{\boldsymbol{w}_k\}_{k=1}^m\bigr) \le mD_{max} - \alpha\sum_{k=1}^m (k-1)= mD_{max} - \alpha\frac{m(m-1)}{2}.
    \end{equation*}

    This upper bound (and a corresponding lower bound) ensures that $\phi$ is bounded.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Compactness.]
    The set of candidate basis vectors is the unit sphere \( S^{n-1} \), which is compact in \( \mathbb{R}^n \). Since continuous functions on compact sets are bounded and attain their extrema, if the operations defining $\phi$ were entirely continuous, then $\phi$ would be guaranteed to have a maximum and minimum. Although the sorting operation in the definition of $\mu_b$ can introduce discontinuities, these occur only at isolated points (a measure-zero set), thus preserving the overall compact behavior of the domain.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Continuity.]

    Consider the projection function   $
    f_{\boldsymbol{w}}(\boldsymbol{x})=\langle \boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{w}\rangle$.

    This function is continuous in \(\boldsymbol{w}\) because the dot product is linear. The absolute value function and finite sums are also continuous. Hence, for almost every \(\boldsymbol{w}_k\),
    \begin{equation*}
    \mu_b(\boldsymbol{w}_k)=\frac{1}{|D_{k,b}|}\sum_{d_{k,ij}\in D_{k,b}} \Bigl|\langle \boldsymbol{x}_i-\boldsymbol{x}_j,\boldsymbol{w}_k\rangle\Bigr|
    \end{equation*}

    is continuous except at those critical points where the order of pairwise distances changes. The penalty term,
    \begin{equation*}
    P_{\text{orth},k}=\alpha\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\Bigl(\boldsymbol{w}_j\cdot \boldsymbol{w}_k\Bigr)^2,
    \end{equation*}

    is a polynomial function in the entries of \(\boldsymbol{w}_k\) and is therefore continuous. Overall, the function \(\phi\) is (almost everywhere) continuous on the compact domain.
\end{proof}


\begin{proof}[Proof of Monotonicity.]
    In our algorithm, each update step for \(\boldsymbol{w}_k\) is performed with the goal of increasing the objective
    \begin{equation*}
    J(\boldsymbol{w}_k)=\mu_b(\boldsymbol{w}_k)-P_{\text{orth},k}.
    \end{equation*}

    Thus, at each iteration \(t\), we have $\phi^{(t+1)} \geq \phi^{(t)}$.

    Since \(\phi\) is bounded above (as shown under Boundedness), the monotonic sequence \(\{\phi^{(t)}\}\) must converge. This monotonicity is crucial to guarantee algorithmic stability and convergence in practice.

\end{proof}


We then show that $\phi$ behaves like a signed measure.
\begin{proof}[Proof of Signed Measure Properties]

It is sufficient to prove that: 

\textbf{Null Empty Set.} For an empty collection of basis vectors, the convention for empty sums gives
\begin{equation*}
    \phi(\emptyset)=\sum_{k\in\emptyset}\left[\mu_b(\boldsymbol{w}_k)-\alpha\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\Bigl(\boldsymbol{w}_j\cdot \boldsymbol{w}_k\Bigr)^2\right]=0.
\end{equation*}

\textbf{Countable Additivity.} Let $B=\{\boldsymbol{w}_i\}_{i\in I}$ be a countable set of basis vectors. Suppose we partition $B$ into disjoint subsets $\{B_k\}_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
B=\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{N}}B_k,\quad B_k\cap B_\ell=\emptyset \text{ for } k\neq\ell.
\end{equation*}

For each subset $B_k$, define
\begin{equation*}
\phi(B_k)=\sum_{\boldsymbol{w}\in B_k}\mu_b(\boldsymbol{w})-\alpha\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{w},\,\boldsymbol{v}\in B_k\\ \text{$\boldsymbol{v}$ precedes $\boldsymbol{w}$}}}\Bigl(\boldsymbol{v}\cdot \boldsymbol{w}\Bigr)^2.
\end{equation*}

By choosing an ordering where vectors in each $B_k$ appear consecutively, we have
\begin{equation*}
\phi\Bigl(\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{N}}B_k\Bigr)=\sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\phi(B_k).
\end{equation*}

This confirms the countable additivity property of $\phi$.
\end{proof}

\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

In this section, we assess the performance of our proposed MPAD method against several established dimensionality reduction techniques on datasets in various areas.


\subsection{Datasets and Setup}

\subsubsection*{Dataset} We evaluate our method on four diverse datasets spanning different domains: Fasttext~\cite{bojanowski2016enriching,joulin2016bag,joulin2016fasttext} (text), Isolet~\cite{isolet_54} (voice), Arcene~\cite{arcene_167} (human health$/$mass-spectrometry), and PBMC3k~\cite{SatijaRahul2015Sros} (biology$/$genomics). The Fasttext dataset consists of pre-trained word vectors; in our experiments, we use the \texttt{wiki-news-300d-1M} model, which comprises one million vectors trained on Wikipedia 2017, the UMBC webbase corpus, and the statmt.org news dataset (16B tokens). The Isolet dataset includes recordings from 150 subjects, each pronouncing the name of every letter of the alphabet twice. We use the designated training set (isolet1+2+3+4), making it well-suited for noisy, perceptual tasks and for testing the scaling abilities of various algorithms. The Arcene dataset, created for the NIPS 2003 feature selection challenge, comprises mass-spectrometry data used to distinguish between cancerous and healthy patients; we employ its training, validation, and test splits. The PBMC3k dataset contains single-cell RNA sequencing data from approximately 3,000 peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) from a healthy human donor. It is commonly used to benchmark and investigate immune cell types and gene expression patterns in scRNA-seq analyses. For this dataset, we utilize the non-empty points from the \texttt{pbmc3k\_processed} data provided by Scanpy. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} summarizes the key characteristics of these four datasets.

