    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{images/gst_alg.png}
    \caption{Evolution of a single view in our partially synchronous Byzantine Agreement protocol, assuming an honest leader L and that the system is after GST.}
    \label{fig:gst_alg}
\end{figure}

This algorithm, to which we refer as \textsc{ByzantineAgreementGST}, solves Byzantine Agreement in partial synchrony:

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{GSTAlgoProp}
Algorithm \textsc{ByzantineAgreementGST} achieves Byzantine Agreement in partial synchrony while tolerating up to $t < n/3$ byzantine failures. Moreover, it uses $\mathcal{O}(n + n\cdot f)$ messages in the worst case.
\end{restatable}

We give a proof for this theorem as well as auxiliary statements in Appendix \ref{sec:apx:gst alg proofs}.

\section{Broadcasting from a Quorum}
\label{sec:qab}
To decrease the message complexity, we will rely on a primitive which we call Quorum-to-All Broadcast (abbreviated QAB). Briefly, this primitive allows a small group (of size $\Theta(t)$) of parties all having the same input value to broadcast it to everyone. The advantage of considering QAB is that it can be quite efficiently optimized, both for message and round complexity, but can also be used as a building block to achieve agreement with optimal complexity when $t$ is small.

The formal definition of the primitive goes as follows. A QAB instance is defined with respect to a group of dedicated nodes called \emph{quorum}. All honest nodes in the quorum start an instance with a value $\vin$ (common among all quorum nodes). We assume that all the honest nodes in the system possess a predicate $\mathrm{verify}(\cdot)$ such that $\mathrm{verify}(v)$ is true if and only if $v = \vin$. QAB exposes the following interface:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $Initiate()$ - start the protocol as a non-quorum node.
    \item $Initiate(\vin)$ - start the protocol as a quorum node. 
    \item $Decide() \rightarrow v$ - decide value $v$.
\end{itemize}

QAB is defined by the following property that encapsulates classical Agreement, Termination, and Validity. 
\begin{property}[Complete Correctness]
    Every honest node eventually decides $v_{in}$.
\end{property}

We provide algorithms that solve QAB in asynchrony and partial synchrony in Sections \ref{sec:qab async} and \ref{sec:qab partial sync}, respectively. 

\subsection{QAB in Asynchrony}
\label{sec:qab async}
In this section, we present an algorithm that solves QAB sending $\mathcal{O}((n + t^2)\cdot \log n)$ words in an asynchronous setting.

\begin{theorem}
    \label{thm:qab async}
    For a Quorum of size $3t + 1$, there exists a deterministic algorithm that, given a PKI, solves QAB in asynchrony in the presence of at most $t$ Byzantine faults with communication complexity of $\mathcal{O}((n + t^2)\cdot \log n)$. 
\end{theorem}

We remark that for simplicity of presentation, we fix the quorum size to be $3t + 1$, though the theorem holds as well for any quorum size $q$ as long as $t < q = \mathcal{O}(t)$.

In order to achieve the stated communication complexity, we restrict the communication between nodes to a specific graph, which we call a communication graph. If there is an edge between two nodes in the communication graph, we say that these two nodes are \emph{linked}. There are three roles in the algorithm (one node can have multiple roles): \textit{quorum nodes} - nodes that have a common value to broadcast, \textit{relayers} - nodes that help quorum nodes disseminate the value and \textit{parties} - all the nodes, those that need to learn the value.

The high-level description of our 3-stage algorithm is as follows. The pseudocode for an algorithm can be found in Appendix \ref{sec:apx:async qab}.
\begin{enumerate} 
    \item \textbf{Quorum-Relayers-Parties.} Each quorum node broadcasts $\vin$ to a group of $\mathcal{O}(t\log n)$ \textit{relayers}. Upon receiving and verifying $\vin$, each relayer then broadcasts it among the parties linked to them. 
    \item \textbf{Parties-Relayers-Quorum.} We call the set of parties linked to a given relayer a \emph{committee}. Every committee has an aggregation signature setup. After receiving and verifying $\vin$, each party signs the message saying that it knows $\vin$ and sends the signature back to the relayer. After receiving a valid signature from \emph{every} party linked to them, a relayer aggregates signatures into a single one and sends it to all the quorum nodes. 
    \item \textbf{Quorum-Undecided.} Once a quorum node receives an aggregated signature for a committee of a relayer, we say that it has a proof that all the nodes from this committee know the value. Each quorum node waits until it has a proof that at least $n - 2t$ parties know $\vin$ and sends $\vin$ to the rest directly. \\
    \item[] \textbf{Decision.} Parties decide $\vin$ once they have received and verified it. 
\end{enumerate}


\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{images/communication_graph.png}
    \caption{The communication graph for stages 1 and 2 of the algorithm. Nodes are assigned three roles (non-exclusive): \textit{parties}, \textit{relayers} and \textit{quorum} with respective sizes of $n$, $O(t\log n)$ and $3t + 1$. Each party is only linked to $O(\log n)$ relayers, and each relayer is linked to each quorum node.}
    \label{fig:communication graph}
\end{figure}

The crux of our result is the utilization of a bipartite (\textit{parties, relayers}) graph that (I) has few edges (II) an adversary can not disconnect many parties from the quorum. Theorem \ref{thm:bipartite expander} states formal properties of this graph. The proof can be found, for instance, in notes by Svenson \cite{svensson2021bipartite}.


\begin{theorem}[\cite{svensson2021bipartite}]
    \label{thm:bipartite expander}
    There exists a bipartite graph $(A, B)$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $|A| = n$,
        \item Every vertex in $A$ has same degree $D < c\cdot\log n$ for some constant $c$,
        \item $|B| = 8c\cdot t\log n$,
        \item For every $S \subset A$ such that $|S| > 2t$, $|N(S)| > t \cdot D$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

We are now ready to prove the existence of an efficient asynchronous algorithm for QAB.


\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:qab async}]
    We need to prove communication complexity, as well as Complete Correctness.

    \textit{Communication complexity.} In the first stage, $(3t + 1) \cdot \mathcal{O}(t\cdot \log n)$ words are being sent by quorum nodes to relayers and $\mathcal{O}(n \log n)$ words by relayers to parties. In the second stage, parties send $\mathcal{O}(n \log n)$ words to relayers, and relayers send $\mathcal{O}(t\log n) \cdot (3t + 1)$ words to quorum nodes. Finally, in stage 3, $(3t + 1)\cdot 2t$ words are sent by quorum nodes to potentially undecided parties. 

    Now, we prove Complete Correctness, splitting it into \textit{Agreement} (every two honest nodes decide the same value), \textit{Termination} (every honest node eventually decides), and \textit{Validity} (if an honest party decides value $v$, then $v = \vin$). 

    \textit{Agreement \& Validity.} An honest party only decides a value $v$ if it passes the $\mathrm{verify}(v)$ check, and by definition of $\mathrm{verify}$, this implies that $v = \vin$.

    \textit{Termination.} Define a relayer to be \emph{blocked} if it is linked to at least one faulty node in parties. We call a party \emph{connected} if it is linked to the relayer that is not blocked and \emph{disconnected} otherwise.\footnote{As mentioned, \textit{nodes, relayers} and \textit{quorum} might not be exclusive roles. For consistency of presentation, if a node $u$ has both party and relayer roles, we say that party$_u$ is linked to relayer$_u$.} We need to show three statements: (I) At most $2t$ parties are disconnected (II) All connected parties will receive the value (and hence decide) in stage $1$ of the algorithm (III) Every quorum node will eventually receive a proof that every connected party knows the value and hence will send messages to all the disconnected parties, hence they will eventually decide.

    Let's show (I).  By property $2$ of Theorem \ref{thm:bipartite expander}, an adversary can block at most $t \cdot D$ relayers. Therefore, by property $4$, if we consider more than $2t$ parties, some of them will be connected.

    To show (II), note that if a party $u$ is linked to a relayer that is not blocked, this in particular means that this relayer is honest, and hence it will eventually send $u$ the value in stage $1$ of the algorithm. 

    Finally, to show (III), note that by definition of a connected party, it is linked to a relayer who is only linked to honest parties. Hence, this relayer will eventually get all the signatures in stage $2$ of the algorithm and will send an aggregated signature (proof that all the parties in its committee know the value) to all the quorum nodes. 
\end{proof}

We remark that the existence of a bipartite expander from Theorem \ref{thm:bipartite expander} is shown non-constructively in \cite{svensson2021bipartite}. Known explicit bipartite expanders \cite{guruswami2009unbalanced}, when utilized in our construction, would imply a message complexity of $(n + t^{2 + \varepsilon})\cdot \log^{\mathcal{O}(1/\varepsilon)}n$.

We are now ready to prove Theorem \ref{thm:async main}.
\mainThmAsync*
\begin{proof}
    We start by describing an algorithm that implements BA in asynchrony, and we show its correctness and message complexity. Our algorithm uses an asynchronous Byzantine Agreement from Cachin et al.~\cite{cachin2000random}, which we refer to as $C$, and asynchronous QAB. The algorithm goes as follows.

    First, run $C$ on the set $Q$ of $3t + 1$ dedicated parties, with each party proposing to $C$ their input in BA. Once a party from $Q$ decides in $C$, it proposes the decided value to the asynchronous QAB, and once a party decides $v$ in QAB, it decides $v$ in BA. 

    We prove the correctness of this protocol by first noting that all honest processes in the quorum $Q$ initiate QAB with the same value by the Agreement property of $C$. Now, let us prove Agreement, Termination, and Strong Unanimity of BA. 

    Termination and Agreement follow trivially from the Termination and Agreement of $C$ and QAB.

    To see why Strong Unanimity holds, note that if all honest processes have $v$ as their input in BA, all honest quorum nodes will propose $v$ to $C$, and hence, by the Strong Unanimity of $C$, $v$ will be decided in $C$, and hence in QAB, and hence in BA. 

    \textit{Communication complexity.} The communication complexity of the protocol consists of the communication complexity of $C$, which is $O(t^2)$ in expectation, and of the communication complexity of QAB, which is $\mathcal{O}((n + t^2)\cdot \log n)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{QAB in Partial Synchrony}
\label{sec:qab partial sync}
In this section, we present an algorithm that solves QAB sending $\mathcal{O}(n + tf)$ words after GST in the partially synchronous setting. 

\begin{theorem}
    \label{thm:qab gst}
    For a Quorum of any size $q$ such that $t < q$ and $q = \mathcal{O}(t)$, there exists a deterministic algorithm that can tolerate up to $t$ Byzantine faults and solves QAB in partial synchrony in the presence of at most $f$ actual faults with communication complexity of $\mathcal{O}(n + tf)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    The algorithm is view-based. We assume a predetermined and commonly known function $\mathrm{leader}(i)$ which tells the leader of a view $i$. The protocol goes as follows. 

    Once $\mathrm{leader}(i)$ enters a view $i$ \textit{and} it hasn't yet decided a value, it broadcasts a value request to all the quorum nodes. A quorum node replies to a value request \textit{once} per party. Once a leader gets $\vin$ in response, it verifies and broadcasts it among all parties. Once a party receives a broadcasted value, it verifies and decides it. 

    We now show the stated Message Complexity and Complete Correctness.

    \textit{Message Complexity.} Recall that we only count messages sent by honest processes after GST. After GST, there will be at most $f$ Byzantine leaders, which may trigger quorum nodes to send a message each, resulting in at most $(3t + 1)\cdot f$ words sent by honest nodes. Once there is an honest leader after GST, it will send $3t + 1$ requests, quorum nodes will issue at most $3t + 1$ responses, the leader will send $n$ messages broadcasting the value, and hence, given that all honest nodes stay in the view for at least $3\Delta$ time, all honest verifiers will receive the value and hence issue no more messages. 

    \textit{Complete Correctness.} Now, we prove Complete Correctness, splitting it into \textit{Agreement} (every two honest nodes decide the same value), \textit{Termination} (every honest node eventually decides), and \textit{Validity} (if an honest party decides value $v$, then $v = \vin$). 

    \textit{Agreement \& Validity.} An honest party only decides a value $v$ if it passes the $\mathrm{verify}(v)$ check, and by definition of $\mathrm{verify}$, this implies that $v = \vin$.

    \textit{Termination.} Eventually, there will be a view $i$ after GST with $\mathrm{leader}(i)$ being honest. So, as long as all honest parties stay in view $i$ for at least $3\Delta$ time, all honest parties will receive the value and hence will decide. 
\end{proof}

We are now ready to prove Theorems \ref{thm:gst main} and \ref{thm:sync main}.
\mainThmGST*
\begin{proof}
    An algorithm that implements BA in partial synchrony uses an adaptive partially synchronous BA from Section \ref{sec:agreement gst}, and a partially synchronous QAB with quorum size $3t + 1$. 

    The description of the protocol and correctness proof are identical to those presented in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:async main}; therefore, we only prove communication complexity.

    \textit{Communication complexity} of the algorithm consists of the communication complexity of the synchronous agreement from Section \ref{sec:agreement gst} and the communication complexity of QAB. The synchronous agreement from Section \ref{sec:agreement gst} invoked on the quorum of size $ 3t+1$ has a communication complexity of $\mathcal{O}(t + t\cdot f)$ whereas QAB adds an additional $\mathcal{O}(n + t\cdot f)$ term, which in total results in a communication complexity of $\mathcal{O}(n + t\cdot f)$.
\end{proof}


\mainThmSync*
\begin{proof}
    An algorithm that implements BA in synchrony uses an adaptive synchronous BA by Civit et al. \cite{PODC:CDGGKV24}, and a partially synchronous QAB with quorum size $2t + 1$. Apart from this change from $3t + 1$ to $2t + 1$, which is to achieve an optimal resilience in synchrony, the proof of correctness and message complexity is identical to the one for Theorem \ref{thm:gst main}.
\end{proof}


\section{Infeasibility in Asynchrony}
\label{sec:lb}
In this section, we prove the lower bound theorem which implies that no adaptive communication complexity is possible in asynchronous setting.

\thmLB*
\begin{proof}
    In this proof, by the \emph{setup} we mean a combination of identities of faulty parties, proposals of honest parties, behavior of faulty parties and the schedule of messages. Let $M$ be a random variable denoting the number of messages sent by the protocol.

    We will prove that there exists a setup \texttt{Main} in which no party is byzantine and $\mathbb{E}(M) \geq t^2/4$.

    \texttt{Main} is defined as follows. We partition the parties $\mathcal{P}$ into two sets $B$ and $C$ such that $|C| = t/2$ and $|B| = n - t/2$.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item All parties in $\mathcal{P}$ are honest and have input $0$.
        \item Messages between parties in $C$ are delayed until after everyone decides.
        \item For every party $p \in C$, the first $t/2$ messages sent to $p$ from processes in $B$ are delayed until after everyone decides.
        \item The other messages are delivered immediately.
    \end{itemize}

    We want to show that this scheduling is valid for the asynchronous model. I.e., we want to show that almost surely, all parties will decide before any of the delayed messages are received.

    We first prove so for every party in $B$. To do that, we consider the setup $S_B$ for which we'll show that (i) all processes in $B$ must decide and (ii) processes in $B$ can not distinguish $S_B$ from \texttt{Main}. $S_B$ is defined as follows:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item All parties in $B$ are honest and have input $0$.
        \item All parties in $C$ are byzantine. They act as if they were executing $\mathcal{A}$ with input $0$, but they ignore all messages from other parties in $C$ and the first $t/2$ messages from parties in $B$.
        \item All messages are immediately delivered.
    \end{itemize}
    Clearly, parties in $B$ cannot distinguish $S_B$ and \texttt{Main}. Moreover, in $S_B$, all messages are eventually delivered, and less than $t$ parties are byzantine. Therefore, $\mathcal{A}$ must ensure probabilistic termination, so parties in $B$ will eventually decide.

    Now, let us prove that for every party $p$ in $C$ it eventually decides in \texttt{Main}. To do so, we introduce a family of setups $\{S_Q\}$, for all $Q \subseteq B$ such that $|Q| = t/2$. $S_Q$ is defined as follows:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item All parties in $B \setminus Q$ are honest and have input $0$.
        \item $p$ is honest and has input $0$.
        \item All parties in $C \setminus \{p\}$ are byzantine. They act as if they were executing $\mathcal{A}$ with input $0$ but ignore messages from other parties in $C$ as well as the first $t/2$ messages from parties in $B$.
        \item All parties in $Q$ are byzantine. They act as if they were executing $\mathcal{A}$ with input $0$ but do not send the first $t/2$ messages to $p$.
        \item All messages are immediately delivered.
    \end{itemize}
    In setting $S_Q$, $t/2 + t/2 - 1 < t$ parties are byzantine and all messages are eventually delivered. Therefore, $A$ must ensure probabilistic termination so all honest parties, and in particular $p$, will eventually decide.

    For any execution of $\mathcal{A}$ in \texttt{Main}, there exists a $Q \subseteq B$ such that $|Q| = t/2$ and $p$ cannot distinguish between \texttt{Main} and $S_Q$. Since there is only a finite amount of such setups $S_Q$ and in any of these setups $p$ eventually decides, it implies that $p$ will eventually decide in \texttt{Main}. Therefore, \texttt{Main} is covered by protocol $\mathcal{A}$, so the behavior of all parties should satisfy probabilistic termination, agreement, and validity. Using validity and the fact that every party has input $0$, we obtain that every party will eventually decide $0$ in \texttt{Main}.

    We will now show that if for \texttt{Main} the expected number of messages sent is less than $t^2/4$, then some party in $C$ decides $0$ without receiving any message with a non-zero probability.

    Having $\mathbb{E}(M) < t^2/4$ and using Markov's inequality, we get that:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}(M \geq t^2/2) \leq 0.5
    \end{align*}
    For a party $p \in C$, let $E_p$ be the event that no more than $t/2$ messages are sent to $p$ from $B$. We remark that $\bigcap_{p\in C} \overline{E_p} \subset \{ M \geq t^2/2\}$. Using the previous inequality and by taking the complement, we get:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}\left(\bigcup_{p \in C} E_p \right) &\geq 0.5 
    \end{align*}
    and by Union bound
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{p \in C} \mathbb{P}(E_p) &\geq 0.5.
    \end{align*}
    Therefore, there exists $p \in C$ such that $\mathbb{P}(E_p) \geq 1/(2|C|) = 1/t$.

    Because the first $t/2$ messages are delayed after the decision, under the assumption that $p$ decides, which happens almost surely, this means that $p$ will decide its output value with probability at least $1/t > 0$ without receiving any message. Conditioning on this event and because deciding happens almost surely, this implies that there exists $T > 0$ such that $p$ will decide a value within time $T$ without receiving any message with probability at least $1/t > 0$.

    We derive a contradiction from this last fact. We consider the following \texttt{Final} setup:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item All parties are honest.
        \item Parties in $\mathcal{P}\setminus \{p\}$ have input $1$.
        \item Party $p$ has input $0$.
        \item All messages are delayed for the first $T$ units of time, afterwards they are received immediately.
    \end{itemize}
    We first consider \texttt{Final'} with the slight difference that $p$ is byzantine, but acting as honest with input $0$. In this case, because of validity, protocol $\mathcal{A}$ guarantees that parties in $\mathcal{P}\setminus \{p\}$ decide $1$. Moreover, these parties cannot distinguish \texttt{Final} and \texttt{Final'}, so they will also decide $1$ in \texttt{Final}. However, for the first $T$ units of time, $p$ cannot distinguish \texttt{Final} and \texttt{Main}. Therefore, it will decide $0$ with probability at least $1/t > 0$. However, all parties are honest and this breaks the agreement with a non-zero probability (one honest party decides $0$, the others decide $1$), hence the contradiction.
\end{proof}

