\begin{abstract}
% Semantic control in music production allows users to manipulate audio effect parameters using natural language, reducing the technical barriers for non-experts. In this work, we explore the potential of Large Language Models (LLMs) to function as semantic audio experts by addressing the text-to-effect parameter prediction (Text2Fx) task: mapping timbre-related textual descriptions to corresponding audio effect settings. We show that LLMs can generate not only plausible parameter predictions but also coherent natural language explanations that elucidate the relationship between timbre semantics and digital signal processing (DSP) operations. To enhance the model’s performance on audio-related tasks, we introduce several in-context examples, including audio DSP feature, DSP function code, and few-shot examples. Our results suggest that LLMs can serve as interpretable, text-driven interfaces for audio production, paving the way for more intuitive and accessible music creation tools.

In music production, manipulating audio effects~(Fx) parameters through natural language has the potential to reduce technical barriers for non-experts. We present \modelname, a framework leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs) to predict Fx parameters directly from textual descriptions without requiring task-specific training or fine-tuning. Our approach address the text-to-effect parameter prediction (Text2Fx) task by mapping natural language descriptions to the corresponding Fx parameters for equalization and reverberation. We demonstrate that LLMs can generate Fx parameters in a zero-shot manner that elucidates the relationship between timbre semantics and audio effects in music production. To enhance performance, we introduce three types of in-context examples: audio Digital Signal Processing (DSP) features, DSP function code, and few-shot examples. Our results demonstrate that LLM-based Fx parameter generation outperforms previous optimization approaches, offering competitive performance in translating natural language descriptions to appropriate Fx settings. Furthermore, LLMs can serve as text-driven interfaces for audio production, paving the way for more intuitive and accessible music production tools.~\footnote{Our demo is available at \url{https://seungheondoh.github.io/llm2fx-demo/}}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
% task introduction
Semantic music production represents an emerging paradigm that leverages natural language descriptions to control audio effects~(Fx)~\cite{moffat2022semantic}. This approach aims to reduce the technical barriers associated with traditional music production interfaces, making the creative process more accessible to novices and non-experts. The text-to-Fx parameter prediction (Text2Fx) task stands at the forefront of this innovation, enabling users to articulate desired sonic transformations in everyday language (e.g., ``Make the guitar sound warmer'' or ``Add a bright, spacious reverb'') rather than navigating complex technical parameters or searching for reference audio samples.

% related works-1
Prior research in semantic audio production has made significant strides in connecting natural language with audio processing parameters. Sabin et al.~\cite{sabin2011weighted} pioneered the mapping of text descriptors to Equalizer~(EQ) and Reverberation~(Reverb) parameters. The SAFE project~\cite{stables2016semantic} expanded this work by developing a comprehensive dataset and ontological framework linking language to audio transformations. The SocialFX series~\cite{cartwright2013social, seetharaman2014reverbalize, zheng2016socialfx} contributed valuable insights through crowdsourced natural language descriptions for EQ, Reverb, and Compression. The Mix Evaluation Dataset~\cite{de2017mix} collected mixes including parameter settings, preference ratings, and free-form descriptions.

For the Text2Fx task, Venkatesh et al.~\cite{venkatesh2022word} proposed an EQ parameter estimation approach using effects-specific word embedding spaces. Balasubramaniam et al.~\cite{balasubramaniam2023word} introduced text-driven audio manipulation, but their black-box approach limited user control and understanding while being constrained by a closed vocabulary. Chu et al.~\cite{chu2025text2fx} addresses these limitations by leveraging CLAP~\cite{elizalde2023clap} embeddings and differentiable DSP modules~\cite{steinmetz2022style, steinmetz2021filtered} to enable open-vocabulary control of Fx. Their method performs single-instance optimization in the embedding space without requiring model retraining, offering a flexible approach to sound transformation.

% related works3-llm
Beyond regression or optimization approaches, the Text2Fx task can be addressed through generative methods. One promising approach is to leverage LLMs for direct Fx parameter prediction, which can potentially offer more flexible solutions compared to traditional techniques. Recent advancements in LLMs have demonstrated remarkable zero-shot capabilities across various domains. LLMs like GPT-4~\cite{achiam2023gpt}, Gemini~\cite{team2024gemini}, and LLaMA series~\cite{grattafiori2024llama} have shown the ability to perform complex tasks, leveraging their extensive pre-training on diverse text corpora. This zero-shot generalization has been particularly effective in domains requiring specialized knowledge and reasoning~\cite{wei2022emergent}. In the music and audio domain, while LLMs have been successfully applied to tasks such as understanding~\cite{doh2023lp, gardner2023llark, tang2023salmonn, liu2024music, zhao2024openmu}, retrieval~\cite{doh2024enriching, manco2024augment, doh2024music, wu2025clamp, doh2025talkplay}, and generation~\cite{yuan2024chatmusician} tasks, they have not yet been extensively explored for music post-production tasks.

% Unlike these other applications, audio post-production requires precise technical knowledge about signal processing operations and their perceptual effects—a specialized domain that presents unique challenges for language models.


