% contribution
In this work, we investigate the capabilities of LLMs as semantic audio experts for the Text2Fx task. Our contributions are threefold: 1) We demonstrate that LLMs can generate plausible Fx parameters settings directly from natural language descriptions without task-specific training; 2) We introduce novel in-context inference strategies specifically designed for audio processing tasks that significantly enhance parameter generation performance; 3) We develop a comprehensive quantitative evaluation pipeline for the Text2Fx task, providing analysis of existing datasets and multiple audio embedding extractors. Our approach offers a promising direction for creating more intuitive and accessible music production interfaces that leverages the semantic understanding capabilities of LLMs.

\section{LLM2Fx: LLM-based Audio~Effects~Parameter~Prediction}

\subsection{Task Formation}
We formalize the Text2Fx task as a conditioned prediction problem. Given a natural language description, the LLM predicts appropriate audio effect parameters $p = f_{\text{LLM}}(d, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{C})$, where $d$ represents natural language descriptions of desired timbre and audio effect transformations (e.g., ``make the sound warmer''), $p$ denotes the predicted audio effect parameter values in structured JSON format, $\mathcal{P}$ is the system prompt that guides the model's behavior, and $\mathcal{C}$ represents in-context information.

The model generates Fx parameters that appropriately realize the requested timbral characteristics for EQ and Reverb. We employ pre-trained LLMs as our mapping function $f_\text{LLM}$ based on their demonstrated zero-shot reasoning capabilities across specialized knowledge domains. We hypothesize that these models have acquired implicit knowledge of audio processing concepts through their pre-training on diverse text corpora, enabling them to effectively bridge the semantic gap between natural language descriptions and technical audio parameters without requiring task-specific training or fine-tuning.

\subsection{System Prompting}
To effectively leverage LLMs for Fx parameter generation, we design a specialized system prompt that frames the task and provides necessary constraints. Our system prompt serves three key functions: \textit{1) Role Definition}: establishes the LLM as an expert audio engineer with specialized knowledge in sound design and audio processing; \textit{2) Task Instruction}: clearly defines the objective of translating natural language description, such as  semantic word, instrument type, Fx type, into specific audio effect parameters; and \textit{3) Response Format}: enforces structured output in JSON format to ensure parameter predictions are machine-readable. This prompt design ensures that the LLM generates structured parameter predictions while leveraging its implicit knowledge of audio processing concepts.

\subsection{In-Context Information}
To enhance the performance of LLMs on the Text2Fx task, we introduce three types of in-context information that provide domain knowledge for audio processing tasks.

\vspace{2mm}
\noindent \textbf{Few-Shot Examples Context:} We utilize the in-context learning capabilities~\cite{brown2020language} of LLMs by providing few-shot examples that demonstrate the mapping between natural language descriptions and corresponding audio effect parameters. Formally, we can express this as: $f_{\text{LLM}}(d, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{few-shot}}) = p$ where $\mathcal{C}_{\text{few-shot}} = \{(d_1, p_1), (d_2, p_2), \ldots, (d_k, p_k)\}$ represents $k$ examples of semantic descriptions $d_i$ paired with their corresponding parameter settings $p_i$. Each example consists of a semantic description (e.g., ``warm piano sound'') paired with appropriate parameter settings in JSON format. These examples serve as implicit guidance for the model, helping it understand the relationship between semantic descriptors and technical parameters without requiring explicit fine-tuning. For few-shot examples, we utilize five manually annotated human examples ($k=5$) for each audio effect type, ensuring high-quality reference points for the LLMs.

\vspace{2mm}
\noindent \textbf{DSP Function Context:} We leverage the code comprehension abilities~\cite{achiam2023gpt, team2024gemini, roziere2023code} of LLMs by including DSP code function at inference time. Using the DASP Python library~\footnote{https://github.com/csteinmetz1/dasp-pytorch}, we include code snippets that explicitly show the mathematical relationships between parameters and their effect on the audio signal. By exposing the model to these implementations, we enable it to reason about the underlying signal transformations associated with different parameter settings, leading to more technically sound predictions.

\vspace{2mm}
\noindent \textbf{DSP Feature Context:} To enhance parameter prediction specifically conditioned on input audio, we provide statistical information of audio DSP features as contextual examples. This approach explores whether adding these parameters as context might improve how LLMs predict audio effects, as blind prediction of audio effects is highly unusual in audio engineering. Given that LLMs might have been trained on extensive literature regarding DSP low-level descriptors and high-level semantic words, we hypothesize that this exposure could help LLMs explore the relationship between these topics. We include representative audio features extracted from the input signal as context for LLM inference, such as:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Sample rate: Number of samples per second
    \item RMS energy: $\sqrt{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}x^2[n]}$, where $x[n]$ is the audio signal
    \item Crest factor: $\frac{\max(|x[n]|)}{RMS}$, ratio of peak amplitude to RMS
    \item Dynamic spread: $\sqrt{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}(|x[n]| - \mu)^2}$, where $\mu$ is the mean amplitude
    \item Spectral centroid: $\frac{\sum_{k=0}^{N/2}f[k]|X[k]|}{\sum_{k=0}^{N/2}|X[k]|}$, where $X[k]$ is the FFT of $x[n]$ and $f[k]$ is the frequency at bin $k$
    \item Spectral flatness: $\frac{\sqrt[N]{\prod_{k=0}^{N/2}|X[k]|}}{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=0}^{N/2}|X[k]|}$, ratio of geometric to arithmetic mean of spectrum
    \item Spectral bandwidth: $\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{k=0}^{N/2}(f[k]-SC)^2|X[k]|}{\sum_{k=0}^{N/2}|X[k]|}}$, spread of frequencies around the centroid
\end{itemize}

\noindent These DSP features provide a numerical representation of the audio signal's characteristics that can be included as context for the LLM. Our pre-trained LLMs can only process natural language. However, we expect that providing DSP features expressed in language will help the model generate audio-specific effect parameters. These parameters will be conditioned on both the semantic word and the input audio content. This approach creates a bridge between audio and language modalities without needing explicit multimodal training.



\section{Experiments}

% \subsection{Target FX}
We evaluated the \modelname framework on two Fx: EQ and Reverb, explicitly using the 6-band Parametric EQ (18 parameters) and the Noise-Shaped Reverberation (25 parameters) implementations from the DASP library.


\subsection{Evaluation Dataset}
An ideal dataset for semantic music–production research should provide four aligned modalities: a dry (unprocessed) audio signal, Fx parameters, the resulting wet (processed) signal, and the natural-language description of the effected audio. Two public datasets currently satisfy these requirements—\emph{SocialEQ}~\cite{cartwright2013social} and \emph{SocialReverb}~\cite{seetharaman2014reverbalize}. \emph{SocialEQ} records one descriptor per example (multi-class), whereas \emph{SocialReverb} may attach several descriptors to the same example (multi-label). These two datasets were merged into the \emph{SocialFX} dataset~\cite{zheng2016socialfx} and are accessible as open-source resources\footnote{\url{http://bit.ly/1WmTP6v}}. Based on \emph{SocialFX} dataset, we reproduced all audio examples using the original parameters with the \emph{Audealize} JavaScript toolkit~\cite{seetharaman2016audealize}\footnote{\url{https://github.com/interactiveaudiolab/audealize_api}}. Following the methodology and using samples from \cite{seetharaman2016audealize}, we applied each parameter set to dry samples of three instruments—\textit{guitar} (10 seconds), \textit{drums} (15 seconds), and \textit{piano} (20 seconds)—to generate wet versions that differ only in Fx, preserving consistent musical content within each instrument class.

It is important to note that semantic annotations (such as ``\textit{warm}'' or ``\textit{bright}'') represent highly subjective concepts. The relationship between audio and semantic words is inherently many-to-many: a single descriptor can correspond to a variety of audio characteristics, and a given audio sample may be described in multiple terms. To support reliable quantitative evaluation, we ensured that each semantic word contains a sufficient number of Fx samples. This was achieved through a three-step preprocessing pipeline: 1) word merging, 2) term frequency filtering, and 3) classification score filtering. Initially, we had 110 words for EQ and 71 words for Reverb. After the three-step preprocessing, this was reduced to 7 and 19 words, respectively.

\vspace{2mm}
\noindent \textbf{Word Merging:} We used GPT-4o~\cite{achiam2023gpt} to create initial clusters of semantically similar words from the entire vocabulary. Using this LLM-based pseudo-clustering as a starting point, we established word merging rules through human validation to confirm semantic similarity (i.e, $\textit{warm, heat, toasty}\xrightarrow{}\textit{warm}$). For each cluster, we selected the word with the highest term frequency as the representative word. This process significantly reduced the vocabulary size to 26 words for EQ and 19 words for Reverb.

\vspace{2mm}
\noindent \textbf{Term Frequency Filtering:} We filtered out words with insufficient audio effect samples to ensure reliable evaluation. Specifically, we used thresholds of 20 samples for EQ and 100 samples for Reverb, removing any words that fell below these minimum requirements. As a result, we obtained 9 words for EQ (\textit{warm, soft, harsh, calm, loud, bright, heavy, happy, cool}) and 19 words for reverb (\textit{echo, distant, spacious, muffled, warm, church, loud, deep, soft, distorted, sad, hollow, sharp, low, tinny, underwater}).


