\vspace{2mm}
\noindent \textbf{Classification Score Filtering:} To ensure strong associations between semantic words and manipulated audio samples, we implemented a classification-based filtering approach. We extracted embeddings from manipulated audio samples using available pre-trained audio embedding extractors, then trained a linear probing layer to predict semantic words. For this linear probing task, we utilized four different audio embedding extractors: \text{VGGish}~\cite{hershey2017cnn}, \text{CLAP}~\cite{wu2023large}, \text{AFx-Rep}~\cite{steinmetz2024st}, and \text{FXenc}~\cite{koo2023music}. We compared the F1 scores of these classifiers with those of a gaussian random embedding baseline, removing any labels whose performance fell below the random baseline. We compared the average F1 scores of these classifiers against a gaussian random embedding baseline, removing semantic words whose performance fell below this threshold. When classifiers underperform the random baseline, it indicates the audio embeddings lack meaningful information related to that semantic word. This filtering process eliminated abstract terms like ``happy'' and ``cool'' from the EQ vocabulary. The final result was a vocabulary of 7 words for EQ and 19 words for Reverb.

Table~\ref{tab:table1} shows the results before and after preprocessing. We named our preprocessed dataset \emph{SocialFX-Eval}. While we end up with fewer audio effect and natural language pairs, we obtain a more sufficient number of samples per word, enabling more robust evaluation.

