\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
Our evaluation process involves an LLM predicting Fx parameters from natural language descriptions, which are then applied to input audio samples via DASP Fx modules. Although our ground truth Fx modules were JavaScript implementations, we use DASP modules as prediction targets to enable fair comparison with prior work~\cite{chu2025text2fx}. We conduct 50 trials to generate different parameter sets for each semantic word. For evaluation metric, we employ Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD)~\cite{gretton2012kernel}, which measures the distance between two distributions: (1) audio features from samples processed with LLM-generated parameters and (2) audio features from samples processed with ground truth parameters for the same semantic word. MMD is defined as:
$\text{MMD}^2(P,Q) = \mathbb{E}_{x,x' \sim P}[k(x,x')] + \mathbb{E}_{y,y' \sim Q}[k(y,y')] - 2\mathbb{E}_{x \sim P, y \sim Q}[k(x,y)]$
where $k$ is a gaussian kernel function that quantifies the distance between distributions in a high-dimensional feature space. MMD scores enable reliable evaluation with small size of reference data~\cite{jayasumana2024rethinking, chung2025kad}. Lower MMD scores indicate better alignment between predicted and ground truth distributions, suggesting the LLM-generated parameters successfully capture the acoustic characteristics implied by the semantic word.

To select appropriate audio embedding extractors for our evaluation, we established upper- and lower-bounds to measure the distribution modeling capability of each method. The upper bound (U.B.) is the MMD between two sets of audio samples synthesized from ground-truth parameters for the same semantic word, obtained by splitting the parameter set in half. The lower bound (L.B.) is the MMD between audio samples from ground-truth parameters and those from randomly sampled parameters. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:table2}, we evaluated five different feature extractors: DSP features, CLAP, VGGish, AFx-Rep, and FXenc. The difference between upper and lower bounds ($\Delta$) served as our primary selection criterion. This demonstrates that the distribution of semantic clusters exhibits significant differences compared to random parameter distributions. For EQ, CLAP and VGGish demonstrated the largest difference (-0.46), while FXenc showed the strongest discrimination for reverb (-0.70). When considering the average difference across both Fx types, FXenc achieved the best overall performance (-0.57). Based on these results, we selected FXenc for evaluation in our subsequent experiments.

\subsection{Baseline Models}
We compare \modelname framework against an optimization-based approach to Fx parameter prediction. Chu et al.~\cite{chu2025text2fx} leverages CLAP~\cite{elizalde2023clap} embedding space and gradient-based optimization to find Fx parameters that maximize similarity between processed audio and target descriptions. We compare against various open-source LLMs, including LLaMA3 series
~\cite{grattafiori2024llama} (1B, 3B, 8B, 70B parameter versions) and Mistral~\cite{jiang2023mistral} (7B parameters), as well as the closed-source GPT-4o accessed through API calls. This comprehensive comparison allows us to assess how different model architectures and parameter scales affect audio effect parameter prediction quality, while maintaining the same system prompt structure and evaluation protocol across all LLM-based approaches to ensure fair comparison.


\section{Results}

\subsection{Can LLMs generate appropriate audio effects parameters from natural language?}
Results in Table~\ref{tab:results} demonstrate that LLMs can effectively generate appropriate Fx parameters from natural language descriptions. For EQ, GPT-4o achieves an average MMD score of 0.22, significantly outperforming both random parameter selection (0.53) and the optimization-based approach by Chu et al.~\cite{chu2025text2fx} (0.55). This demonstrates that LLMs can exceed specialized optimization techniques for Fx parameter prediction. However, for reverb, while Mistral-7B-Instruct substantially improves over random parameters (0.75), it only matches the optimization approach (both 0.45). These results indicate LLMs have acquired implicit knowledge about Fx during pre-training, with a notable disparity between Fx types.

\subsection{Does model size affect Text2Fx prediction performance?}
The impact of model scale on parameter generation quality is evident when comparing performance across different model sizes. For EQ, we observe a dramatic improvement from Llama3.2-1B-Instruct (0.64) to Llama3.2-3B-Instruct (0.27), with further gains as model size increases. Llama3.3-70B-Instruct achieves an average MMD score of 0.24, approaching GPT-4o's performance (0.22). For reverb, larger models generally perform better, with Llama3.3-70B-Instruct (0.52) substantially outperforming smaller Llama variants. Interestingly, Mistral-7B-Instruct achieves the best reverb performance (0.45) despite its smaller parameter count, suggesting that model architecture and training dataset also play important roles. The relationship between model scale and parameter quality indicates that audio signal processing knowledge is encoded more comprehensively in larger models.

\subsection{Does in-context information influence the Fx parameters generation?}
The bottom section of Table~\ref{tab:results} demonstrates the substantial impact of in-context information on parameter generation quality. Using Mistral-7B-Instruct as the base model, we observe adding different types of contextual information provide varying benefits. Adding DSP features of input audio improves both EQ (from 0.30 to 0.26) and reverb performance (from 0.45 to 0.40), indicating providing input context assists finer parameter predictions. Providing DSP Python code yields modest improvements for EQ parameters (0.30 to 0.21) but significantly degrades reverb performance (0.45 to 0.79). This disparity can be attributed to the fundamental differences between these Fx: while EQ modules follow relatively standardized implementations across audio systems~\cite{valimaki2016all}, reverberation algorithms exhibit substantial variation in design and implementation details~\cite{valimaki2012fifty}. These results suggest that simpler Fx, like EQ, where implementation variations have relatively less impact, are better suited to utilizing the pre-trained knowledge of current LLMs. Conducting experiments with various reverb implementations might further validate the observed performance disparity.

However, when DSP Python code is combined with input audio features and few-shot examples, we observe significant performance improvements for both Fx. The complementary nature of these context types allows the model to understand both the algorithmic implementation and real-world parameter mappings, resulting in the best overall performance (0.17 for EQ and 0.26 for reverb). This suggests that while code alone may be insufficient, its combination with exemplar-based reasoning creates a powerful framework for Fx parameter prediction.


\section{Conclusion}
In this work, we introduced \modelname, demonstrating that LLMs can effectively translate natural language descriptions into Fx parameters without requiring task-specific training. Our experiments reveal that in-context information—particularly the synergistic combination of DSP features, function code, and few-shot examples—substantially enhances prediction performance by providing both algorithmic understanding and practical parameter mappings. Despite these promising results, important limitations remain: our approach currently processes only a single Fx rather than a chain of Fx, and works with single-track instrument sources rather than multitrack music. Also, we used only one sample per instrument class, so further analysis is needed to fully assess the LLMs' ability to generalize Fx parameter predictions. Future work should explore more complex Fx chains, multitrack music processing, and personalized parameter generation that adapts to individual preferences. Overall, our findings suggest that LLMs offer a promising path toward more intuitive and accessible audio production tools that bridge the gap between natural language descriptions and technical audio manipulations.

