\subsection{Discovering Stochastic Models}\label{sec:model_disc_results}
In order to quantify the degree to which our model discovery loop is effective at identifying reasonable candidate functions for risk analysis, we first test our framework on known SDEs. We consider three SDEs that are commonly used in finance and economics. These are the constant elasticity of variance (CEV) model, the Cox-Ingersoll-Ross (CIR) model, and a simple jump-diffusion (JD) model \cite{beckers1980constant,overbeck1997estimation,cheang2012modern}. We give more details of each below. For each model, we use the same lowercase Roman alphabet for parameter variables, i.e. $a, b, c, \ldots$, for consistency with other models suggested by our \textit{risk analyst} agent and to simplify the generation of the symbolic trees, outlined in \autoref{sec:sym_trees}. This means that the underlying interpretation of the parameters is model-dependent and requires human oversight in their application. We note that these parameters are then calibrated and the model discovery loop can suggest any number of parameters. 

First, the CEV model is described by  
\begin{equation}
\diff S_{t}=a S_{t}\diff t+b S_{t}^{c }\diff W_{t}
\label{eq:CEV}
\end{equation}
in which $S_t$ is the stochastic variable, representing the price of the security over time, $a$ is the strength of the drift term and $b$ is the elasticity parameter, denoting the strength between volatility and the asset price and $dW_t$ denotes Brownian noise. This SDE is widely used in modelling asset price dynamics, especially when the volatility is thought to depend non-linearly on the asset price \cite{beckers1980constant}. 

The CIR model is described by
\begin{equation}
\diff S_{t}=a(b-S_{t})\diff t+c {\sqrt {S_{t}}}\diff W_{t}
\label{eq:CIR}
\end{equation}
in which $S_t$ is the stochastic variable, and $a, b, c$, are model parameters. We note that the CIR model is typically used to model interest rates, where $S_t$ would be replaced by, e.g. $r_t$ to denote the interest rate and $a,b, c$ denotes the mean-reversion speed, long-term mean, and volatility strength, respectively \cite{overbeck1997estimation}. Here, we use $S_t$ for consistency with the other tested SDEs. We include this SDE as it is relatively simple and widely used, though we consider only equity price paths in later sections. 

Finally, our jump-diffusion (JD) model is described by
\begin{equation}
\diff S_{t} = a S_{t}\diff t + b S_{t} \diff W_{t} + S_t \diff J_{t}
\label{eq:jump_diffusion}
\end{equation}
in which $a$ is the expected rate of return, $b$ is volatility, which we assume to be constant, and $\diff J_t$ is a jump process, typically modelled as a Poisson process, $\diff N_t$,  with intensity parameter $\lambda$ \cite{cheang2012modern}. We note that the jump term is often further expanded as $(Y - 1)\diff N_t$, for random variable $Y$, representing the (log-normal) intensity of jumps. Here, we specify the ground-truth target equation as a simpler jump diffusion term but allow the model discovery process to identify models of increasing complexity. 

