For \texttt{4o-mini} and \texttt{o1-mini} the performance worsens with \textit{context}$^\mathrm{N}_\mathrm{M}$ compared to \textit{context}$^\mathrm{N}$. These LLMs performed poorly at the model discovery step as shown in \autoref{tab:equations_performance}. This suggests that if the LLM is working with a poor model of the financial time series it will likely not be effective at producing profitable trading strategies. In addition to selecting suboptimal models, poor performance during model discovery is often caused by SDE models that fail to compile due to incorrect coding strategies. Rather than introducing complicated fallback solutions or attempting per-LLM prompt engineering, we tried to agentically debug each of these LLMs three times before marking this SDE as a failure. A loop failure is recorded when all fifteen SDE models selected during the model discovery process fail, we denote this as a dash (--), we show this in \autoref{tab:trading_results}. We note that almost all LLMs showed at least one model failure during the model discovery process except for \texttt{Sonnet 3.7} and \texttt{4o-mini}, which we believe is due to their superior coding capabilities. The LLM with the highest failure rate is \texttt{Llama 3.3} as we discuss below. 

