We introduce the first comprehensive framework for applying reasoning-capable LLMs (specifically OpenAI's O4-Mini and Anthropic's Claude 3.7) to the multi-objective HPC job scheduling problem. Unlike previous work that focuses on traditional heuristics or deep reinforcement learning, our approach leverages the advanced reasoning capabilities developed for natural language tasks to handle the complex constraint satisfaction and trade-off analysis inherent in HPC scheduling.
We develop a novel ReAct (Reason + Act) prompting framework specifically designed for sequential scheduling decisions. This architecture enables LLMs to explicitly articulate reasoning about resource constraints, fairness, and performance trade-offs; make structured scheduling actions (StartJob, BackfillJob, Delay, Stop) based on this reasoning; maintain decision continuity across multiple scheduling steps without requiring model retraining
We introduce a persistent scratchpad mechanism that accumulates reasoning history, actions, and feedback across scheduling decisions. This enables context-aware scheduling that learns from previous decisions within the same workload; natural language feedback integration when constraint violations occur; and zero-shot adaptation to new scheduling scenarios without fine-tuning
