\subsection{Constraint Enforcement}
The scheduling problem is inherently numeric and constraint-driven\textendash involving understanding resource requirement, Node/memory fitting\textendash which makes LLM vulnerable to hallucinations and silent arithmetic errors (e.g., job has asked for 16 Nodes but LLM processes as 8). To address this, we decoupled reasoning from execution: the LLM suggests an action, but the simulation environment processes and validates it against real job constraints before applying any state change. If an action is infeasible (e.g., resource overload), the environment blocks it and provides natural language feedback. This feedback is injected into the scratchpad to inform subsequent LLM decisions. By doing so, we ensure that the agent remains flexible in reasoning while maintaining strict correctness in simulation outcomes.
