\begin{comment}
The seven benchmark scenarios are described below:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Homogeneous Short:} All jobs have similar characteristics, with walltimes between 30--120 seconds, 2 nodes, and 4\,GB memory. This simulates lightweight workloads such as CI pipelines or test jobs, focusing on throughput optimization.

    \item \textbf{Heterogeneous Mix:} Jobs vary in runtime and resource requirements. Walltimes follow a Gamma distribution (\texttt{shape=1.5}, \texttt{scale=300}), while node and memory requests are drawn from powers-of-two distributions up to system limits. This scenario reflects a realistic production workload with mixed job classes.

    \item \textbf{Long-Job Dominant:} Roughly 20\% of the jobs are extremely long-running (up to 50,000 seconds) and request 128 nodes. The remaining jobs are short and small (under 500 seconds, 2 nodes). This scenario evaluates how schedulers handle jobs that dominate system resources and time.

    \item \textbf{High Parallelism:} All jobs are parallel and demand between 64 and 256 nodes. Walltimes follow a Gamma distribution (\texttt{shape=1}, \texttt{scale=800}). Memory scales with the node count. This simulates compute-heavy workloads such as tightly coupled simulations.

    \item \textbf{Resource Sparse:} Jobs are resource-light (1 node and less than 8\,GB memory) but may have varying walltimes (30--300 seconds). This tests the scheduler's ability to handle sparse but potentially long-running jobs efficiently.

    \item \textbf{Bursty + Idle:} This scenario is traditionally designed to simulate alternating phases of high submission intensity and idle gaps over time. However, in our setup, all jobs are submitted at time zero, and burstiness is instead expressed through sharp variation in job walltimes. The job mix alternates between short and long-running jobs, while resource demands remain modest. This simulates periods of rapid queue turnover followed by long system engagement, testing the scheduler's ability to balance responsiveness and long-term efficiency under uneven job durations.

    \item \textbf{Adversarial:} Designed to stress schedulers, this scenario begins with a single large job (128 nodes, 100,000 seconds walltime), followed by many small jobs (1 node, 60 seconds). This exposes weaknesses in naive FCFS strategies due to the convoy effect and evaluates starvation handling and throughput recovery.
\end{enumerate}
\end{comment}

Each scenario was instantiated with [10, 20, 40, 60, 80, 100] jobs. All jobs are submitted at time zero to eliminate timing noise and isolate scheduling effects. The objectives, including makespan, average wait time, turnaround time, node/memory utilization, throughput, and fairness, are collected for comparison across all scheduling strategies.
\savevspace
\subsection{Objectives}

We evaluate scheduling performance using seven standard objectives that capture both system-level efficiency and user-perceived responsiveness. \textbf{Makespan}~\cite{englert2008power} measures the total elapsed time from earliest job submission to completion of the last job ($\max_{j \in J} (x_j + d_j)$), reflecting overall schedule efficiency. \textbf{Average wait time}~\cite{smith1999using} captures user-perceived latency as the time jobs spend queued before execution ($w_j = x_j$ since all $s_j = 0$), while \textbf{average turnaround time}~\cite{smith1999using} measures the total time from submission to completion ($x_j + d_j$). \textbf{Throughput}~\cite{mekkittikul1998practical} indicates queue processing speed as jobs completed per unit time ($n/(\text{Makespan} - \min_{j}(x_j))$). \textbf{Resource utilization}~\cite{lee2014improving} is measured separately for nodes ($\frac{1}{C\cdot \text{Makespan}}\sum_{j \in J}n_j\cdot d_j$) and memory ($\frac{1}{M \cdot \text{Makespan}}\sum_{j \in J}m_j\cdot d_j$), where $C$ and $M$ represent total system capacity and $n_j$, $m_j$ are per-job resource requirements. \textbf{Fairness}~\cite{ajtai1998fairness} is evaluated using Jain's index~\cite{sediq2013optimal} from both per-job ($\frac{(\sum_{j \in J} w_j)^2}{n \cdot \sum_{j \in J} w_j^2}$) and per-user ($\frac{(\sum{i \in U} u_i)^2}{|U| \cdot \sum_{i \in U} u_i^2}$) perspectives, where $u_i$ represents average wait time per user, with values ranging from 0 (worst) to 1 (perfect fairness).

\begin{comment}
We consider seven objectives that capture both system-level efficiency and user-perceived responsiveness. 
These metrics are computed after all jobs in the static queue have been scheduled and completed, ensuring fair comparison across all scenarios. 

% 
% \begin{itemize}
%     \item $x_j$: start time,
%     \item $d_j$: duration or processing time,
%     \item $e_j = x_j + d_j$: end time,
%     \item $r_{ij}$: demand of resource time $i \in I$.
% \end{itemize}
% 
\paragraph{Makespan}
The total elapsed time from the earliest job submission to the completion of the last job. 
It reflects overall schedule efficiency and resource throughput.
\begin{align}
    \text{Makespan} = \max_{j \in J} (x_j + d_j).
\end{align}

\paragraph{Average wait time}
The time jobs spend in the queue before starting execution. 
Lower values indicate reduced user-perceived latency.
since all $s_j = 0$, the wait time of job $j$ is:
\begin{align}
    w_j = x_j - s_j = x_j.
\end{align}

\paragraph{Average turnaround time}
The turnaround time is the time difference from submission time to the end of the job, i.e.,
\begin{align}
    \text{Turnaround}_j = x_j + d_j - s_j = e_j.
\end{align}

\paragraph{Throughput}
The throughput defines the number of jobs completed per unit time. 
This indicates how quickly the scheduler processes the queue under a fixed workload.
\begin{align}
    \text{Throughput} = \frac{n}{\text{Makespan} - \min_{j}(x_j)}.
\end{align}

\paragraph{Resource utilization}
For the resource utilization, we split into two parts, the node and memory utilization, respectively.
For the node utilization, it defines the percentage of node resources actively used over the scheduling period. 
Higher utilization indicates better packing and resource efficiency.
\begin{align}
    \text{Node Utilization} = \frac{1}{C\cdot \text{Makespan}}\sum_{j \in J}n_j\cdot d_j,
\end{align}
where $C$ is the total count of node resources, and $n_j$ is the node requirements for job $j$.
% 
Similar to node, we define memory utilization as 
\begin{align}
    \text{Memory Utilization} = \frac{1}{M \cdot \text{Makespan}}\sum_{j \in J}m_j\cdot d_j,
\end{align}
where $M$ is the total memory resources, and $m_j$ is the memory requirements for job $j$.

\paragraph{Fairness}
Fairness is evaluated using Jain's fairness index, which quantifies how evenly scheduling delays are distributed. 
Here, we consider two perspectives:

\emph{(i) Per-job fairness}: Measured using per-job slowdown (or wait time), this reflects how equally individual jobs experience delays.
\begin{align}
    % w_j = Slowdown\ or\ WaitTime\ for\ job\ j \\
    \text{Jain's Index}_{job} = \frac{\left(\sum_{j \in J} w_j\right)^2}{n \cdot \sum_{j \in J} w_j^2}
\end{align}
where $w_j$ is the wait time for job $j$.

\emph{(j) Per-user fairness}: This assesses whether users, regardless of how many jobs they submit, experience similar scheduling quality. We aggregate average slowdown (or wait time) per user:
\begin{align}
    % u_i = Average\ Slowdown\ or\ WaitTime\ for\ user\ i \\
    % U = the\ set\ of\ all\ unique\ users\ in\ the\ workload \\
    \text{Jain's Index}_{user}= \frac{\left(\sum{i \in U} u_i\right)^2}{|U| \cdot \sum_{i \in U} u_i^2}
\end{align}
where $u_i$ is the average wait time for user $i$.

The Jain's fairness values range from 0 (worst fairness) to 1 (perfect fairness), and a high Jain’s index indicates that delays are distributed more uniformly, either across jobs or users.
