We evaluated our LLM-based scheduling approach against traditional baselines across seven workload scenarios with 60 jobs each, with performance metrics normalized relative to FCFS as the baseline. The results, shown in the normalized metrics chart (Figure~\ref{fig:react-exp2}), reveal distinct performance patterns that highlight the strengths and limitations of different scheduling approaches under varying workload characteristics.
Note that the figure did not include Heterogeneous Mix becasue it will be covered in next section.


The Long Job Dominant scenario shows the most dramatic performance differentiation across methods. FCFS exhibits severely degraded performance, particularly in wait time metrics where normalized values approach zero, indicating extremely poor user experience due to the convoy effect. In stark contrast, both LLM-based schedulers (Claude 3.7 and O4-Mini) maintain normalized performance close to 1.0 across all metrics, effectively mitigating long job starvation through intelligent job ordering and backfilling strategies. This represents the approximately 80\% wait time improvement mentioned, demonstrating the LLMs' ability to handle complex scheduling cases that affect traditional heuristics.

The Bursty Idle scenario clearly demonstrates the adaptive limitations of optimization-based approaches. OR-Tools shows a dramatic performance spike in average wait time (normalized value of $\sim$1.75), indicating severe degradation under dynamic workload pressure. In contrast, both LLM-based methods (Claude 3.7 and O4-Mini) maintain stable performance across all metrics with normalized values close to 1.0, demonstrating superior adaptivity during burst phases. SJF shows moderate performance but lacks the multiobjective balancing capabilities of the LLM approaches, highlighting the importance of scheduling adaptivity in dynamic environments.

The High Parallelism scenario reveals distinct resource management capabilities across methods. OR-Tools and Claude 3.7 achieve superior resource utilization with normalized values exceeding 1.3 for both node and memory utilization, demonstrating effective job packing strategies for large parallel workloads. FCFS, SJF, and O4-Mini show more modest utilization improvements, with SJF particularly struggling due to resource fragmentation caused by its bias toward shorter jobs. The results confirm that large-scale parallel workloads require sophisticated packing-aware scheduling strategies, where optimization-based approaches (OR-Tools) and reasoning-capable LLMs (Claude 3.7) excel over traditional heuristics.

In the Homogeneous Short, Resource Sparse, and Adversarial scenarios, all scheduling methods achieved nearly identical normalized performance across all metrics (values clustered around 1.0). The uniformity in job characteristics (Homogeneous Short), minimal resource contention (Resource Sparse), and unexpectedly benign adversarial conditions provided limited opportunities for scheduling differentiation. These results demonstrate that sophisticated scheduling approaches offer minimal advantage when workload characteristics are uniform or resource pressure is low.


