\subsection{Scalability analysis}


\begin{figure*}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{exp1_scalability_one_scenario_across_job_sizes/expt1.png}
    \savevspace
\caption{Scalability analysis showing normalized performance metrics across increasing job queue sizes (10 to 100 jobs) for the Heterogeneous Mix workload scenario. All metrics are normalized relative to FCFS performance (baseline = 1.0). The results demonstrate how scheduling performance differentiates as problem complexity increases. At small scales (10 jobs), all methods perform similarly, but significant performance gaps emerge with larger job queues. OR-Tools achieves exceptional resource utilization (up to 2.0× FCFS) at higher scales but shows more variable performance across other metrics. LLM-based schedulers (Claude 3.7, O4-Mini) maintain consistently balanced performance across all metrics and scales, demonstrating robust scalability characteristics. Traditional heuristics (FCFS, SJF) show limited improvement potential as queue size increases, highlighting the importance of advanced scheduling approaches for large-scale HPC workloads.}
\label{fig:scalability-analysis}
\end{figure*}
% 

To evaluate how scheduling performance scales with problem complexity, we analyzed the Heterogeneous Mix workload scenario across increasing job queue sizes from 10 to 100 jobs. The results shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:scalability-analysis} demonstrate a clear relationship between problem scale and the effectiveness of different scheduling approaches, with performance differentiation becoming increasingly pronounced as queue size grows.

 At the smallest scale (10 jobs), all scheduling methods achieve nearly identical normalized performance across all metrics, with values clustered tightly around 1.0. This convergence suggests that when scheduling problems are small and resource contention is minimal, the choice of scheduling algorithm has a limited impact on system performance. The uniform performance indicates that the overhead of advanced scheduling approaches may not be justified for very small job queues.

 As the job queue size increases from 20 to 40 jobs, clear performance gaps begin to emerge between methods. OR-Tools starts demonstrating superior resource utilization capabilities, while traditional heuristics (FCFS and SJF) show minimal improvement from their baseline performance. LLM-based schedulers begin to exhibit their balanced multiobjective optimization characteristics, maintaining stable performance across all metrics while traditional methods struggle to adapt to increased complexity.

At higher scales (60-100 jobs), the performance differentiation becomes most pronounced. OR-Tools achieves higher resource utilization improvements, reaching normalized values up to 2.0$\times$ better than FCFS for both node and memory utilization. However, this optimization comes at the cost of other metrics, particularly wait time fairness, where OR-Tools shows more variable performance. The single-objective focus of OR-Tools becomes apparent as it optimizes heavily for resource efficiency while potentially sacrificing user experience metrics.

 Both Claude 3.7 and O4-Mini demonstrate consistent scalability characteristics across all job queue sizes. Unlike OR-Tools, which shows differences in individual metrics, the LLM-based schedulers maintain balanced performance across makespan, wait times, utilization, throughput, and fairness metrics. This consistency suggests that the reasoning-based approach scales gracefully with problem complexity, maintaining multiobjective balance even as scheduling decisions become more complex.

 FCFS and SJF show the most limited scalability, with normalized performance remaining relatively static across all queue sizes. These methods fail to capitalize on the increased optimization opportunities that larger job queues provide, highlighting their fundamental algorithmic limitations. The lack of adaptivity in traditional heuristics becomes particularly apparent at larger scales, where intelligent resource allocation and job ordering become critical for system performance.

The scalability analysis reveals that the benefits of advanced scheduling approaches become increasingly important as HPC workload complexity grows. For small research clusters with limited job queues, simple heuristics may suffice. However, for large-scale HPC installations processing hundreds of concurrent jobs, the performance advantages of LLM-based scheduling become significant. The consistent multiobjective balancing exhibited by LLM schedulers across all scales makes them particularly attractive for HPC environments where both efficiency and fairness are critical operational requirements.

